window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["ihbas-2024-d"] = {"id": "ihbas-2024-d", "title": "IHBAS April 2024: MP-2024 Series D", "fullTitle": "MP-2024 Series D, filed as IHBAS April 2024 (real paper, official key)", "sub": "99 real questions · official signed key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Intelligence is not defined as the:", "options": {"a": "Ability to do abstract critical thinking, planning the strategy and solving the problem", "b": "Ability to comprehend complex ideas, integrate the information, adapting oneself to situations", "c": "Ability to change the environment and one's personality", "d": "Ability to choose appropriate response to stimuli and learning from experience"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: intelligence is not defined as the ability to change the environment and one's personality. Psychologists have offered many definitions, but they share a core of reasoning, learning and adaptation. Alfred Binet stressed judgement and adapting oneself to circumstances; Lewis Terman stressed abstract thinking; David Wechsler called intelligence the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment. In 1994 Linda Gottfredson drafted a statement signed by 52 researchers describing intelligence as a very general mental capability involving the ability to reason, plan, solve problems, think abstractly, comprehend complex ideas, learn quickly and learn from experience. Options a, b and d are paraphrases of exactly these ideas. Personality, by contrast, is a separate domain (traits, temperament, character), and no standard definition treats remaking one's personality as an intellectual ability. Option c is right because it is the only choice that adds something outside the construct, namely changing one's personality. Exam trap: Robert Sternberg's practical (contextual) intelligence does include adapting to, selecting and shaping one's environment, so \"changing the environment\" alone could sound valid; it is the personality half that makes c the odd one out. In \"not\" questions, hunt for the element that belongs to a different construct.", "topic": "Standard definitions of intelligence"}, {"q": "Which of the statement is not correct about intelligence?", "options": {"a": "As we grow older fluid intelligence tends to decrease", "b": "As we grow older crystallized intelligence tends to decrease", "c": "Fluid intelligence is influenced by genetics", "d": "Crystallized intelligence is influenced by genetics"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: it is not correct that crystallized intelligence decreases as we grow older. Raymond Cattell, later working with John Horn, split general intelligence into two kinds. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, as in Raven's Progressive Matrices or a puzzle you have never seen. Crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and culture. Research, including K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study, shows that fluid ability peaks in early adulthood and then declines gradually, while crystallized ability stays stable or keeps rising into the sixties and beyond, dropping only in very old age. A 65-year-old teacher may be slower on a novel puzzle than at 25 but knows more words and facts. Both abilities are partly heritable, as twin studies show. Option b is right because it reverses the well-known age pattern: crystallized intelligence is the one that holds up with age. Exam trap: students mix up which ability declines. Remember \"fluid flows away, crystal stays\": fluid declines, crystallized is maintained.", "topic": "Fluid vs crystallized intelligence with age"}, {"q": "Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an adaptation of:", "options": {"a": "Vineland Social Maturity Scale", "b": "Raven's Colour Progressive Matrices", "c": "Stanford Binet Intelligence Scale for Children", "d": "Wechsler's Intelligence Scale for Children"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: MISIC is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC). David Wechsler published the WISC in 1949 as a children's version of his adult scale. Like every Wechsler scale up to the WISC-III and WAIS-III, it has a verbal set of subtests (for example information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, vocabulary, digit span) and a performance set (for example picture completion, block design, object assembly, coding, mazes), and it yields verbal, performance and full-scale IQs (from the WISC-IV and WAIS-IV on, verbal and performance IQ gave way to four index scores). Arthur J. Malin adapted the WISC for Indian children in Nagpur, replacing culturally unsuitable items and building Indian norms; the result, Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, is still widely used in Indian clinics. A clinician assessing a 9-year-old with school difficulties in India would typically use MISIC to obtain verbal and performance IQs separately. Option d is right because MISIC keeps the Wechsler structure of verbal and performance subtests with deviation IQ scoring. Exam trap: Malin also produced the Indian adaptation of the Vineland Social Maturity Scale, so his name appears with two different tests. Keep them apart: MISIC measures intelligence (IQ), while Malin's Vineland measures social competence (social age and social quotient).", "topic": "MISIC as Indian adaptation of WISC"}, {"q": "Which of the followings is not a correct branch of social process theory?", "options": {"a": "Social learning theory", "b": "Social control theory", "c": "Labeling theory", "d": "Social information processing theory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: social information processing theory is not a branch of social process theory. In criminology, social process theories argue that anyone can become delinquent, and that crime grows out of a person's interactions with family, peers, school and the justice system, rather than out of poverty or neighbourhood structure alone (the focus of social structure theories such as strain theory). They have three branches. Social learning theory says crime is learned: Edwin Sutherland's differential association theory held that people learn criminal attitudes and techniques in close groups, and Ronald Akers added reinforcement. Social control theory, best known through Travis Hirschi's social bond theory (1969), asks why most people do not offend and answers that bonds of attachment, commitment, involvement and belief hold them back. Labeling theory, linked with Edwin Lemert and Howard Becker, says that being publicly labelled deviant pushes a person toward a deviant identity (secondary deviance). Option d is right because social information processing is a different model: Nicki Crick and Kenneth Dodge's step-by-step account of how children encode and interpret social cues and choose responses, explaining aggression through biases such as hostile attribution. Exam trap: both names contain \"social\"; one is a criminological family of theories, the other a cognitive model of aggression.", "topic": "Branches of social process theory in criminology"}, {"q": "A person has information from multiple observations at different times and situations and can perceive the relationship of an observed effect and its causes. This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Personalism", "b": "Attribution", "c": "Participative leadership", "d": "Intuitiveness"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: attribution. Attribution is the process by which we infer the causes of behaviour and events. Fritz Heider (1958) described people as \"naive scientists\" who look for causes, and Harold Kelley (1967) showed how we do this when we have many observations. His covariation model says we notice which possible cause is present when the effect occurs and absent when it does not, using three kinds of information: consensus (do other people react the same way?), consistency (does this person react this way at different times and in different situations?) and distinctiveness (does the person react this way to other things too?). For example, if your friend Meera has praised the same cafe's coffee every week for months, most of your other friends praise it too, and she rarely praises other cafes, you attribute her praise to the coffee, not to her personality. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly Kelley's situation: information from multiple observations across times and situations used to link an effect to its cause. Exam trap: when only a single observation is available, Kelley says we fall back on causal schemas and the discounting principle instead of covariation, but both are still attribution.", "topic": "Attribution from repeated observations"}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of Freud's Psychosexual Stages?", "options": {"a": "Latency, anal, oral, genital, phallic", "b": "Oral, genital, latency, phallic, anal", "c": "Oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital", "d": "Anal, genital, oral, phallic, latency"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages in which the libido (sexual or life energy) is focused on a different erogenous zone. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth: sucking, biting, feeding. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) it centres on retaining and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the key conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) it centres on the genitals, and the Oedipus complex is resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is quiet and redirected into schoolwork, skills and same-sex friendships. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexuality directed at others. Too much frustration or indulgence at a stage can cause fixation, for example an \"oral\" adult who smokes or overeats, or an \"anal retentive\" adult who is stingy and obsessively neat. Option c is right because it is the only option that lists the stages in this developmental order. Exam trap: do not confuse these five stages with Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages, and remember latency comes before, not after, the genital stage.", "topic": "Sequence of Freud's psychosexual stages"}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of collective unconscious?", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Hans Eysenck", "d": "Carl Gustav Jung"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Carl Gustav Jung. Jung, a Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, divided the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds each individual's forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes. Below it lies the collective unconscious, a deeper layer shared by all humanity and inherited from our ancestral past. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in certain ways, which appear again and again in myths, dreams, religions and fairy tales across cultures. Important archetypes include the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, rejected side), the anima and animus (the feminine side of men and masculine side of women), the great mother, the wise old man, the hero and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala). Jung pointed out that the same images, such as the hero's journey or the great flood, occur in cultures that never met. Option d is right because the collective unconscious is Jung's signature concept. Exam trap: Freud also wrote about the unconscious, but his is personal, made of repressed wishes; \"collective\", \"archetypes\" and \"inherited\" always point to Jung.", "topic": "Jung's collective unconscious and archetypes"}, {"q": "The Big Five Theory is commonly also known as ______.", "options": {"a": "CEANO", "b": "OCEAN", "c": "EANCO", "d": "CANEO"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: OCEAN. The Big Five (also called the Five-Factor Model) is the most widely accepted trait model of personality. It grew out of the lexical hypothesis, the idea that important personality differences become encoded as words in language; factor analyses of trait words and ratings, from Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal and Warren Norman onward, repeatedly produced five broad factors. Lewis Goldberg popularised the label \"Big Five\", and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae built the NEO Personality Inventory to measure them. The five factors are Openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), Extraversion (sociable and energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (warm and cooperative versus antagonistic) and Neuroticism (anxious and moody versus emotionally stable). Their initials spell OCEAN. A student who plans every exam week in a timetable and never misses a deadline is high on Conscientiousness. Option b is right because OCEAN is the standard mnemonic formed by the first letters of the five factors. Exam trap: the same five letters are also arranged as CANOE, a genuine alternative mnemonic; the other jumbled arrangements in this question (CEANO, EANCO, CANEO) are not used anywhere.", "topic": "OCEAN acronym for the Big Five"}, {"q": "Robert McCrae and ______ developed Big Five personality traits theory.", "options": {"a": "Gordon Allport", "b": "Carl Jung", "c": "Paul Costa", "d": "Normann Munn"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Paul Costa. Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae, working at the US National Institute on Aging, are the researchers most closely associated with the Big Five or Five-Factor Model. Starting from a three-factor model (neuroticism, extraversion, openness), they added agreeableness and conscientiousness and published the NEO Personality Inventory in 1985, revised as the NEO-PI-R in 1992 with six facets under each of the five domains. Their longitudinal work showed that the five traits are fairly stable in adulthood, appear across many cultures and are partly heritable. The five factors themselves had earlier roots: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal and then Warren Norman found five factors in trait ratings, and Lewis Goldberg coined the name \"Big Five\". Option c is right because \"McCrae and Costa\" is the standard pairing for the Five-Factor Model and the NEO inventories. Exam trap: the distractor \"Normann Munn\" plays on two names. Warren Norman did help discover the five factors, but Norman L. Munn was the author of a well-known introductory psychology textbook and had nothing to do with the Big Five.", "topic": "Costa and McCrae's Five-Factor Model"}, {"q": "Which is the fourth stage of psycho-social development of an individual according to Erikson?", "options": {"a": "Industry v/s inferiority", "b": "Intimacy v/s isolation", "c": "Identity v/s role confusion", "d": "Generativity v/s stagnation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: industry versus inferiority. Erik Erikson, a psychoanalyst trained in Anna Freud's circle in Vienna, extended Freud's theory into eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and successful resolution gives a basic virtue. In order: (1) trust versus mistrust, infancy, hope; (2) autonomy versus shame and doubt, toddlerhood, will; (3) initiative versus guilt, preschool years, purpose; (4) industry versus inferiority, about 6 to 12 years, competence; (5) identity versus role confusion, adolescence, fidelity; (6) intimacy versus isolation, young adulthood, love; (7) generativity versus stagnation, middle adulthood, care; (8) integrity versus despair, old age, wisdom. In the fourth stage the school-age child learns the skills valued by the culture; a child praised for finishing a science project develops a sense of industry, while one who keeps failing or is unfavourably compared with others may feel inferior. Option a is right because industry versus inferiority is fourth in this sequence and corresponds to Freud's latency period. Exam trap: count carefully. Identity versus role confusion is the famous adolescent stage, but it is the fifth, not the fourth.", "topic": "Erikson's fourth psychosocial stage"}, {"q": "According to Carl Rogers which role a teacher should play while giving knowledge that helps to create an atmosphere favorable to learning?", "options": {"a": "Authoritative", "b": "Facilitator", "c": "Commanding", "d": "Authoritarian"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: facilitator. Carl Rogers, founder of client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, applied the same ideas to education, especially in his book Freedom to Learn (1969). He argued that significant learning cannot simply be poured into students; it happens when learners are self-directed and the material feels personally relevant. The teacher's job is therefore to facilitate learning: to create a climate of trust in which students set goals, explore and evaluate their own progress. Rogers said the facilitator needs the same core conditions that make therapy work: genuineness or realness (being a real person rather than playing the expert), unconditional positive regard (prizing and accepting the learner) and empathic understanding (seeing the classroom from the student's side). In a Rogerian class the teacher might help students choose a project on a topic that interests them and act as a resource, rather than lecture and test recall. Option b is right because \"facilitator\" is the exact term Rogers used for a teacher who creates an atmosphere favourable to learning. Exam trap: \"authoritative\" sounds positive because Diana Baumrind's authoritative parenting is the recommended style, but it is a parenting term and still teacher-centred; Rogers wanted learner-centred teaching.", "topic": "Rogers: teacher as facilitator of learning"}, {"q": "Maslow theory states that individuals who are well adjusted attempt to follow the following process:", "options": {"a": "Convince others that they have no faults", "b": "Actualized themselves", "c": "Win others to their way of thinking", "d": "Remain aloof from the rest of society"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: well-adjusted individuals try to actualize themselves. Abraham Maslow, a founder of humanistic psychology, proposed a hierarchy of needs (1943, expanded in Motivation and Personality, 1954): physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, and at the top self-actualization, the drive to become everything one is capable of becoming. He called the lower needs deficiency needs (D-needs) and the growth needs at the top being needs (B-needs). Maslow studied people he saw as psychologically healthy, such as Abraham Lincoln, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described self-actualizers as realistic, accepting of themselves and others, spontaneous, problem-centred, autonomous, creative and prone to peak experiences, with deep relationships and a feeling of kinship with humanity. A doctor who leaves a well-paid post to run a rural clinic because it fulfils her values is striving toward self-actualization. Option b is right because, for Maslow, self-actualization is the very process that marks healthy adjustment. Exam trap: Maslow noted that self-actualizers need privacy and are independent of their culture, which can tempt you toward \"remain aloof\"; but this is healthy detachment, not withdrawal from society, since they also have strong social interest.", "topic": "Maslow's self-actualization in well-adjusted people"}, {"q": "What does attribution theory primarily seek to explain?", "options": {"a": "How individuals perceive and interpret the behaviour of others", "b": "How individuals perceive themselves", "c": "How individuals develop personality traits", "d": "How individuals form friendships"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: attribution theory primarily seeks to explain how individuals perceive and interpret the behaviour of others. Fritz Heider, in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), argued that ordinary people act as \"naive psychologists\" who constantly ask why others behave as they do. His key distinction was between internal (dispositional) causes, such as ability, effort or personality, and external (situational) causes, such as luck, task difficulty or pressure from others. Later theorists built on this: Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) explained when we infer a person's traits from their acts (correspondent inference), Harold Kelley (1967) explained how we use consensus, consistency and distinctiveness, and Bernard Weiner added stability and controllability. Example: when a classmate snaps at you, you might decide she is rude (internal) or that she just got bad news (external), and that judgement shapes how you respond to her. Option a is right because the theory was built to explain how we make sense of other people's behaviour by assigning causes to it. Exam trap: attribution ideas were later applied to the self as well (Daryl Bem's self-perception theory), but that is an extension, not the primary aim.", "topic": "Purpose of attribution theory"}, {"q": "Which term refers to the tendency to attribute one's successes to internal factors and failures to external factors?", "options": {"a": "Fundamental attribution error", "b": "Self-serving bias", "c": "Actor-observer bias", "d": "Attributional bias"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: self-serving bias. The self-serving bias is the tendency to take credit for our successes by attributing them to internal causes (ability, effort) while blaming our failures on external causes (bad luck, an unfair examiner, a noisy hall). A student who tops a test says \"I worked hard\", but after failing says \"the paper was out of syllabus\". The usual explanation is motivational: the bias protects and enhances self-esteem, and it also helps us present ourselves well to others. It is one of several attributional biases studied in social psychology, along with the fundamental attribution error and the actor-observer bias. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the success-internal, failure-external pattern that defines the self-serving bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (named by Lee Ross in 1977) is about how we explain other people's behaviour, overestimating their dispositions and underestimating the situation; the self-serving bias is about explaining our own outcomes and depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. If the stem mentions success versus failure, think self-serving bias; if it mentions judging others' behaviour as personality-driven, think fundamental attribution error.", "topic": "Self-serving bias in attribution"}, {"q": "Which term describes the tendency to attribute one's own actions to external factors while attributing others' actions to internal factors?", "options": {"a": "Self-serving bias", "b": "Fundamental attribution error", "c": "Actor-observer bias", "d": "Ultimate attribution error"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: actor-observer bias. Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) proposed that people explain the same behaviour differently depending on whether they are the actor doing it or an observer watching it. As actors we tend to explain our own behaviour by the situation; as observers we tend to explain other people's behaviour by their personality. If you trip on the stairs you blame the wet floor, but when a stranger trips you think he is clumsy. Two reasons are usually given: perceptual salience (the actor sees the situation, while the observer's eyes are on the actor) and information (we know our own behaviour varies across situations, but we know little of the stranger's history). Option c is right because the stem has both halves, external for one's own actions and internal for others' actions, which is the defining contrast of the actor-observer bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error describes only the observer half (over-attributing others' behaviour to dispositions); the actor-observer bias adds the contrast with oneself. And the self-serving bias depends on whether the outcome was a success or a failure, not on whose behaviour it is.", "topic": "Actor-observer bias: self versus others"}, {"q": "Kelley's Attribution Theory focuses on:", "options": {"a": "Internal attributions only", "b": "External attributions only", "c": "Both internal and external attributions", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Kelley's theory deals with both internal and external attributions. Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) explains how, with repeated observations, we decide whether a behaviour is caused by the person (internal) or by the stimulus or situation (external). We use three kinds of information: consensus (do other people behave the same way toward this stimulus?), consistency (does this person behave this way toward it on other occasions?) and distinctiveness (does this person behave this way toward other stimuli?). Example: Arjun laughs at a comedian. If few others laugh (low consensus), Arjun laughs at this comedian every time (high consistency) and laughs at all comedians (low distinctiveness), we make an internal attribution: Arjun is easily amused. If everyone laughs (high consensus), Arjun always laughs at this comedian (high consistency) but rarely at others (high distinctiveness), we make an external attribution: the comedian is funny. Low consistency points to passing circumstances. Option c is right because the whole point of the model is to choose between internal and external causes. Exam trap: Jones and Davis's correspondent inference theory deals only with inferring internal dispositions, so \"internal attributions only\" describes them, not Kelley.", "topic": "Kelley's covariation model: internal and external"}, {"q": "Which is not a sampling method?", "options": {"a": "Cluster", "b": "Stratified", "c": "Random", "d": "Logistic"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: logistic is not a sampling method. Sampling is the process of choosing a subset (sample) from a population so that results can be generalised. Methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of selection: simple random sampling (lottery or random number tables), systematic sampling (every kth person after a random start), stratified random sampling (divide the population into strata such as gender or class, then sample randomly within each) and cluster sampling (randomly select whole naturally occurring groups, such as schools or villages, and study everyone in them). In non-probability sampling selection is not random: convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. \"Logistic\" is not a sampling method at all; the word belongs to logistic regression, a statistical technique for predicting a two-category (dichotomous) outcome, such as relapse versus no relapse, from one or more predictors. Option d is right because it names a data-analysis technique, not a way of selecting participants. Exam trap: examiners often slip an analysis term into a list of sampling methods; ask of each option \"is this a way of picking people, or a way of analysing data?\"", "topic": "Sampling methods vs statistical techniques"}, {"q": "In order to pursue the research, which of the following is priorly required?", "options": {"a": "Developing a research design", "b": "Formulating a research question", "c": "Deciding about the data analysis procedure", "d": "Formulating a research hypothesis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: formulating a research question is required before anything else. Research is usually described as a sequence of steps: identify and formulate the research problem or question, review the literature, state the hypothesis, choose a research design and sample, collect data, analyse the data, interpret the results and write the report. The research question is the starting point because every later decision depends on it. You cannot write a hypothesis until you know what you are asking, you cannot choose between an experiment and a survey without knowing what you want to find out, and the statistical test depends on the kind of question and data. For example, the question \"Does yoga reduce exam anxiety in college students?\" leads to the hypothesis \"Students who practise yoga will report lower anxiety than those who do not\", which in turn suggests a two-group design and a t-test. A good research question is clear, specific, feasible, researchable and significant. Option b is right because the question must exist before a hypothesis, design or analysis plan can be built. Exam trap: the hypothesis is sometimes thought to come first, but a hypothesis is a tentative answer to a question, so the question always comes before it.", "topic": "First step of research: the research question"}, {"q": "Questions in which only two alternatives are possible is called:", "options": {"a": "Multiple choice questions", "b": "Dichotomous questions", "c": "Open ended questions", "d": "Structured questions"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: dichotomous questions. A dichotomous question offers only two possible answers, such as yes or no, true or false, or agree or disagree. Example: \"Have you ever consulted a psychologist? Yes / No\". Such items are quick to answer, easy to code (often as 0 and 1) and easy to analyse, which makes them useful for screening and factual questions. Their drawbacks are that they force people into one of two boxes, lose information about degree or intensity, and invite guessing or acquiescence. Questionnaire items are often classified as closed (structured) or open. Closed questions give fixed answer choices and include dichotomous items (two choices), multiple-choice items (three or more choices) and rating scales; open-ended questions let respondents answer in their own words. Option b is right because \"only two alternatives\" is the defining feature of a dichotomous question; the word comes from the Greek for \"cut in two\". Exam trap: dichotomous questions are a kind of structured question, so \"structured\" is not false, but it is the broader category; when the stem specifies exactly two alternatives, the precise answer is dichotomous.", "topic": "Dichotomous questions in questionnaires"}, {"q": "Which one is called non-probability sampling?", "options": {"a": "Quota sampling", "b": "Cluster sampling", "c": "Systematic sampling", "d": "Stratified random sampling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: quota sampling is a non-probability method. In non-probability sampling the chance of any member being selected is unknown, because selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience rather than on chance. In quota sampling the researcher first decides how many people of each type are needed, usually to mirror the population on characteristics such as gender, age or religion, and then fills each quota with whoever is available. For example, an interviewer told to survey 50 men and 50 women at a mall will stop passers-by until both quotas are full; the proportions look right, but who gets picked within each quota is not random. Other non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement) and snowball sampling. Option a is right because quota sampling fills subgroups by convenience, so it lacks random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum (probability), while quota sampling does not (non-probability).", "topic": "Quota sampling as non-probability sampling"}, {"q": "Random sampling is also called ______.", "options": {"a": "Availability sampling", "b": "Probation sampling", "c": "Probability sampling", "d": "Prospect sampling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: random sampling is also called probability sampling. Probability sampling means that every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, so selection is governed by chance rather than by the researcher's choice. This is why many textbooks use \"random sampling\" and \"probability sampling\" as rough synonyms. Strictly speaking, simple random sampling (every member has an equal chance, as in drawing names from a hat or using random number tables) is the basic type, and systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling are other forms of probability sampling. Because selection is by chance, probability samples allow sampling error to be estimated and results to be generalised to the population with stated confidence. For example, to survey 200 of 2,000 college students, you could number all the students and let a computer pick 200 numbers at random. Option c is right because random selection is the defining feature of probability sampling. Exam trap: the opposite family, non-probability sampling, includes availability (convenience), purposive, quota and snowball sampling, where the chance of selection is unknown and results cannot be generalised with the same confidence.", "topic": "Random sampling as probability sampling"}, {"q": "SPSS is an acronym of the following:", "options": {"a": "Statistical Predictions for Social Sciences", "b": "Sexual Preferences for the Sixties and Seventies", "c": "Statistical Package for the Social Sciences", "d": "Sexual Performance and SAD Syndrome"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: SPSS stands for Statistical Package for the Social Sciences. It is a computer program for entering, managing and statistically analysing data, developed in 1968 at Stanford University by Norman Nie, Dale Bent and C. Hadlai (Tex) Hull to help social scientists analyse survey data. IBM acquired SPSS Inc. in 2009, and the program is now sold as IBM SPSS Statistics. Data are entered in a spreadsheet-like Data View, with each row a participant and each column a variable, while the Variable View defines each variable's name, type, labels and level of measurement. Analyses run from menus or from syntax commands and cover descriptive statistics, t-tests, ANOVA, correlation, regression, factor analysis, chi-square and non-parametric tests. A psychology student who has collected anxiety scores from 100 participants would typically enter them in SPSS and run an independent-samples t-test to compare men and women. Option c is right because it gives the program's actual full name. Exam trap: option a sounds technical and plausible, but SPSS is a \"package\" of statistical procedures, not \"predictions\". Other common packages are R, SAS, Stata and JASP.", "topic": "SPSS: Statistical Package for the Social Sciences"}, {"q": "Which test is the part of the parametric test?", "options": {"a": "Sign Test", "b": "Run Test for Randomness", "c": "Kruskal-Wallis Test", "d": "Z-test"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the z-test is a parametric test. Parametric tests estimate or compare population parameters such as the mean, and they rest on assumptions: the data are on an interval or ratio scale, the population (or sampling distribution) is approximately normal, and, when groups are compared, variances are roughly equal. The main parametric tests are the z-test, t-tests, ANOVA and Pearson's correlation. A z-test compares a sample mean with a population mean, or two means, when the population standard deviation is known or the sample is large (commonly above 30). For example, testing whether the mean IQ of 100 children in a school differs from the population mean of 100 (SD 15) uses a z-test. Non-parametric (distribution-free) tests make fewer assumptions and usually work with ranks, signs or frequencies; they suit ordinal data, small samples or skewed distributions. Option d is right because it is the only test listed that assumes normality and works directly with means and standard deviations. Exam trap: learn the pairs: independent t-test with Mann-Whitney U, paired t-test with Wilcoxon signed-rank (or the sign test), one-way ANOVA with Kruskal-Wallis, and Pearson r with Spearman rho.", "topic": "Parametric vs non-parametric tests"}, {"q": "The split-half method is used as a test of:", "options": {"a": "Stability", "b": "Internal reliability", "c": "Inter-observer consistency", "d": "External validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the split-half method is a test of internal reliability (internal consistency). Reliability means consistency of measurement. In the split-half method a test is given once, then divided into two comparable halves, most often odd-numbered versus even-numbered items, and scores on the two halves are correlated. If the items measure the same thing, people who do well on one half should do well on the other. Because each half is only half as long as the full test, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the whole test; it is corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula: full-test reliability = 2r / (1 + r). If the halves correlate 0.60, the corrected reliability is 1.20 / 1.60 = 0.75. Other internal consistency methods are the Kuder-Richardson formulas (KR-20, KR-21) and Cronbach's alpha, which in effect average across all possible splits. Option b is right because the split-half method needs only one administration and checks whether the items hang together. Exam trap: stability over time is test-retest reliability, which needs two administrations, and equivalence is parallel-forms reliability; split-half is neither.", "topic": "Split-half method and internal consistency"}, {"q": "The degree of freedom for paired t-test based on n pairs of observations is:", "options": {"a": "2n-1", "b": "n-2", "c": "2(n-1)", "d": "n-1"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: n - 1. Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values free to vary once a statistic has been fixed; with n scores and a known mean, n - 1 of them can vary but the last one is determined. A paired (dependent or related samples) t-test is used when each score in one condition is matched with a score in the other, as in a before-and-after design or matched pairs. The test works on the difference score for each pair: it computes the mean and standard deviation of the n differences and tests whether the mean difference is zero. Because it is really a one-sample t-test on n difference scores, df = n - 1. Example: anxiety is measured in 15 patients before and after therapy; there are 15 pairs, so df = 14. Option d is right because n pairs give n difference scores, and one df is lost in estimating their mean. Exam trap: for an independent-samples t-test, df = n1 + n2 - 2, which equals 2(n - 1) when both groups have n people, so option c is the independent-groups answer; and n - 2 is the df for testing the significance of a Pearson correlation.", "topic": "Degrees of freedom for paired t-test"}, {"q": "The strange situation study is related to:", "options": {"a": "Schaffer and Emerson", "b": "Main and Solomon", "c": "Mary Ainsworth", "d": "Jean Piaget"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Mary Ainsworth. The Strange Situation is a laboratory procedure developed by Mary Ainsworth (first described with Barbara Wittig in 1969 and fully reported in Patterns of Attachment, 1978, with Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall) to assess the quality of an infant's attachment to the caregiver. Infants of about 12 to 18 months go through eight short episodes, each about 3 minutes, in an unfamiliar playroom: mother and infant explore, a stranger enters, the mother leaves and later returns, the infant is left alone, and so on. Observers note how the infant uses the mother as a secure base for exploration, how upset it is at separation and, above all, how it behaves at reunion. Ainsworth identified three patterns: secure (Type B), anxious-avoidant (Type A) and anxious-resistant or ambivalent (Type C), and linked them to the mother's sensitivity, based on home observations in Baltimore and earlier fieldwork in Uganda. Option c is right because the procedure was created by Ainsworth. Exam trap: Mary Main and Judith Solomon later used the Strange Situation to add a fourth, disorganized pattern (Type D), so their names are linked with it, but they did not create it.", "topic": "Ainsworth's Strange Situation procedure"}, {"q": "A child throws regular tantrum in eating dinner and the parents are suggested to decrease the quantity of food at tea time and praising the child when the child eats dinner properly. This is known as:", "options": {"a": "Negative and positive reinforcement", "b": "Antecedent operation and consequent operation", "c": "Systematic de-sensitization", "d": "Differential reinforcement"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: antecedent operation and consequent operation. Behaviour analysts describe behaviour with the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens before), the Behaviour itself, and Consequences (what follows). Behaviour can be changed from either end. An antecedent operation alters conditions before the behaviour so that it becomes more or less likely; an important kind is the motivating (establishing) operation, a concept developed by Jack Michael, which changes how valuable a reinforcer is. Food deprivation, for example, makes food more reinforcing. A consequent operation alters what follows the behaviour, such as reinforcement, extinction or punishment. Here the parents cut down the tea-time snack, so the child arrives at dinner hungry and eating becomes more attractive: an antecedent (establishing) operation. They then praise the child for eating dinner properly: a consequent operation (positive reinforcement). Option b is right because the plan combines one change before the behaviour with one change after it, and only option b names both parts. Exam trap: option a is tempting because praise is positive reinforcement, but reducing the snack is not negative reinforcement. Negative reinforcement means removing an unpleasant stimulus after a behaviour; the snack reduction happens before dinner and does not depend on how the child behaves.", "topic": "Antecedent and consequent operations in behaviour change"}, {"q": "Which personality theory is also called the Tripartite Theory?", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung", "b": "Eysenck", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Carl Rogers"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud. Freud's structural model of the mind, set out in The Ego and the Id (1923), divides personality into three parts, which is why it is called the tripartite theory. The id is present from birth, entirely unconscious, and seeks immediate gratification of instinctual drives according to the pleasure principle, using primary process thinking. The ego develops to deal with reality; it follows the reality principle, uses secondary process (logical) thinking and mediates between the id, the superego and the outside world. The superego, which forms as the child resolves the Oedipus complex by internalising parental and social standards, contains the conscience (which punishes with guilt) and the ego ideal (which rewards with pride). Example: a hungry student in a lecture wants to grab a snack (id), feels it would be rude (superego) and decides to eat at the break (ego). Option c is right because \"tripartite\" refers to Freud's three-part structure of id, ego and superego. Exam trap: Freud's earlier topographic model (conscious, preconscious, unconscious) also has three parts, but it describes levels of awareness, not structures of personality; and Eysenck's three dimensions (P, E, N) are traits, not a tripartite structure.", "topic": "Freud's tripartite structural model"}, {"q": "Who was the proposer of the following model of intelligence?<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Figure: Guilford-style cube with OPERATIONS, PRODUCTS and CONTENT axes\" style=\"display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:12px auto 4px;background:#fff;border-radius:6px;padding:6px\">", "options": {"a": "Guilford", "b": "Spearman", "c": "Thurstone", "d": "Sternberg"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Guilford. The cube in the figure is J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect (SI) model, introduced in the 1950s and set out fully in The Nature of Human Intelligence (1967). Guilford rejected a single general factor and proposed that every intellectual ability is a combination of three dimensions. Operations (what the mind does): cognition, memory, divergent production, convergent production and evaluation. Contents (what it works on): figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural. Products (the form the result takes): units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications. Each small block of the cube is one ability, so the original model had 5 x 4 x 6 = 120 abilities. Guilford later split figural content into visual and auditory (150 abilities) and memory into memory recording and memory retention (180 abilities). Example: listing as many uses for a brick as possible is divergent production of semantic units. Option a is right because the three-faced cube of operations, contents and products is uniquely Guilford's. Exam trap: Guilford's divergent production is the origin of modern creativity tests, so link him with creativity too; and do not confuse his 120 independent abilities with Thurstone's seven primary mental abilities, which were also multiple but far fewer.", "topic": "Guilford's Structure of Intellect model"}, {"q": "Harry Harlow's experiment on maternal deprivation and social isolation in 1950s and 1960s explored:", "options": {"a": "Impact of early bonds on behaviour and functioning", "b": "Impact of social isolation on personality development", "c": "Impact of maternal deprivation on developmental milestones", "d": "Impact of insecure relationship on emotional development"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: impact of early bonds on behaviour and functioning. Harry Harlow, working with infant rhesus monkeys at the University of Wisconsin, separated babies from their mothers and raised them with two surrogate mothers: one of bare wire that held a feeding bottle, and one covered in soft terry cloth that gave no food. The infants spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother and ran to her when frightened, visiting the wire mother only to feed. In his 1958 paper \"The Nature of Love\" Harlow concluded that contact comfort, not feeding, is the basis of the infant's bond, contradicting the drive-reduction or \"cupboard love\" view. His later isolation studies reared monkeys alone for months; they developed rocking, self-clasping, fearfulness, aggression and severe social difficulties, and isolated females later neglected or abused their own infants. Effects of shorter isolation could be partly reversed by contact with younger \"therapist\" monkeys. Option a is right because it is the broad conclusion that covers both programmes: early bonds shape later social, emotional and parenting behaviour. Exam trap: options b, c and d each describe only a slice of the work, or the wrong one; also keep Harlow (monkeys, contact comfort) apart from John Bowlby (the human maternal deprivation hypothesis).", "topic": "Harlow's monkey studies on early bonds"}, {"q": "The following figure is an example of:<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Figure: a diagonal line interrupted by a vertical bar\" style=\"display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:12px auto 4px;background:#fff;border-radius:6px;padding:6px\">", "options": {"a": "Phantaglyphs", "b": "Ternus illusion", "c": "Orbison illusion", "d": "Poggendorff illusion"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Poggendorff illusion. In the figure a straight diagonal line passes behind a vertical bar, and on the far side two parallel candidate lines emerge. The segment that looks like the continuation is usually not the true one, because the line seems to shift out of alignment as it crosses the gap. The second panel makes the bar see-through and shows which segment is truly collinear with the first. The illusion is named after the German physicist Johann Christian Poggendorff and was first reported in 1860. It is a geometric illusion of direction and alignment, in the same family as the Zöllner, Hering and Müller-Lyer figures. One classic explanation is that the acute angles where the line meets the edges of the bar are perceptually enlarged, which bends the apparent path of each segment; other accounts appeal to depth, since the bar looks like an object lying in front of the line. Option d is right because an interrupted oblique line whose two halves look misaligned is the defining Poggendorff figure. Exam trap: the Zöllner illusion also uses oblique lines, but there long parallel lines look tilted because of short crossing hatch marks; in the Poggendorff, a single straight line looks broken across a gap.", "topic": "Poggendorff illusion of misaligned diagonals"}, {"q": "According to Luria, the fundamental symptoms in schizophrenia essentially are:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive in nature", "b": "Affective in nature", "c": "Positive symptoms", "d": "Negative symptoms"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: Standard textbooks do not credit Alexander Luria, the Soviet neuropsychologist, with a theory of the \"fundamental symptoms\" of schizophrenia; that phrase belongs to Eugen Bleuler (1911), so the item has probably mixed up the names. The official key (a, cognitive) still fits Bleuler's view and the modern one, so answer a in the exam. The answer is a: cognitive in nature. Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist who coined the term schizophrenia (a splitting of psychic functions, not a split personality), divided its symptoms into fundamental symptoms, present in every case, and accessory symptoms, such as hallucinations, delusions and catatonia, which may or may not appear. The fundamental symptoms are the four As: loosening of Associations, disturbed Affect, Ambivalence and Autism (withdrawal into an inner world). Bleuler regarded the disturbance of associations, a disorder of thinking, as the primary one. Modern research agrees that cognitive impairment (attention, working memory, processing speed, executive function) is a core, early feature that predicts functional outcome. Option a is right because the fundamental disturbance is a cognitive one. Exam trap: positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions are Bleuler's accessory symptoms and Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms, not the fundamental ones.", "topic": "Cognitive core of schizophrenia's fundamental symptoms"}, {"q": "Children from families where parents may serve as both a source of comfort and fear may develop:", "options": {"a": "Avoidant attachment", "b": "Disorganized attachment", "c": "Ambivalent attachment", "d": "Secured attachment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: disorganized attachment. Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation originally sorted infants into three patterns: secure, avoidant and ambivalent (resistant). Some infants fitted none of them, and in 1986 and 1990 Mary Main and Judith Solomon described a fourth, disorganized/disoriented pattern (Type D). These infants show no coherent strategy at reunion: they may approach the parent with the head turned away, freeze, rock, look dazed, or start toward the parent and then back off. Main and Erik Hesse explained why: the parent is frightening (for example, abusive) or frightened (for example, haunted by unresolved trauma or loss). The child is caught in a paradox, because the person it should run to for safety is also the source of fear, so approach and avoidance are triggered at once, a state described as \"fright without solution\". Disorganized attachment is common among maltreated children and predicts later dissociation and behaviour problems. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this: a parent who is both a source of comfort and a source of fear. Exam trap: ambivalent attachment comes from inconsistent but not frightening care; the infant clings and resists at the same time, yet still has an organised strategy, while the disorganized infant has none.", "topic": "Disorganized attachment from frightening caregivers"}, {"q": "People tend to see cause-and-effect relationships is a part of:", "options": {"a": "Hurlock's child development theory", "b": "Erik Erikson's psychosocial development theory", "c": "Guilford's Intelligence theory", "d": "Heider's attribution theory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d, Heider's attribution theory. Attribution is the process by which we explain why a behaviour or event happened. Fritz Heider, an Austrian-born psychologist, set out the idea in his 1958 book The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations. He argued that ordinary people act like \"naive scientists\": we need the social world to feel orderly and predictable, so we constantly look for cause-and-effect links behind what people do. Heider's key distinction was between internal (personal or dispositional) causes, such as ability, effort or personality, and external (situational) causes, such as luck, task difficulty or pressure from others. For example, if a classmate fails an exam, you may decide she is lazy (internal) or that the paper was unfair (external). Later theories built on this base: Edward Jones and Keith Davis's correspondent inference theory (1965), Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) and Bernard Weiner's model of success and failure. Option d is right because the stem describes the core assumption of attribution theory, the human tendency to perceive cause-and-effect relationships in behaviour. The exam trap is Heider's other famous idea, balance theory, which is about keeping our likes and dislikes of people and objects consistent, not about explaining causes.", "topic": "Heider's attribution theory: perceiving causes"}, {"q": "When a person assigns the cause of behavior to some internal characteristic of a person rather than to outside forces, that is known as:", "options": {"a": "Social desirability", "b": "Situational attribution", "c": "Dispositional attribution", "d": "Incidental behaviour"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c, dispositional attribution. When we explain a behaviour we can locate its cause inside the person or outside the person. Fritz Heider (1958) called these internal and external attributions; today they are usually called dispositional and situational attributions. A dispositional attribution says the behaviour reflects something stable about the person, such as a trait, attitude, ability or motive: \"Ravi shouted at the waiter because he is rude.\" A situational attribution says the behaviour was produced by circumstances: \"Ravi shouted because the waiter spilled hot tea on him.\" Which kind we make matters, because it shapes how we judge, blame, help or punish people. Harold Kelley's covariation model later described the information (consensus, distinctiveness and consistency) we use to choose between the two. Option c is right because the stem defines exactly this: assigning the cause to an internal characteristic of the person rather than to outside forces. The exam trap is option b, situational attribution, which is the direct opposite; remember that \"disposition\" means something inside the person, while \"situation\" means something around the person.", "topic": "Dispositional versus situational attribution"}, {"q": "The ability of the therapist to share the client's emotions is an important process known as:", "options": {"a": "Reflection", "b": "Empathy", "c": "Catharsis", "d": "Transference"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b, empathy. Empathy is the therapist's ability to sense and understand the client's feelings and personal meanings as if they were the therapist's own, while never losing that \"as if\" quality. Carl Rogers made it central to client-centred (person-centred) therapy. In his 1957 paper on the \"necessary and sufficient conditions\" of therapeutic change he named three therapist conditions: accurate empathic understanding, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). For example, when a client says \"Nobody at home even notices me\", an empathic therapist grasps the loneliness and hurt behind the words and conveys that understanding, instead of judging or giving advice. Meta-analyses of psychotherapy research find that therapist empathy is reliably related to better outcome. Option b is right because the stem asks for the therapist's ability to share and understand the client's emotions, which is the definition of empathy. The exam trap is to confuse empathy with sympathy or with reflection: sympathy is feeling sorry for the client from the outside, and reflection is a technique (restating feelings) through which empathy is communicated, while empathy is the underlying understanding itself.", "topic": "Empathy as a Rogerian core condition"}, {"q": "The figure given below depicts which theory of personality?<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Figure: concentric circles labelled conscious (ego), personal unconscious, collective unconscious, persona, shadow, anima, animus\" style=\"display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:12px auto 4px;background:#fff;border-radius:6px;padding:6px\">", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Carl Jung", "c": "Eysenck", "d": "Erikson"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b, Carl Jung. The figure shows Jung's three-layer model of the psyche from his analytical psychology. Carl Gustav Jung, a Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud around 1913, described an outer layer of consciousness organised by the ego; beneath it the personal unconscious, holding forgotten or repressed material grouped into emotionally charged \"complexes\"; and at the deepest level the collective unconscious, an inherited store of experience shared by all humankind. The contents of the collective unconscious are archetypes, universal predispositions to perceive and react in certain ways. The figure names four of them: the persona (the social mask we show to others), the shadow (the dark, animal side we deny in ourselves), the anima (the feminine side of a man) and the animus (the masculine side of a woman). Jung's central archetype, the self, strives for wholeness through individuation. Option b is right because the collective unconscious and the archetypes are uniquely Jungian; no other theorist in the options uses them. The exam trap is option a: Freud also layered the mind (conscious, preconscious and unconscious, drawn as an iceberg, plus id, ego and superego), but Freud's unconscious is personal, built from one's own repressed wishes, with no collective layer or archetypes.", "topic": "Jung's model of the psyche and archetypes"}, {"q": "The predisposition of an individual to evaluate some symbol or object or aspect of his world in a favourable or unfavourable manner is defined as:", "options": {"a": "Judgmental heuristics", "b": "Cognitive bias", "c": "Social prediction", "d": "Attitudes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d, attitudes. An attitude is a learned, relatively lasting evaluation of an \"attitude object\", which can be a person, group, idea, symbol or thing, as favourable or unfavourable. The wording in the stem is Daniel Katz's classic 1960 definition: an attitude is the predisposition of the individual to evaluate some symbol or object or aspect of his world in a favourable or unfavourable manner. Katz also proposed that attitudes serve four functions: utilitarian (adjustive), ego-defensive, value-expressive and knowledge. Attitudes are commonly described by the ABC model with three components: affective (how you feel about the object), behavioural (how you tend to act toward it) and cognitive (what you believe about it). For example, a student who enjoys yoga, believes it reduces stress and attends classes regularly holds a favourable attitude toward yoga. Option d is right because the key words \"predisposition\" and \"evaluate in a favourable or unfavourable manner\" are the defining features of an attitude. The exam trap is to choose a thinking-process term such as heuristics or bias: those describe how we think or where we go wrong, while an attitude is an evaluative stance toward a particular object.", "topic": "Attitude: Katz's classic definition"}, {"q": "Uncompleted or interrupted tasks are remembered better than completed ones. This is which type of memory bias?", "options": {"a": "Von Restorff effects", "b": "Primacy effects", "c": "Zeigarnik effects", "d": "Modality effects"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, the Zeigarnik effect. This is the tendency to remember tasks that were interrupted or left unfinished better than tasks that were completed. It was demonstrated by Bluma Zeigarnik, a student of Kurt Lewin in Berlin, in work published in 1927. The story goes that Lewin noticed a waiter who remembered every detail of unpaid orders but forgot them once the bill was settled. Zeigarnik gave participants a series of simple tasks (puzzles, modelling, arithmetic) and interrupted some before they were finished; later, people recalled the interrupted tasks more often than the completed ones. Lewin's field theory explained this as a \"tension system\": starting a goal creates psychological tension that keeps the task active in memory until the goal is reached, and finishing releases it. A familiar example is an unanswered question that keeps returning to your mind. Option c is right because the stem defines exactly the Zeigarnik effect: better memory for uncompleted or interrupted tasks. The exam trap is the Von Restorff effect, which is also a memory-advantage effect but is about distinctiveness (an item that stands out from the rest is remembered better), whereas Zeigarnik is about incompleteness.", "topic": "Zeigarnik effect: memory for unfinished tasks"}, {"q": "When we explain the behavior of others, we look for enduring internal attributions, such as personality traits. This is known as:", "options": {"a": "Fundamental attribution errors", "b": "Selection bias", "c": "Cognitive errors generalization", "d": "Sampling bias"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a, the fundamental attribution error. This is our tendency, when explaining other people's behaviour, to overestimate internal (dispositional) causes such as personality traits and to underestimate the influence of the situation. Lee Ross named it in 1977; it is also called the correspondence bias. The classic demonstration is Edward Jones and Victor Harris's 1967 study: students read essays for or against Fidel Castro and were told the writer had been assigned the position, yet they still judged that the writer really held the view expressed. In daily life, if a driver cuts in front of you, you think \"what a reckless person\" and do not consider that he may be rushing to a hospital. One explanation is that the person is the most noticeable thing we see, while situational pressures are invisible. The bias is weaker in collectivist cultures, which pay more attention to context. Option a is right because the stem describes looking for enduring internal causes such as traits when explaining others' behaviour. The exam trap is the actor-observer effect: the fundamental attribution error is about how we explain others, while the actor-observer effect is the contrast between explaining others by their dispositions and explaining our own behaviour by the situation.", "topic": "Fundamental attribution error (correspondence bias)"}, {"q": "Which one of the statements is not correct?", "options": {"a": "Aditi smokes a cigarette when she goes out for a meal with her friend. If her friend smokes, her behavior is high in consensus.", "b": "Aditi smokes a cigarette when she goes out for a meal with her friend. If she only smokes, her behavior is low in consensus.", "c": "If Aditi only smokes when she is out with friends, her behavior is high in distinctiveness.", "d": "If Aditi smokes at any time or place, her distinctiveness is low but consensus is high."}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d, because it is the only incorrect statement. Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) says we decide whether a behaviour has an internal or an external cause by checking three kinds of information. Consensus asks whether other people behave the same way in the same situation. Distinctiveness asks whether this person behaves this way only in this situation or with this stimulus, or in many situations. Consistency asks whether the person behaves this way in this situation repeatedly over time. Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency lead to an internal attribution (\"it is something about Aditi\"); high consensus, high distinctiveness and high consistency lead to an external attribution (\"it is the situation\"). Applying this: if Aditi's friend also smokes, consensus is high (a); if only Aditi smokes, consensus is low (b); if she smokes only when out with friends, distinctiveness is high (c). Option d is right as the answer because its second half is wrong: smoking at any time or place does mean low distinctiveness, but it says nothing about whether other people smoke, so it cannot make consensus high. The exam trap is mixing the three up: consensus is always about other people, distinctiveness is about the same person across different situations, and consistency is about the same person in the same situation over time.", "topic": "Kelley covariation: consensus versus distinctiveness"}, {"q": "Progressive series of changes that occur in an orderly predictable pattern as a result of maturation and experience in a human being was proposed in:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud's psychosexual development theory", "b": "Elizabeth Hurlock's life-span development theory", "c": "Lawrence Kohlberg's moral development theory", "d": "Erik Erikson's psychosocial development theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b, Elizabeth Hurlock's life-span development theory. Elizabeth B. Hurlock was an American developmental psychologist whose textbooks, Child Development and Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach, are standard reading in Indian courses. She defined development as a progressive series of orderly, coherent changes resulting from maturation and experience. \"Progressive\" means the changes are directional, leading forward rather than backward; \"orderly\" and \"coherent\" mean each stage builds on the one before in a predictable pattern; \"maturation and experience\" means development is the joint product of biological unfolding and learning. Hurlock also listed principles of development: early foundations are critical, the pattern is predictable, all individuals are different, each period has characteristic behaviour and social expectations (developmental tasks), and each stage carries potential hazards. She traced development across the whole life span, from the prenatal period to old age. For example, babies sit before they stand and stand before they walk in every culture, though the exact ages differ between children. Option b is right because the stem quotes Hurlock's own definition of development. The exam trap is to pick a famous stage theorist: Freud, Kohlberg and Erikson each describe one domain of development, not a general definition of development itself.", "topic": "Hurlock's definition of development"}, {"q": "The author Gilligan in her book \"In a Different Voice: Psychological Theory and Women's Development\" emphasizes on:", "options": {"a": "Since women focus more on relationships, they choose to suppress their own voices.", "b": "Women's psychosocial development is primarily different than men depending upon the permissiveness of the society.", "c": "Women are not deficient but different in giving moral reasoning during moral dilemmas.", "d": "Men and women are physically and biologically different, therefore they are psychologically different."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: women are not deficient but different in their moral reasoning. Carol Gilligan, an American psychologist who worked with Lawrence Kohlberg at Harvard, published In a Different Voice in 1982. She criticised Kohlberg's stage theory because it was built on interviews with boys and men and treated an abstract \"ethic of justice\" (rights, rules, fairness) as the highest form of morality. When women were scored on Kohlberg's scale they often landed at Stage 3 (the \"good boy, good girl\" stage), which made them look morally less developed. Gilligan argued that many women reason from an \"ethic of care\", which stresses relationships, responsibility and avoiding harm. From interviews, including with women deciding about abortion, she proposed three levels: concern for individual survival, goodness as self-sacrifice, and a morality of nonviolence that balances care for self and others. Option c is right because the book's central claim is that women speak in a different moral voice, not a weaker one. The exam trap is option a: Gilligan did describe women suppressing their own needs, but only as the middle \"self-sacrifice\" level to be outgrown, not as her main thesis. Note also that later research finds only small gender differences in care versus justice reasoning.", "topic": "Gilligan's ethic of care: different, not deficient"}, {"q": "Which of the following statement is false about Piaget's concept development theory?", "options": {"a": "Schemata are mental models that help us disseminate information to others.", "b": "Assimilation is when we take in information that is comparable to what we already know.", "c": "Accommodation is when we change our schemata based on new information.", "d": "All children try to strike a balance between assimilation and accommodation using a mechanism he called equilibration."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a, because it is the false statement. Jean Piaget, the Swiss developmental psychologist, described the child as an active builder of knowledge. The basic units of knowledge are schemas (schemata): organised patterns of action or thought that a child uses to understand and interpret experience, such as a sucking schema in infancy or a \"dog\" schema later. Schemas grow through adaptation, which has two complementary processes. Assimilation is taking in new information by fitting it into an existing schema (calling a new dog \"dog\"). Accommodation is changing an existing schema, or creating a new one, when information does not fit (learning that a cow is not a dog). Equilibration is the self-regulating drive to restore balance between assimilation and accommodation; when new experience creates a mismatch (disequilibrium), the child is pushed to a higher level of understanding. Option a is right as the answer because schemas are internal frameworks for organising and interpreting information for oneself, not models for spreading or \"disseminating\" information to others. The exam trap is to hunt for small wording flaws in b, c and d; they are standard textbook definitions, and the error to spot is the wrong purpose given to schemas in a.", "topic": "Piaget: schemas, assimilation, accommodation, equilibration"}, {"q": "Which one of the followings is an example of assimilation?", "options": {"a": "A child's schema of \"dog\" based on the family's golden retriever being expanded to include two newly adopted golden retrievers.", "b": "The same child's schema of \"dog\" being adjusted to exclude other four-legged furry animals such as sheep and foxes.", "c": "When child balances his/her schema of \"dog\" and \"cow\" when exposed to both kinds of animals together.", "d": "A child's schema of \"dog\" fails to differentiate from his/her schema of \"cow\"."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a. In Jean Piaget's theory a schema is an organised mental framework for a category of experience. Assimilation means taking new experience into an existing schema without changing the schema; accommodation means changing the schema because the new experience does not fit. Imagine a child whose \"dog\" schema comes from the family's golden retriever: four legs, furry, barks, friendly. When the family adopts two more golden retrievers, they match the existing schema perfectly, so the child simply applies the \"dog\" schema to them. The schema now covers more animals, but its rules are unchanged; that is assimilation. If the same child then sees a sheep and calls it \"dog\", and the parent says \"no, that is a sheep\", the child must modify the schema so that it excludes sheep and foxes; that is accommodation. Option a is right because the new golden retrievers fit the existing schema and are absorbed into it unchanged, the textbook definition of assimilation. The exam trap is the word \"expanded\" in option a, which tempts students toward accommodation; what matters is whether the schema's rules change (accommodation) or the same schema is simply applied to more cases (assimilation).", "topic": "Assimilation versus accommodation: worked examples"}, {"q": "In Piaget vs Vygotsky argument which of the followings is/are true?", "options": {"a": "Piaget believed intelligence as a product of internal factors while Vygotsky placed more emphasis on influence of external factors.", "b": "Piaget postulated that a child learned to think first, and then from that thought, speak, whereas Vygotsky believed that thought and speech were separate, intact processes.", "c": "Both \"A\" and \"B\"", "d": "Neither \"A\" nor \"B\""}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c (both A and B). Statement B oversimplifies Lev Vygotsky, who held that thought and speech have separate roots but merge at about age two into verbal thought, so they do not stay separate; the setter clearly accepts B's contrast as true, so answer c in the exam. Jean Piaget saw the child as building knowledge through his or her own actions; development is driven largely from within, by maturation and equilibration, and learning follows development. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist, stressed that higher mental functions are social in origin: they arise through interaction with more skilled people and through cultural tools such as language, as in his zone of proximal development (what a child can do with help but not yet alone). On language, Piaget held that thought comes first and speech reflects it, so egocentric speech simply mirrors egocentric thought and fades away. Vygotsky argued that early thought and early speech develop separately, then join, and that private speech turns inward as inner speech that guides thinking. Option c is right because the key accepts both A (internal versus external emphasis) and B (thought first versus separate origins). The exam trap is egocentric speech: for Piaget it shows immature thought, for Vygotsky it is a self-guidance tool that becomes inner speech.", "topic": "Piaget versus Vygotsky: development, thought and speech"}, {"q": "Who amongst the followings for the first time distinguished psychology from biology and philosophy and is considered as father of modern psychology?", "options": {"a": "JB Watson", "b": "Wilhelm M Wundt", "c": "Ivan Pavlov", "d": "Albert Bandura"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt. Before Wundt, questions about the mind were handled by philosophers (through reasoning) and by physiologists (through studies of nerves and senses). Wundt, a German physician and physiologist, argued that the mind could be studied as a separate experimental science. His book Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) set out the programme, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig he founded the first laboratory devoted to psychological research, which is why 1879 is often given as the birth year of psychology as an independent science. He studied immediate conscious experience, such as sensations, feelings and reaction times, using controlled introspection, founded the first psychology journal and trained many students who spread psychology worldwide, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin. Option b is right because Wundt is the person credited with separating psychology from philosophy and physiology (the \"biology\" of the stem), and he is called the father of modern or experimental psychology. The exam trap is to choose the founder of a later school: John B. Watson founded behaviourism and Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, but both worked after psychology already existed as a science.", "topic": "Wundt: founder of modern experimental psychology"}, {"q": "Which of the following learning theories states that behavior is based on how one interacts with and is influenced by their environment?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive learning theory", "b": "Behavioral learning theory", "c": "Social learning theory", "d": "Constructivist learning theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c, social learning theory. The stem's wording also fits behavioural learning theory (behaviourism), which holds that behaviour is shaped by the environment, so b is a reasonable reading; in the exam answer c, taking \"interacts with\" as the cue for Bandura's two-way interaction. Social learning theory was developed by Albert Bandura from the 1960s and later renamed social cognitive theory. It holds that people learn by observing others in their social environment (observational learning or modelling), and that such learning depends on attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Bandura's key idea is reciprocal determinism: behaviour, personal-cognitive factors (beliefs, expectations, self-efficacy) and the environment all influence one another. A child who sees an older sibling praised for tidying up may start tidying; the child's tidying then changes how the parents respond, which further shapes the child. Option c is right under the key because social learning theory describes behaviour as the product of continuous interaction with, and influence from, the social environment. The exam trap is behaviourism: in strict behaviourism (John B. Watson, B. F. Skinner) influence runs one way, from environmental consequences to behaviour, with no role for thought, whereas Bandura's theory makes it two-way and includes cognition.", "topic": "Bandura's social learning and reciprocal determinism"}, {"q": "Which of the followings proved that learning occurs through association?", "options": {"a": "Proximal conditioning", "b": "Instrumental conditioning", "c": "Classical conditioning", "d": "Operant conditioning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, classical conditioning. Classical conditioning is learning in which a neutral stimulus comes to produce a response after being repeatedly paired with a stimulus that already produces that response. It was discovered by Ivan Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, around the turn of the 20th century while he was studying digestion in dogs. Food in the mouth (unconditioned stimulus, UCS) naturally produced salivation (unconditioned response, UCR). When a neutral sound such as a metronome or bell (conditioned stimulus, CS) was presented just before the food again and again, the sound alone came to produce salivation (conditioned response, CR). Pavlov thus showed experimentally that an organism learns an association between two stimuli, giving the philosophers' old laws of association (contiguity, repetition) a laboratory method. In daily life, feeling anxious at the smell of a hospital after a painful treatment is a conditioned association. Option c is right because Pavlov's classical conditioning is the classic demonstration that learning occurs through association, specifically stimulus-stimulus association. The exam trap is operant conditioning: it is also associative, but the association is between a response and its consequence (reinforcement or punishment), and its classic names are Edward Thorndike and B. F. Skinner, not Pavlov.", "topic": "Classical conditioning as associative learning"}, {"q": "Which is not a correct statement about Albert Bandura's social learning theory?", "options": {"a": "Learning occurs through observation", "b": "Observations can take place at any time", "c": "Focuses on the give-and-take interaction between social, cognitive, and environmental influences.", "d": "Learning something necessarily results in a behaviour change"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d, because it is the incorrect statement. Albert Bandura's social learning theory holds that people learn by watching others (models) and the consequences of their actions, without having to perform the behaviour or be reinforced themselves. A central point is the distinction between learning (acquiring the knowledge or ability to perform a behaviour) and performance (actually doing it). In Bandura's 1965 Bobo doll study, children watched an adult model hit an inflatable Bobo doll and then saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Children who saw the model punished imitated less aggression. But when all the children were later offered rewards for showing what the model had done, they reproduced the aggressive acts about equally well. The behaviour had been learned by everyone; whether it was performed depended on expected consequences. Bandura also described reciprocal determinism, the two-way interaction of social, cognitive and environmental influences. Option d is right as the answer because Bandura specifically showed that learning can occur without any change in observable behaviour. The exam trap is to think like a behaviourist: behaviourists defined learning as a change in behaviour, while Bandura showed learning can stay hidden until there is a reason to perform it.", "topic": "Bandura: learning versus performance"}, {"q": "Which statement is false about the difference between temperament and personality?", "options": {"a": "Temperament is about neurochemically-based individual differences in behavioural regulation, noted in animals, infants and adult humans, whereas personality is a concept describing individual differences primarily from the socio-cultural perspective specific to humans.", "b": "Temperament traits have a well-documented biological basis and some of these traits are also incorporated in structural models of personality but with a socio-cultural perspective.", "c": "Temperament traits are conceived as individual differences in consistent formal-dynamical aspects of behavioural regulation that show up universally across situations and contexts, whereas personality traits are classically considered as comprising the content characteristics shaped by socio-cultural appraisal and interpretation.", "d": "Temperamental traits are relatively unstable over the life course, whereas personality aspects, on the other hand, such as our systems of values and attitudes, and various aspects of personal identity, often change after dramatic life events."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d, because it is the false statement. Temperament refers to biologically based, early-appearing individual differences in the energy, speed, intensity and emotional reactivity of behaviour. Gordon Allport treated temperament as part of the inherited \"raw material\" from which personality is built. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess's New York Longitudinal Study classified infants as easy, difficult or slow-to-warm-up, and the Polish psychologist Jan Strelau described temperament as the formal (energetic and temporal) side of behaviour, seen even in animals. Personality is the broader, distinctly human organisation of traits, motives, values, attitudes and identity, shaped by culture, learning and life experience. Because temperament is rooted in biology, it is relatively stable across the life span, while personality content such as values and attitudes is more open to change, for example after dramatic life events. Option d is right as the answer because it calls temperament \"relatively unstable\" over the life course, which reverses the standard finding; its second half about personality change is accurate. The exam trap is the long, technical wording of a, b and c, which looks suspicious but is correct; read each option for the single factual reversal, here \"unstable\" where it should say \"stable\".", "topic": "Temperament versus personality: stability"}, {"q": "Where was the first laboratory ever to be exclusively devoted to psychological studies?", "options": {"a": "Vienna, Austria", "b": "Zurich, Switzerland", "c": "Leipzig, Germany", "d": "Heidelberg, Germany"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, Leipzig, Germany. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt established a laboratory at the University of Leipzig devoted exclusively to psychological research, which is why 1879 is treated as the formal start of psychology as an independent experimental science. Wundt's laboratory measured reaction times, sensations, perception and attention under controlled conditions, using trained introspection, and it attracted students from across Europe and America who then set up laboratories of their own. Before Leipzig, Wundt had worked at Heidelberg (including as an assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz) and had spent about a year at Zurich. William James had a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard around 1875, but it was used for teaching rather than research, so Leipzig is credited as the first true psychological laboratory. Option c is right because Leipzig is where the first laboratory exclusively for psychological studies was founded. The exam trap is that the other cities are also linked to famous names: Heidelberg and Zurich were earlier stops in Wundt's own career, and Vienna is Freud's city, so fix the triple \"Wundt, Leipzig, 1879\" firmly in memory.", "topic": "Leipzig 1879: the first psychology laboratory"}, {"q": "Mr. R, 26 years old, complains of extremely fearful thoughts about something bad that will happen to him, without any apparent reason, and these thoughts keep coming back every now and then. He also complains that he is not able to stop these thoughts and spends hours together trying to control them. He stopped going out of the house because of these fearful thoughts. Mr. R most likely has:", "options": {"a": "A phobia", "b": "Obsessive disorder", "c": "Anxiety disorder", "d": "Panic reaction"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b, obsessive disorder. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, images or urges that are experienced as intrusive and unwanted and that usually cause marked anxiety or distress. The person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them, often with compulsions, and recognises them as coming from his or her own mind (unlike thought insertion in psychosis). DSM-5-TR places obsessive-compulsive disorder in its own chapter and requires that the symptoms be time-consuming (for example more than an hour a day) or cause significant distress or impairment; ICD-10 even had a subtype for predominantly obsessional thoughts or ruminations without compulsions. Mr R shows each feature: fearful thoughts that something bad will happen, coming back again and again without reason, which he cannot stop and spends hours trying to control, to the point that he avoids leaving home. Option b is right because the vignette stresses intrusion, recurrence, failed resistance and hours spent fighting the thoughts, the hallmark of obsessions. The exam trap is generalized anxiety: GAD worry is excessive concern about real-life matters (health, money, family) that feels like ordinary, if exaggerated, worrying, whereas an obsession is felt as an alien, unwanted intrusion that the person actively struggles against.", "topic": "Obsessions: intrusive, resisted, time-consuming thoughts"}, {"q": "Which statement is true about American Psychological Association (APA)?", "options": {"a": "Out of its 36 divisions, division 3 defines clinical psychology", "b": "Out of its 41 divisions, division 15 defines clinical psychology", "c": "Out of its 56 divisions, division 12 defines clinical psychology", "d": "Out of its 27 divisions, division 4 defines clinical psychology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c. The American Psychological Association (APA), founded in 1892 with G. Stanley Hall as its first president, is the largest professional body of psychologists in the United States. It is organised into divisions, each representing a subfield or interest area. The division numbers run from 1 to 56, but two numbers (4 and 11) are vacant, so there are 54 active divisions; exam questions and many textbooks simply say \"56 divisions\". Division 12 is the Society of Clinical Psychology, the home of clinical psychologists, which promotes clinical research, training and practice and is known for its lists of empirically supported treatments. Clinical psychology itself began when Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term \"clinical psychology\". Other well-known divisions include Division 15 (Educational Psychology), Division 17 (Counseling Psychology) and Division 39 (Psychoanalysis). Option c is right because it is the only option that pairs the usual division count (56) with the correct clinical division (12). The exam trap is the 56 versus 54 detail: an option saying 54 divisions with Division 12 as clinical would also be correct; what never changes is that Division 12 is clinical psychology.", "topic": "APA divisions: Division 12, Clinical Psychology"}, {"q": "What does the following graph show?<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Graph: frequency of inappropriate behaviour over time, with baseline, treatment, extinction burst, rebound effects and stability\" style=\"display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:12px auto 4px;background:#fff;border-radius:6px;padding:6px\">", "options": {"a": "This learning progress curve shows the diminishing effects of intervention.", "b": "The S-Curve or Sigmoid function showing the improvement of proficiency starts slowly, then increases rapidly, and finally levels off.", "c": "Extinction and spontaneous recovery curve of bad behaviour.", "d": "Steep learning and regression curve in response to treatment effects."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c, the extinction and spontaneous recovery curve of bad behaviour. In operant terms, extinction means stopping the reinforcement that has been maintaining a behaviour, so the behaviour gradually weakens. The graph shows its typical course for an inappropriate behaviour. During baseline the behaviour is frequent. When treatment begins (withholding reinforcement, for example no longer giving attention for a child's tantrums), there is first an extinction burst: a short-lived increase in the frequency or intensity of the behaviour, as if the child is testing whether it still works. Then the behaviour falls steeply. Later it may reappear for a while, the \"rebound effects\" on the graph, which corresponds to spontaneous recovery, a phenomenon first described by Ivan Pavlov. If the caregiver stays consistent, the behaviour settles at a low, stable level (\"treatment takes\"). If the caregiver gives in during the burst or the rebound, the behaviour is intermittently reinforced, becomes more resistant to extinction and can end up worse than at baseline, which is the dotted line. Option c is right because the burst, fall and rebound labelled on the graph are the signature of extinction and spontaneous recovery. The exam trap is option d: \"regression\" sounds right, but the graph is not a learning curve; it plots a problem behaviour being extinguished.", "topic": "Extinction burst and spontaneous recovery curve"}, {"q": "Verbal declarative memory deficits is found more in:", "options": {"a": "Patients of 1st episode schizophrenia", "b": "Patients with PTSD with comorbid depression", "c": "Generalized anxiety patients with a history of Holocaust", "d": "Patients of alcohol induced psychosis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is b, PTSD with comorbid depression. Verbal memory is also one of the largest cognitive deficits in first-episode schizophrenia, so a is defensible if \"more\" means size of deficit; the item comes from PTSD memory research, so answer b in the exam. Declarative (explicit) memory is memory for facts and events that can be consciously recalled; verbal declarative memory is tested by word-list or story recall, for example the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test. Research on posttraumatic stress disorder repeatedly finds poorer learning and recall of emotionally neutral verbal material; a meta-analysis by Chris Brewin and colleagues (2007) found the deficit is larger for verbal than for visual memory. Some studies found the deficit is greater when PTSD is accompanied by major depression, which itself impairs memory. A common explanation involves the hippocampus, which is vital for declarative memory and sensitive to stress hormones; several PTSD studies report smaller hippocampal volume. Option b is right under the key because PTSD with comorbid depression is the group in which this specific deficit has been highlighted. The exam trap is option c: studies of Holocaust survivors by Rachel Yehuda's group linked verbal memory deficits to PTSD, not to generalized anxiety, so a trauma history alone does not make an option correct.", "topic": "Verbal declarative memory deficits in PTSD"}, {"q": "The ability to use one's knowledge of ocean tides to navigate unfamiliar seas is describing the concept of:", "options": {"a": "Crystallized intelligence", "b": "Fluid intelligence", "c": "Episodic memory", "d": "Procedural memory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a, crystallized intelligence. Raymond Cattell, later working with John Horn, divided general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on prior learning, such as completing abstract patterns in Raven's Progressive Matrices; it peaks in early adulthood and then declines. Crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills acquired through education and experience, together with the ability to apply it; it keeps growing or stays stable into late adulthood. Both are now part of the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model. In the stem, knowledge of ocean tides is learned, stored information, and navigating with it is applying that knowledge, which is the essence of crystallized intelligence; an experienced old sailor would be expected to do this better than a bright beginner. Option a is right because the key phrase is \"use one's knowledge\", the hallmark of Gc. The exam trap is the phrase \"unfamiliar seas\", which suggests novelty and pulls students toward fluid intelligence; ask whether the person is reasoning from scratch (fluid) or applying what they already know (crystallized), and here the stored knowledge does the work.", "topic": "Crystallized versus fluid intelligence"}, {"q": "When we look out of the window of a train, nearby things pass very quickly, while far off objects appear stationary. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Phi phenomenon", "b": "Motion parallax", "c": "Kinetic illusion", "d": "Depth perception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, motion parallax. Depth perception, the ability to see the world in three dimensions and judge distance, relies on binocular cues (needing two eyes, such as retinal disparity and convergence) and monocular cues (available to one eye). Motion parallax is a monocular, movement-produced cue: when you move, objects at different distances move across your visual field at different speeds and in different directions. Objects near you seem to rush past quickly in the direction opposite to your movement, while distant objects seem to move slowly, to stand almost still, or even to travel along with you. The brain uses these differences in relative speed to judge which objects are near and which are far. The train window is the textbook example: nearby poles and trees flash by, while distant hills and the moon seem to stay still. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this difference in apparent motion between near and far objects seen by a moving observer. The exam trap is option d: depth perception is the general ability, while motion parallax is one specific cue for it; when a question describes a particular mechanism, choose the specific cue, not the umbrella term.", "topic": "Motion parallax as a monocular depth cue"}, {"q": "Which one of the following problem-solving strategies, though time consuming, guarantees solution?", "options": {"a": "Trial and error", "b": "Use of algorithm", "c": "Use of heuristics", "d": "Study of the problem in parts"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, use of an algorithm. An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that, if followed correctly, is guaranteed to produce the solution, because it systematically checks every possibility or applies a proven rule. Examples are a mathematical formula, or trying every combination of a three-digit lock (000 to 999) until it opens. The drawback is that algorithms can be very slow, sometimes impractical, when the number of possibilities is large. Heuristics, studied in problem solving by Allen Newell and Herbert Simon and in judgement by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, are rules of thumb or mental shortcuts (such as working backward, means-end analysis or breaking a problem into subgoals) that are fast and often successful but do not guarantee a solution. In daily life, searching for lost keys by checking every room in order is algorithmic; checking only the places you usually leave them is heuristic. Option b is right because the stem pairs \"time consuming\" with \"guarantees solution\", the defining trade-off of an algorithm. The exam trap is trial and error: it also feels slow and often succeeds in the end, but because it is unsystematic it may repeat attempts or miss options, so it does not guarantee a solution.", "topic": "Algorithms versus heuristics in problem solving"}, {"q": "Who is regarded as the \"Father of Psychology\"?", "options": {"a": "Carl Rogers", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Wilhelm Wundt", "d": "Ivan Pavlov"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, Wilhelm Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) was a German physician, physiologist and philosopher who made psychology a separate, experimental science. In 1879 he opened the first laboratory for psychological research at the University of Leipzig; he also wrote Principles of Physiological Psychology, founded the first psychology journal and trained a generation of psychologists. He studied the elements of conscious experience (sensations, feelings) through controlled introspection and measured reaction times, and his student Edward Titchener carried these ideas to America as structuralism. For these reasons he is called the father of psychology, or more precisely the father of experimental or modern psychology. Other \"fathers\" appear in exam lore: Aristotle is sometimes called the father of psychology for his early writing on the mind, William James the father of American psychology, and Sigmund Freud the father of psychoanalysis. Option c is right because, when a question simply says \"Father of Psychology\", the standard answer is Wundt, the founder of psychology as a science. The exam trap is option b: Freud is the psychologist best known to the public, but he is the father of psychoanalysis, one school within psychology, not of psychology itself.", "topic": "Wilhelm Wundt, the father of psychology"}, {"q": "Where was the first psychology laboratory established?", "options": {"a": "Leipzig", "b": "Romania", "c": "London", "d": "California"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a, Leipzig. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt set up the first formal laboratory for experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in Germany. It is treated as the birthplace of scientific psychology because, for the first time, mental processes such as sensation, perception, attention, feeling and reaction time were studied with controlled experiments in a setting devoted to that purpose. The laboratory trained many of the first professional psychologists, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin, who carried its methods to America and into psychiatry. Other early landmarks are often confused with it: Francis Galton opened an anthropometric laboratory in London in 1884 to measure people's physical and sensory abilities, an early step in mental testing; G. Stanley Hall founded the first American psychology laboratory at Johns Hopkins University in 1883; and Lewis Terman at Stanford University in California published the Stanford-Binet test in 1916. Option a is right because Leipzig is the city where Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory. The exam trap is option c: London did have an early laboratory, Galton's, but it came five years later and measured individual differences rather than founding experimental psychology.", "topic": "Leipzig: site of the first psychology laboratory"}, {"q": "Which school of thought in psychology emphasized the analysis of the basic elements of consciousness?", "options": {"a": "Functionalism", "b": "Psychodynamic", "c": "Structuralism", "d": "Humanism"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, structuralism. Structuralism was the first school of psychology. It aimed to analyse conscious experience into its basic elements, much as chemistry breaks compounds into elements, and to find the laws by which those elements combine. The ideas grew out of Wilhelm Wundt's laboratory work, but the school was named and systematised by his student Edward Bradford Titchener at Cornell University in the United States. Titchener proposed three basic elements of consciousness: sensations (elements of perception), images (elements of ideas) and affections (elements of feelings). The method was introspection by highly trained observers, who reported the raw sensory qualities of an experience rather than the meaning of the object; describing an apple as \"red, round, shiny\" rather than \"an apple\" avoided what Titchener called the stimulus error. Structuralism faded because introspection was unreliable and could not be checked by others, and because it could not study children, animals or abnormal minds. Option c is right because \"analysis of the basic elements of consciousness\" is the defining aim of structuralism. The exam trap is functionalism: both schools studied consciousness, but functionalism asked what consciousness is for (its adaptive purpose), while structuralism asked what it is made of.", "topic": "Structuralism: elements of consciousness"}, {"q": "Which of the following does not make psychology a science?", "options": {"a": "Objectivity", "b": "Empirical observation", "c": "Falsifiability", "d": "Absolutism"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d, absolutism. Psychology is called a science because it studies behaviour and mental processes with the scientific method, and that method has recognisable features. Objectivity means observations and conclusions should not depend on the personal beliefs or feelings of the observer, so independent observers should agree. Empirical observation means claims are tested against evidence gathered through observation and measurement, not accepted on authority or reasoning alone. Falsifiability, the criterion proposed by the philosopher Karl Popper, means a scientific hypothesis must be stated so that some possible result could prove it wrong; \"everything people do is controlled by fate\" cannot be tested and so is not scientific. Other features are replicability, systematic procedures and tentativeness: scientific conclusions are provisional and open to revision when new evidence arrives. Absolutism is the opposite of this last feature. It treats a claim as final, certain and beyond question regardless of evidence, which is how dogma works, not science. Option d is right because absolutism contradicts the open, self-correcting nature of science, while the other three are core scientific features. The exam trap is to confuse absolutism with objectivity: objectivity is about unbiased observation, whereas absolutism is about refusing to accept that conclusions might change.", "topic": "Features of science: what makes psychology scientific"}, {"q": "Who are the proponents of Humanism?", "options": {"a": "Abraham Maslow, Donald Meichenbaum", "b": "Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow", "c": "Erik Erikson, Wilhelm Wundt", "d": "Erik Erikson, Carl Rogers"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow. Humanistic psychology arose in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as the \"third force\", a reaction against both psychoanalysis (seen as too focused on unconscious, pathological drives) and behaviourism (seen as treating people like machines shaped by reinforcement). It stresses free will, personal responsibility, subjective experience and each person's natural tendency toward growth. Abraham Maslow proposed the hierarchy of needs (1943), rising from physiological and safety needs through love and belonging and esteem to self-actualisation, the full realisation of one's potential, and he studied self-actualising people. Carl Rogers developed client-centred (person-centred) therapy, built on the self-concept, the actualising tendency and the therapist conditions of empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence; he explained how \"conditions of worth\" create incongruence between self and experience. A Rogerian therapist, for example, does not interpret or direct but trusts the client to find his or her own solutions. Option b is right because Rogers and Maslow are the two founding figures of humanistic psychology. The exam trap is the half-right pairs: a includes Maslow and d includes Rogers, but each is paired with a non-humanist, so only b has both names correct.", "topic": "Humanistic psychology: Rogers and Maslow"}, {"q": "Pick the correct order of psychosexual stages of development.", "options": {"a": "Oral, Anal, Latency, Phallic, Genital", "b": "Anal, Oral, Phallic, Genital, Latency", "c": "Latency, Oral, Genital, Anal, Phallic", "d": "Oral, Anal, Phallic, Latency, Genital"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905) and later works, proposed that personality develops through stages in which the libido (pleasure-seeking energy) is focused on different body zones. Oral stage (birth to about 1 to 1.5 years): pleasure comes from the mouth through sucking and biting. Anal stage (about 1.5 to 3 years): pleasure centres on holding and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the key conflict. Phallic stage (about 3 to 5 or 6 years): focus on the genitals, with the Oedipus complex in boys (and what Carl Jung named the Electra complex in girls), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. Latency (about 6 years to puberty): sexual impulses are repressed and energy goes into school, skills and same-sex friendships; Freud saw it as a quiet period rather than a true stage. Genital stage (puberty onward): mature sexual interest in others. A handy mnemonic is \"Old Age Pensioners Love Grapes\". Option d is right because it is the only option with the full sequence in order. The exam trap is option a, which swaps phallic and latency; remember that latency is the quiet period that follows the Oedipal conflict.", "topic": "Freud's psychosexual stages in order"}, {"q": "Classical experiment by John B. Watson demonstrating classical conditioning was on:", "options": {"a": "Little Hans", "b": "Stuart Little", "c": "Rat Man", "d": "Little Albert"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d, Little Albert. In 1920 John B. Watson, the founder of behaviourism, and his assistant Rosalie Rayner published \"Conditioned Emotional Reactions\", showing that fear could be learned through classical conditioning. Albert was an infant of about nine months at first testing, who showed no fear of a white rat. During conditioning, at about 11 months, each time Albert reached for the rat the experimenters struck a steel bar with a hammer behind his head, making a loud noise. The noise was the unconditioned stimulus, startle and crying the unconditioned response, the rat the conditioned stimulus and fear of the rat the conditioned response. After several pairings Albert cried at the sight of the rat alone, and his fear generalised to similar furry objects such as a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat and a Santa Claus mask (stimulus generalisation). Albert's fear was never removed, and the study is now cited as an ethical breach; in 1924 Mary Cover Jones showed how such fears could be unlearned, with Little Peter. Option d is right because Little Albert is Watson's classic classical conditioning experiment. The exam trap is Little Hans: also a child with a phobia, but it is Sigmund Freud's 1909 psychoanalytic case, explained through the Oedipus complex rather than conditioning.", "topic": "Watson and Rayner's Little Albert experiment"}, {"q": "Which type of memory is responsible for the conscious recall of factual information, such as historical dates or capital cities?", "options": {"a": "Procedural memory", "b": "Semantic memory", "c": "Episodic memory", "d": "Sensory memory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, semantic memory. Endel Tulving, a Canadian psychologist, proposed in 1972 that long-term declarative (explicit) memory has two parts. Semantic memory holds general facts, meanings and concepts that are no longer tied to when or where you learned them: knowing that New Delhi is the capital of India, that the Battle of Plassey was fought in 1757, or that a triangle has three sides. Episodic memory holds personal events stamped with a time and place, such as your own tenth birthday or last night's dinner. Both are declarative because you can consciously bring them to mind and put them into words. In Larry Squire's taxonomy they sit under declarative memory, while non-declarative (implicit) memory covers procedural skills, priming and classically conditioned responses. Option b is right because the stem asks for conscious recall of factual knowledge such as dates and capitals, which is general world knowledge. The exam trap is episodic memory: both are explicit, so ask whether the memory is a fact anyone could know (semantic) or an event you personally lived through (episodic). \"I know Paris is in France\" is semantic; \"I remember my trip to Paris in 2019\" is episodic.", "topic": "Semantic versus episodic memory"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes Edward Thorndike's Law of Effect?", "options": {"a": "Behaviors that are followed by favorable consequences are more likely to be repeated.", "b": "Learning occurs through the association of a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus.", "c": "Reinforcement is most effective when presented immediately after the desired behavior.", "d": "Learning is influenced by the observation of others' behaviors and their consequences."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a. Edward L. Thorndike, an American psychologist, studied hungry cats in puzzle boxes in the late 1890s (Animal Intelligence, 1898). A cat had to pull a loop or press a lever to escape and reach food. On early trials it struggled randomly; across trials its escape time fell gradually, which he called trial-and-error learning. He explained this with the Law of Effect: responses followed by a satisfying state of affairs are \"stamped in\" and become more likely in that situation, while responses followed by an annoying state of affairs are \"stamped out\". This was the forerunner of B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning and the modern idea of reinforcement. Thorndike also proposed the Law of Exercise (use and disuse) and the Law of Readiness. Option a is right because it states the core claim: favourable consequences make a behaviour more likely to be repeated. The exam trap is option c, which sounds operant and is true, but it is the principle of immediacy (temporal contiguity) of reinforcement, a later refinement, not the Law of Effect itself. Remember: the Law of Effect is about what the consequence is (satisfying or annoying), not when it arrives.", "topic": "Thorndike's Law of Effect"}, {"q": "According to classical conditioning, which term describes a previously neutral stimulus that, after being paired with an unconditioned stimulus, triggers a conditioned response?", "options": {"a": "Unconditioned stimulus", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Unconditioned response", "d": "Conditioned response"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, the conditioned stimulus. Ivan Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began to salivate before food reached their mouths. In his classic procedure, food (the unconditioned stimulus, UCS) naturally produces salivation (the unconditioned response, UCR) without any learning. A tone, such as a metronome or buzzer, is at first a neutral stimulus that produces no salivation. After repeated pairings in which the tone comes just before the food, the tone alone produces salivation. The tone is now the conditioned stimulus (CS), and the salivation it triggers is the conditioned response (CR). In everyday life, the whistle of a pressure cooker can make you hungry because it has so often preceded meals. Option b is right because the stem describes a stimulus that was once neutral and acquired its power through pairing, which is the definition of a CS. The exam trap is mixing up stimulus with response, and conditioned with unconditioned: a stimulus is what comes in, a response is what the organism does, unconditioned means unlearned and conditioned means learned. The same salivation is a UCR to food but a CR to the tone.", "topic": "Conditioned stimulus in classical conditioning"}, {"q": "Which term refers to the process of decreasing the likelihood of a behavior by removing a desirable stimulus after the behavior occurs?", "options": {"a": "Positive reinforcement", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Positive punishment", "d": "Negative punishment"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d, negative punishment. In B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, consequences are sorted by two questions. First, does the consequence make the behaviour more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. That gives four cells: positive reinforcement (add something pleasant, such as praise for homework, so homework increases); negative reinforcement (remove something unpleasant, such as the seatbelt alarm stopping when you buckle up, so buckling increases); positive punishment (add something unpleasant, such as a scolding, so the behaviour decreases); and negative punishment (remove something pleasant, such as taking away a teenager's phone for coming home late, so coming home late decreases). Time-out and response cost (fines, loss of tokens) are the classic clinical forms of negative punishment. Option d is right because the stem has both clues: the behaviour decreases (punishment) and a desirable stimulus is taken away (negative). The exam trap is negative reinforcement, which students confuse with punishment because of the word \"negative\". Negative reinforcement always increases behaviour by removing something aversive; negative punishment decreases behaviour by removing something desirable.", "topic": "Negative punishment vs negative reinforcement"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the concept of working memory?", "options": {"a": "The storage of long-term memories for future retrieval", "b": "The process of encoding sensory information into the memory system", "c": "The temporary storage and manipulation of information needed for cognitive tasks", "d": "The unconscious processing of emotional stimuli without conscious awareness"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c. Working memory is the small, active workspace in which information is held for a short time and worked on during a task, such as keeping a phone number in mind while dialling, multiplying 47 by 6 in your head, or following a long sentence. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposed the model in 1974 to replace the single, passive short-term store of the Atkinson-Shiffrin model (1968). Their model has a central executive that directs attention and coordinates two slave systems: the phonological loop (an inner voice and inner ear for speech-based material) and the visuospatial sketchpad (for images and spatial layout). In 2000 Baddeley added the episodic buffer, which links working memory with long-term memory. Capacity is limited: George Miller's classic estimate for short-term span was seven plus or minus two items, and Nelson Cowan later argued for about four chunks. Option c is right because it names both defining features: temporary storage and active manipulation in the service of a cognitive task. The exam trap is plain short-term memory, which stresses passive holding for a brief period; working memory stresses manipulation, so \"storage plus processing\" is its signature.", "topic": "Working memory: Baddeley and Hitch"}, {"q": "Which term best describes the psychological drive that energizes and directs behavior toward a specific goal?", "options": {"a": "Arousal", "b": "Need", "c": "Motivation", "d": "Homeostasis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c, motivation. Motivation is the general term for the processes that start (energise), direct and sustain behaviour toward a goal; the word comes from the Latin movere, \"to move\". Textbooks such as Morgan and King describe a motivational cycle: a need (a deficit, such as low blood sugar) gives rise to a drive (an aroused state of tension, such as hunger), which pushes behaviour toward an incentive or goal (food), and reaching the goal reduces the drive. Clark Hull's drive-reduction theory (1943) is built on this cycle. Motivation is the umbrella term covering needs, drives, incentives and arousal, and it shows itself in activation (getting started), persistence (keeping going) and intensity (how hard you try). A student who studies every evening for months to clear an M.Phil entrance shows all three. Option c is right because the stem asks for the force that both energises and directs behaviour toward a specific goal, and only motivation covers both energy and direction. The exam trap is option b, need: a need is the deficit that starts the cycle, not the force that steers behaviour, while arousal (option a) supplies energy without direction.", "topic": "Motivation versus need, drive and arousal"}, {"q": "According to the James-Lange theory of emotion, what is the sequence of events in the experience of emotion?", "options": {"a": "Physiological arousal → Emotional experience", "b": "Emotional experience → Physiological arousal", "c": "Cognitive appraisal → Emotional experience", "d": "Environmental stimulus → Emotional experience"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a. The James-Lange theory was proposed independently by the American psychologist William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885). Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid, and then tremble and run. James reversed this: we see the bear, the body reacts (pounding heart, trembling, running), and our perception of those bodily changes is the emotion. In his famous phrase, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble. So the sequence is stimulus, then physiological arousal, then the felt emotion, and each emotion should have its own bodily pattern. Walter Cannon and Philip Bard attacked the theory in the 1920s, arguing that visceral changes are too slow and too similar across emotions; they proposed that the thalamus sends signals to the cortex (feeling) and the body (arousal) at the same time. Option a is right because it is the only choice that places physiological arousal before emotional experience, the defining claim of James-Lange. The exam trap is the rival theories: Cannon-Bard says arousal and feeling are simultaneous, Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory (1962) says arousal plus a cognitive label, and Richard Lazarus's appraisal theory puts cognitive appraisal first.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion"}, {"q": "School of Individual Psychology was founded by:", "options": {"a": "Erik Erikson", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Anna Freud", "d": "Carl Jung"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b, Alfred Adler. Adler (1870-1937), an Austrian physician, was an early member of Sigmund Freud's Vienna circle but broke with him in 1911, rejecting Freud's emphasis on sexuality, and named his own approach Individual Psychology. \"Individual\" here means indivisible: Adler saw the person as a unified whole moving toward goals, not a battleground of id, ego and superego. His key ideas are the feelings of inferiority every child has, compensation for them, striving for superiority (later, striving for perfection or completion), the style of life, fictional finalism (guiding goals that may be imagined), social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl) as the mark of mental health, and birth order effects, such as the \"dethroned\" first-born. The popular term \"inferiority complex\" comes from his school. Option b is right because Individual Psychology is the specific name of Adler's school. The exam trap is Carl Jung, the other famous dissenter from Freud, whose school is called Analytical Psychology. Memory hook: Adler, Individual; Jung, Analytical; Freud, Psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Adler's Individual Psychology"}, {"q": "As per Erikson's psychosocial stages of development, at what age do children take the initiative on some activities, and may develop guilt when unsuccessful or when boundaries are overstepped?", "options": {"a": "7-11 years", "b": "5-12 years", "c": "1-2 years", "d": "3-6 years"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d, 3-6 years. Erik Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who worked in the United States, described eight psychosocial stages in Childhood and Society (1950). Each stage is a crisis between two poles, and resolving it well yields a basic virtue. The third stage, initiative versus guilt, covers the preschool or play years, roughly 3 to 6 (Freud's phallic stage). Children now plan and start activities on their own: inventing games, asking endless questions, trying to help cook. If adults support this, the child develops initiative and the virtue of purpose; if the child is constantly criticised, or oversteps limits and is shamed, guilt develops. The sequence to memorise is: trust versus mistrust (0-1), autonomy versus shame and doubt (1-3), initiative versus guilt (3-6), industry versus inferiority (6-12), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood) and integrity versus despair (old age). Option d is right because 3-6 years is the age band of initiative versus guilt. The exam trap is the neighbouring stages: autonomy versus shame (1-3) is about doing things by oneself, while industry versus inferiority (school age) is about competence compared with peers.", "topic": "Erikson: initiative versus guilt"}, {"q": "The definition of health provided by the World Health Organization (WHO) includes which of the following aspects of health?", "options": {"a": "Biological and environmental", "b": "Biological, spiritual, and social", "c": "Biological, social and psychological", "d": "Biological, psychological, social and environmental"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c. The Constitution of the World Health Organization, adopted in New York in 1946 and in force from 7 April 1948 (now marked as World Health Day), defines health as \"a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity\". In the option's wording, physical is biological, mental is psychological, and social stays social. The definition mattered because it moved health beyond the narrow medical model (no disease) toward positive well-being. It fits George Engel's biopsychosocial model (1977), which says illness must be understood through biological, psychological and social factors together, the foundation of modern health and clinical psychology. In 1998 the WHO Executive Board proposed adding \"spiritual\" well-being (and the word \"dynamic\"), but the World Health Assembly never adopted it, so the official definition still has three dimensions. Option c is right because biological, social and psychological map exactly onto physical, social and mental. The exam trap is option b, which adds spiritual: students remember the 1998 proposal and pick it, but it never became part of the definition. Likewise, environment is a determinant of health, not a dimension of the WHO definition.", "topic": "WHO definition of health"}, {"q": "In Herzberg's motivation theory, the factors that can cause higher level of dissatisfaction in a job are classified as:", "options": {"a": "Motivators", "b": "Hygiene factors", "c": "Performing factors", "d": "Expectancy factors"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b, hygiene factors. Frederick Herzberg, an American psychologist, interviewed about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh and published The Motivation to Work (1959). He asked about times they felt very good or very bad about their jobs and found that the causes of unhappiness differed from the causes of happiness. This led to his two-factor (motivation-hygiene) theory. Hygiene factors belong to the job context: salary, company policy, supervision, working conditions, job security and relations with co-workers. When poor, they cause dissatisfaction; when good, they only remove dissatisfaction and bring a person to neutral, not to motivation. Motivators belong to the job content: achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth, and these create satisfaction and motivation. So satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one scale. A fair salary and a clean office stop complaints; challenging work and recognition make people want to work harder. Option b is right because the stem asks which factors cause dissatisfaction, and in Herzberg's theory that is the role of hygiene factors. The exam trap is option a, motivators: they produce satisfaction, and their absence leaves a worker unmotivated rather than actively dissatisfied.", "topic": "Herzberg's hygiene factors and motivators"}, {"q": "Who suggested a three-factor theory of personality?", "options": {"a": "Gordon Allport", "b": "Raymond Cattell", "c": "Hans Eysenck", "d": "McCrae & Costa"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c, Hans Eysenck. Hans J. Eysenck (1916-1997), a German-born British psychologist at the Institute of Psychiatry in London, used factor analysis to reduce many traits to a few broad, biologically based superfactors, which he called types. His first two were extraversion-introversion (E) and neuroticism-emotional stability (N); crossed together, they reproduce the four classical temperaments of Hippocrates and Galen. In the 1970s he added a third, psychoticism (P), describing a cold, aggressive, impulsive and tough-minded style. This is the three-factor PEN model, measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975, with Sybil Eysenck). He linked extraversion to the arousal level of the ascending reticular activating system (introverts are chronically more aroused, so they avoid extra stimulation) and neuroticism to the reactivity of the limbic system or \"visceral brain\". Option c is right because the three-factor theory of personality, PEN, is Eysenck's. The exam trap is counting factors: Raymond Cattell has 16 source traits (the 16PF), Robert McCrae and Paul Costa have five (the Big Five), and Gordon Allport has no fixed number but cardinal, central and secondary traits. Three means Eysenck.", "topic": "Eysenck's three-factor PEN model"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big 5 Personality Traits?", "options": {"a": "Creativity", "b": "Agreeableness", "c": "Conscientiousness", "d": "Neuroticism"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a, creativity. The Big Five, or five-factor model, describes personality with five broad dimensions that emerged repeatedly from factor analyses of trait words (the lexical hypothesis) and questionnaires. Early work came from Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) and Warren Norman (1963); Lewis Goldberg popularised the name \"Big Five\", and Robert McCrae and Paul Costa built the NEO Personality Inventory to measure them. The five, remembered as OCEAN, are Openness to experience (imaginative, curious, broad interests), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable, disciplined), Extraversion (sociable, assertive, energetic), Agreeableness (warm, trusting, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious, moody, emotionally unstable). Each is a continuum; a person low on conscientiousness, for instance, is careless and disorganised. Creativity is related to openness, and some older lists labelled that factor \"intellect\" or \"culture\", but creativity itself is not the name of any of the five. Option a is right because agreeableness, conscientiousness and neuroticism are all standard Big Five names, while creativity is not. The exam trap is that overlap: an option naming creativity, intelligence or imagination is trying to pull you toward openness, yet only Openness to experience is the official label.", "topic": "The Big Five (OCEAN) traits"}, {"q": "Who has referred to adolescence as a period of Identity Crisis?", "options": {"a": "Erikson", "b": "Jean Piaget", "c": "Freud", "d": "Anna Freud"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a, Erikson. Erik Erikson coined the term \"identity crisis\" in the 1940s while working with World War II veterans who had lost their sense of who they were, and he made it the centre of his fifth psychosocial stage, identity versus role confusion, which covers adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 or 20). The adolescent's task is to integrate childhood identifications, new sexual maturity, abilities and social roles into a coherent answer to \"Who am I, and where am I going?\" Erikson saw adolescence as a psychosocial moratorium, a socially permitted delay in which young people try out roles, beliefs and careers before committing. Success brings the virtue of fidelity; failure leads to role confusion or a negative identity. He developed the idea in Childhood and Society (1950) and Identity: Youth and Crisis (1968). James Marcia later turned it into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement) defined by crisis (exploration) and commitment. Option a is right because \"identity crisis\" is Erikson's own term for the adolescent stage. The exam trap is G. Stanley Hall, who called adolescence a period of \"storm and stress\" (1904): Hall is storm and stress, Erikson is identity crisis, Piaget is formal operations.", "topic": "Erikson's adolescent identity crisis"}, {"q": "According to Freud there is/are only:", "options": {"a": "One instinct", "b": "Two instincts", "c": "Three instincts", "d": "Four instincts"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b, two instincts. Sigmund Freud's instinct (drive) theory changed over time, but his final version, set out in Beyond the Pleasure Principle (1920), grouped all instincts into two great classes. The life instincts, together called Eros, serve the survival of the individual and the species: hunger, thirst and above all sex; their energy is the libido. The death instinct, later labelled Thanatos by his followers, is an unconscious wish to return to an inorganic, tension-free state; turned outward it shows as aggression and destructiveness, turned inward as self-punishment. Freud was led to it partly by the repetition compulsion he saw in traumatised World War I soldiers who kept reliving painful experiences. He also said every instinct has four features: a source (a bodily need), an aim (removing the excitation), an object (what satisfies it) and an impetus (its strength). Option b is right because Freud's mature theory names only two instincts, life and death. The exam trap is the earlier version: his first theory also had two classes (sexual versus ego or self-preservative instincts), so the count is the same; and do not confuse the four characteristics of an instinct with four instincts.", "topic": "Freud's two instincts: Eros and Thanatos"}, {"q": "According to Hurlock's theory, human development has how many stages?", "options": {"a": "Six", "b": "Eleven", "c": "Eight", "d": "Ten"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c (Eight), but Elizabeth Hurlock's Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach divides life into ten periods, and many Indian guides count eleven by splitting adolescence into early and late; eight is Erik Erikson's number of stages, not Hurlock's. Accept c for this paper, but in another exam choose eleven, or ten if eleven is not offered. Elizabeth B. Hurlock was an American developmental psychologist whose textbooks were long standard in Indian universities. Her ten periods are: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to about two weeks), babyhood (two weeks to two years), early childhood (2 to 6), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12), puberty (10 or 12 to 13 or 14), adolescence (13 or 14 to 18), early adulthood (18 to 40), middle age (40 to 60) and old age (60 onward). Dividing adolescence into early (13 to 16 or 17) and late (16 or 17 to 18) gives eleven. Option c is the official key, apparently because the setter borrowed Erikson's count of eight. The exam trap is exactly this mix-up of stage counts: Erikson has eight psychosocial stages, Freud five psychosexual stages, Piaget four cognitive stages, and Hurlock ten (or eleven) life-span periods.", "topic": "Hurlock's life-span developmental periods"}, {"q": "During stress the part of the brain which gets activated is:", "options": {"a": "Central Nervous System", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Amygdala", "d": "Autonomic nervous system"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is d (autonomic nervous system), but the ANS is not part of the brain; it is a division of the peripheral nervous system. Standard texts name the hypothalamus (b) as the brain structure that switches on the stress response, so if a stem asks for a part of the brain, b is the safer textbook answer; on this paper, mark d. Here is the full pathway. When a threat is perceived, the amygdala, in the medial temporal lobe, flags it as dangerous and signals the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus then drives two systems. The fast one is the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system: the sympathetic branch of the ANS raises heart rate, blood pressure and breathing, and stimulates the adrenal medulla to release adrenaline and noradrenaline, which Walter Cannon called the fight-or-flight response. The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which then feeds back to shut the axis down. Option d is keyed because the setter treated the ANS, whose sympathetic branch produces the visible stress reaction, as the system activated in stress. The exam trap is mixing a brain structure (hypothalamus, amygdala) with a nervous system division (ANS, CNS).", "topic": "Brain and body in the stress response"}, {"q": "A pattern of relatively permanent traits or dispositions or characteristics that give some consistency to a person's behaviour is called:", "options": {"a": "A general trait", "b": "Personality", "c": "Temperament", "d": "A genetic predisposition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b, personality. Gordon Allport (1937) gave the most quoted definition: personality is \"the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment\" (in 1961 he changed the ending to \"characteristic behavior and thought\"). Most textbooks paraphrase it as a relatively stable, enduring pattern of traits, dispositions and characteristics that gives consistency and uniqueness to a person's behaviour across situations and over time. The word comes from the Latin persona, the mask worn by actors in Roman theatre. Two features define it: consistency (you behave in recognisably similar ways in different settings) and distinctiveness (it marks you off from other people). A friend who is cheerful, punctual and talkative at college, at home and at weddings shows a consistent personality. Personality is the whole pattern; traits, temperament and genes are its parts or sources. Option b is right because the stem describes the overall pattern of relatively permanent traits that gives consistency to behaviour, which is the definition of personality itself. The exam trap is temperament: it is the biologically based, early-appearing core of personality (activity level, emotional reactivity, as studied by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess), so it is narrower than the full pattern.", "topic": "Definition of personality"}, {"q": "Under stressful condition which of the following hormone is produced?", "options": {"a": "Epinephrine", "b": "Dopamine", "c": "Adrenaline", "d": "Serotonin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "⚠ Key debate: Epinephrine (a) and adrenaline (c) are two names for the same hormone (epinephrine is the name used in American texts, adrenaline the British name), so a and c are equally correct in content; this paper keyed c. If both appear in an exam, nothing in the science separates them, so go with adrenaline as this setter did. Adrenaline is made by the adrenal medulla, the inner part of the adrenal glands that sit on top of the kidneys. Under stress the hypothalamus activates the sympathetic nervous system, whose nerves stimulate the adrenal medulla to release adrenaline and noradrenaline (norepinephrine) into the blood. These raise heart rate, blood pressure, blood sugar and breathing, and send blood to the muscles, preparing the body for what Walter Cannon called fight or flight. The slower, longer-lasting stress hormone is cortisol, released by the adrenal cortex through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis; Hans Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion) describes what happens when stress persists. Option c is right because adrenaline is the classic hormone of the immediate emergency response. The exam trap is the neurotransmitter options: dopamine and serotonin act mainly as brain neurotransmitters for reward and mood, not as the hormones the body releases in stress.", "topic": "Adrenaline as the stress hormone"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a feature of agoraphobia?", "options": {"a": "Fear of using public transportation", "b": "Fear of being outside home", "c": "Fear of public speaking", "d": "Fear of being in a crowd"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c, fear of public speaking. In DSM-5-TR, agoraphobia is marked fear or anxiety about at least two of five situations: using public transportation, being in open spaces (markets, parking lots, bridges), being in enclosed spaces (shops, cinemas), standing in line or being in a crowd, and being outside the home alone. The person fears these places because escape might be difficult or help might not be available if panic-like or embarrassing symptoms occur (such as fainting or losing bladder control). The situations are avoided, endured with intense fear, or need a companion, and the pattern lasts six months or more. Since DSM-5 (2013), agoraphobia is a separate diagnosis that can be given with or without panic disorder. The word comes from the Greek agora, the marketplace. Option c is right because fear of public speaking is about being watched and judged, the hallmark of social anxiety disorder (social phobia), whose \"performance only\" specifier covers fear limited to speaking or performing in public. The exam trap is that crowds can appear in both: the agoraphobic person fears a crowd because escape is hard, the socially anxious person because of scrutiny. Ask: fear of being trapped, or fear of being judged?", "topic": "Agoraphobia situations versus social anxiety"}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders includes an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, characterized by palpitations, sweating, trembling, chest pain, and fear of losing control or dying?", "options": {"a": "Specific phobia", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder", "c": "Panic disorder", "d": "Social phobia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c, panic disorder. DSM-5-TR defines a panic attack as an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, with at least four of thirteen symptoms, including palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat sensations, numbness or tingling, derealisation or depersonalisation, fear of losing control or going crazy, and fear of dying. Panic disorder is diagnosed when attacks are recurrent and unexpected (out of the blue) and at least one is followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences, or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise. For example, a woman has a sudden attack in a shopping mall, is sure she is having a heart attack, is cleared in casualty, and then lives in dread of the next one. Option c is right because the stem quotes the DSM wording for a panic attack, and panic disorder is the disorder built around these attacks. The exam trap is that panic attacks can occur in any anxiety disorder; in specific or social phobia they are expected, cued by the feared situation, whereas in panic disorder at least some are unexpected and the person comes to fear the attacks themselves.", "topic": "Panic attack and panic disorder"}, {"q": "Blood-Injection-Injury is what type of disorder?", "options": {"a": "Social phobia", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder", "c": "Agoraphobia", "d": "Specific phobia"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is specific phobia: blood-injection-injury (BII) is one of the five subtypes of specific phobia in DSM-5-TR, and ICD-11 also files fear of blood or injury under specific phobia. Specific phobia is marked, out-of-proportion fear of a particular object or situation that almost always provokes immediate fear, is actively avoided, and lasts six months or more. DSM-5-TR asks the clinician to specify the stimulus: animal (spiders, dogs), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury (seeing blood, injections, medical procedures), situational (planes, lifts, enclosed places) and other (such as choking or vomiting). BII phobia is unusual for its diphasic response: heart rate and blood pressure first rise, then drop sharply through a vasovagal reaction, so the person may faint, which is rare in other phobias. Lars-Göran Öst developed applied tension (tensing the muscles to keep blood pressure up) for it, used alongside graded exposure. Option d is right because BII is a named subtype of specific phobia, not a separate disorder. The exam trap is agoraphobia: agoraphobia spans several situations feared because escape would be hard or help unavailable, whereas a specific phobia is tied to one kind of cue, here blood, needles or injury.", "topic": "Blood-injection-injury: a specific phobia subtype"}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders includes a persistent worry or uneasiness spread across multiple situations?", "options": {"a": "Agoraphobia", "b": "Depression", "c": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "d": "Acute stress disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is generalized anxiety disorder (GAD): persistent worry spread across many situations is its defining feature in DSM-5-TR. GAD is excessive anxiety and worry about a number of events or activities (work, health, money, family, small daily matters), present more days than not for at least six months, which the person finds hard to control. Adults need at least three of six symptoms (children only one): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating or mind going blank, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance. ICD-11 similarly describes general apprehension or worry not restricted to any particular circumstance. Picture a mother who worries all day that her husband will crash the car, her son will fail, and the rent will be late, and cannot switch it off. Option c is right because the stem's two cues, persistent and spread across multiple situations, are exactly the free-floating worry of GAD. The exam trap is depression, which shares fatigue, poor concentration and sleep problems with GAD; depression centres on low mood and loss of interest, GAD on future-oriented worry. Acute stress disorder is the other trap: it needs a traumatic event and lasts only three days to one month.", "topic": "Generalized anxiety disorder: pervasive worry"}, {"q": "Which of the following psychotherapy is indicated for treatment of obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "options": {"a": "Humanistic Therapy", "b": "Exposure and Response Prevention", "c": "Existential Therapy", "d": "Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b, exposure and response prevention (ERP). ERP is a behaviour therapy for obsessive-compulsive disorder. It grew from Victor Meyer's work in London in 1966 and was developed and tested by Isaac Marks and Stanley Rachman and later by Edna Foa. The logic: obsessions raise anxiety and compulsions bring it down, so the compulsion is negatively reinforced and the person never learns that the anxiety would fade on its own. In ERP the client is exposed, gradually and using a fear hierarchy, to the cues that trigger obsessions (touching a doorknob, leaving the house without checking the gas) and is helped to refrain from the compulsion (no washing, no checking). Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls (habituation), and the client learns that the feared outcome does not happen. ERP, usually delivered within cognitive-behavioural therapy, is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD in guidelines such as those of NICE, often combined with an SSRI. Option b is right because ERP is the specific, evidence-based psychotherapy for OCD. The exam trap is option d, REBT: it is a cognitive therapy that can be used for anxiety, but it is not the OCD-specific treatment; the words \"response prevention\" are what mark the OCD answer.", "topic": "Exposure and response prevention for OCD"}, {"q": "A 6-year-old girl was referred because she had not been attending school. If forced to attend school she would cry excessively and cling to her mother. She would only settle down in the classroom if her mother was in her sight at school. Which disorder is she likely suffering from?", "options": {"a": "Social anxiety disorder", "b": "Agoraphobia", "c": "Separation anxiety disorder", "d": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c, separation anxiety disorder. In DSM-5-TR, separation anxiety disorder is developmentally inappropriate and excessive fear or anxiety about separation from attachment figures, shown by at least three of eight features: distress when separation happens or is expected, worry about losing the attachment figure or harm coming to them, worry that an event such as getting lost or kidnapped will cause separation, reluctance or refusal to go out or to school, fear of being alone, reluctance to sleep away from the attachment figure, nightmares about separation, and physical complaints such as stomach aches when separation is expected. It must last at least four weeks in children and adolescents (typically six months in adults). DSM-5 moved it into the anxiety disorders chapter and allowed diagnosis in adults. Separation protest is normal in infants and toddlers, but this intensity at age six is excessive. Option c is right because the girl's school refusal, crying and clinging, and settling only when her mother is in sight all centre on separation from her mother. The exam trap is social anxiety disorder, which can also cause school refusal; there the child fears the judgement of classmates or teachers, and her mother's presence would not settle her.", "topic": "Separation anxiety disorder and school refusal"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of Body Dysmorphic Disorder?", "options": {"a": "Reassurance seeking", "b": "Hair pulling", "c": "Excessive grooming", "d": "Repeated mirror checking"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b, hair pulling. Body dysmorphic disorder (BDD), placed in DSM-5-TR under obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, is preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable or look slight to others. At some point the person performs repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing their appearance with others) in response to the concern, and it causes significant distress or impairment. Specifiers include muscle dysmorphia (believing one's body is too small or not muscular enough) and level of insight, which can range down to absent insight with delusional beliefs. The Italian psychiatrist Enrico Morselli called it \"dysmorphophobia\" in 1891. Option b is right because hair pulling is the defining behaviour of trichotillomania (hair-pulling disorder), a separate diagnosis in the same chapter, and it is not a BDD criterion. The exam trap is that a BDD patient may pluck hair to improve appearance, and a trichotillomania patient may be ashamed of hair loss; the tie-breaker is motive. In BDD the act aims to fix a perceived flaw, whereas in trichotillomania the pulling itself is the problem, with repeated failed attempts to stop.", "topic": "Body dysmorphic disorder versus trichotillomania"}, {"q": "Which one of the following is not the feature of a projective test?", "options": {"a": "Ambiguous stimuli", "b": "Structured questions", "c": "Projection of the unconscious", "d": "Responses are subjective"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b, structured questions. A projective test presents an ambiguous, relatively unstructured stimulus and an open task (say what you see, tell a story, finish the sentence), so the person must impose their own organisation on it. This rests on the projective hypothesis, named by Lawrence K. Frank in 1939: faced with an ambiguous stimulus, people project their own needs, conflicts, wishes and ways of seeing onto it, revealing parts of personality they may not be aware of or willing to report. Well-known examples are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), sentence completion tests and the Draw-a-Person test. Because the person cannot easily tell what a \"good\" answer is, faking is harder; because responses are open-ended and scored with more examiner judgement, reliability is a known weakness. Option b is right because structured questions with fixed response choices are the hallmark of objective self-report inventories such as the MMPI or 16PF, the opposite of projective tests. The exam trap is to keep the pair straight: projective means ambiguous stimulus, free response and indirect, often unconscious, content; objective means structured items, fixed answers and standardised scoring.", "topic": "Projective versus objective tests"}, {"q": "Which of the following personality tests is not used in a clinical setting?", "options": {"a": "Myers Briggs Type Indicator", "b": "Thematic Apperception Test", "c": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "d": "16 PF"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a, the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator. The MBTI was developed in the 1940s by Katharine Cook Briggs and her daughter Isabel Briggs Myers, based on Carl Jung's Psychological Types (1921). It sorts people on four dichotomies: Extraversion or Introversion, Sensing or Intuition, Thinking or Feeling, and Judging or Perceiving, giving 16 types such as INTJ or ESFP. It describes normal preferences, has no scales for psychopathology, and is used mainly in counselling, career guidance, team building and organisational training. Psychologists also criticise it because many people get a different type on retest and because forcing continuous traits into either-or types loses information. The other three are used clinically: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is the most widely used clinical personality inventory, the Thematic Apperception Test is a projective test used in clinical assessment, and Raymond Cattell's 16PF, though built for the normal range, is used in clinical and counselling work. Option a is right because the MBTI is a type indicator for normal personality, not a clinical or diagnostic tool. The exam trap is the 16PF, which also measures normal personality but still appears in clinical batteries, whereas the MBTI never diagnoses.", "topic": "MBTI is not a clinical test"}, {"q": "Agoraphobia is often associated with:", "options": {"a": "Fear of closed spaces", "b": "Panic attacks", "c": "Depression", "d": "Eating alone in public"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b, panic attacks. Agoraphobia and panic are closely linked, historically and clinically. In DSM-III-R and DSM-IV, agoraphobia was most often diagnosed as panic disorder with agoraphobia: a person has unexpected panic attacks and then starts avoiding places where an attack would be embarrassing or escape hard, such as buses, markets or crowds. DSM-5 (2013) made them independent diagnoses that can be given together, because some people develop agoraphobia without full panic disorder, but the link remains strong: many people with agoraphobia have panic attacks or panic disorder, and the catastrophe they fear is usually panic-like or incapacitating symptoms. Picture a man who had a panic attack on a metro train and now cannot travel unless his brother comes along. In learning terms this is avoidance learning: the place becomes linked with the attack, and avoiding it reduces anxiety, so avoidance is negatively reinforced. Option b is right because panic attacks are the condition most typically associated with agoraphobia. The exam trap is option a: enclosed spaces are one of the five agoraphobic situations, but a fear limited to closed spaces is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia, not what agoraphobia is classically associated with.", "topic": "Agoraphobia and panic attacks"}, {"q": "Which disorder can be characterized by repeated checking of locks, gas stoves or light switches?", "options": {"a": "Obsessive Compulsive Disorder", "b": "Panic Disorder", "c": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "d": "Specific phobia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a, obsessive-compulsive disorder. In DSM-5-TR, OCD is the presence of obsessions, compulsions or, usually, both. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause marked anxiety or distress, such as \"Did I leave the gas on? The house will burn down.\" Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules, aimed at reducing anxiety or preventing a dreaded event, but either not realistically connected to it or clearly excessive. Checking (locks, gas stoves, switches, doors) is one of the most common symptom dimensions, along with contamination and washing, symmetry and ordering, and forbidden or taboo thoughts. The symptoms must be time-consuming (more than one hour a day) or cause significant distress or impairment, and insight is specified as good or fair, poor, or absent with delusional beliefs. Option a is right because repeatedly checking locks, gas stoves and light switches is the textbook example of a checking compulsion. The exam trap is generalized anxiety disorder: GAD involves worry about real-life matters, but not rituals driven by an intrusive thought and repeated until things \"feel right\".", "topic": "Checking compulsions in OCD"}, {"q": "A 19-year-old male presents with excessive checking of his face in the mirror, believing that he is ugly. He spends 2-4 hours daily checking his face and is socially withdrawn. Which disorder is he suffering from?", "options": {"a": "Social Anxiety Disorder", "b": "Body Dysmorphic Disorder", "c": "Panic Disorder", "d": "Obsessive Compulsive Disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b, body dysmorphic disorder. BDD, in the DSM-5-TR chapter on obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, is preoccupation with one or more perceived defects in appearance that are not observable or look slight to others, together with repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's looks with others), causing significant distress or impairment. Typical concerns are the skin, hair and nose, but any body part can be involved. The most common age of onset is early adolescence, around 12 or 13, and patients often seek cosmetic surgery or dermatological treatment, which rarely helps. Social withdrawal, missed college or work and high rates of suicidal thinking are common. Treatment is cognitive-behavioural therapy with exposure and response prevention, plus SSRIs. Here a 19-year-old believes he is ugly, checks his face for 2 to 4 hours a day and has withdrawn socially. Option b is right because a belief in a defect in appearance plus repetitive mirror checking is the exact core of BDD. The exam trap is social anxiety disorder, because he avoids people; in BDD the avoidance comes from shame about a perceived physical flaw, whereas in social anxiety the fear is of acting in a way that will be judged.", "topic": "Body dysmorphic disorder case recognition"}, {"q": "In Obsessive Compulsive Disorder, compulsions are:", "options": {"a": "Ritualised worrying about negative events happening", "b": "Overwhelming fear of encounters with opposite sex", "c": "Performing repetitive behaviour which the individual feels driven to perform to reduce anxiety", "d": "Repetitive thoughts about harming others"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is option c: compulsions are repetitive behaviours the person feels driven to perform to reduce anxiety, which is almost word for word the DSM-5-TR definition. In obsessive-compulsive disorder, obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (for example, \"my hands are covered in germs\"). Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (praying, counting, repeating words silently) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rules that must be applied rigidly. They aim to prevent or reduce anxiety or distress, or to prevent a dreaded event, but are not realistically connected to what they are meant to prevent, or are clearly excessive. The brief relief negatively reinforces the ritual, which is why exposure and response prevention works by blocking it. Option c is right because it names both parts of a compulsion: a repetitive act, and the drive to perform it to reduce anxiety. The exam trap is option d: repetitive thoughts about harming others are obsessions, not compulsions. Ask whether it intrudes on the person (obsession) or is something the person does to neutralise it (compulsion).", "topic": "Compulsions versus obsessions in OCD"}, {"q": "People with social phobia avoid social situations because they fear:", "options": {"a": "Panic attacks", "b": "Negative evaluation or criticism by people", "c": "Contamination by other people", "d": "Enclosed spaces"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b, fear of negative evaluation. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) in DSM-5-TR is marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the person may be scrutinised by others: conversations, meeting strangers, being watched while eating or drinking, or performing, such as giving a speech. The core fear is that they will act in a way, or show anxiety symptoms such as blushing, sweating or trembling, that will be negatively evaluated: humiliating, embarrassing, or leading to rejection. The situations are avoided or endured with intense fear, the fear is out of proportion, and it lasts six months or more. A \"performance only\" specifier applies when the fear is limited to speaking or performing in public. The cognitive model of David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) explains how attention turns inward, people use safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences), and then misjudge how they came across. Option b is right because fear of negative evaluation or criticism by others is the defining fear in social phobia. The exam trap is option a: socially anxious people may have panic attacks, but these are cued by social scrutiny, whereas fear of the panic attacks themselves points to panic disorder or agoraphobia.", "topic": "Social phobia: fear of negative evaluation"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"ihbas-2024-d": [{"q": "Intelligence is not defined as the:", "concept": "The answer is c: intelligence is not defined as the ability to change the environment and one's personality. Psychologists have offered many definitions, but they share a core of reasoning, learning and adaptation. Alfred Binet stressed judgement and adapting oneself to circumstances; Lewis Terman stressed abstract thinking; David Wechsler called intelligence the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment. In 1994 Linda Gottfredson drafted a statement signed by 52 researchers describing intelligence as a very general mental capability involving the ability to reason, plan, solve problems, think abstractly, comprehend complex ideas, learn quickly and learn from experience. Options a, b and d are paraphrases of exactly these ideas. Personality, by contrast, is a separate domain (traits, temperament, character), and no standard definition treats remaking one's personality as an intellectual ability. Option c is right because it is the only choice that adds something outside the construct, namely changing one's personality. Exam trap: Robert Sternberg's practical (contextual) intelligence does include adapting to, selecting and shaping one's environment, so \"changing the environment\" alone could sound valid; it is the personality half that makes c the odd one out. In \"not\" questions, hunt for the element that belongs to a different construct.", "traps": {"a": "This matches Terman's abstract thinking and the Gottfredson statement (reason, plan, solve problems), so it is an accepted definition, not the exception.", "b": "Comprehending complex ideas and adapting to situations come straight from the Gottfredson statement and Binet's emphasis on adaptation, so this is an accepted definition.", "d": "Choosing good responses and learning from experience echo Edward Thorndike's \"power of good responses\" and the classic \"capacity to learn from experience\" definitions, so it is valid."}}, {"q": "Which of the statement is not correct about intelligence?", "concept": "The answer is b: it is not correct that crystallized intelligence decreases as we grow older. Raymond Cattell, later working with John Horn, split general intelligence into two kinds. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, as in Raven's Progressive Matrices or a puzzle you have never seen. Crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and culture. Research, including K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study, shows that fluid ability peaks in early adulthood and then declines gradually, while crystallized ability stays stable or keeps rising into the sixties and beyond, dropping only in very old age. A 65-year-old teacher may be slower on a novel puzzle than at 25 but knows more words and facts. Both abilities are partly heritable, as twin studies show. Option b is right because it reverses the well-known age pattern: crystallized intelligence is the one that holds up with age. Exam trap: students mix up which ability declines. Remember \"fluid flows away, crystal stays\": fluid declines, crystallized is maintained.", "traps": {"a": "This is a true statement: fluid intelligence peaks in early adulthood and declines gradually, partly because processing speed slows.", "c": "True: fluid intelligence is usually described as closely tied to biological and genetic factors, and twin studies confirm substantial heritability.", "d": "Cattell saw crystallized intelligence as shaped mainly by education and culture, but twin studies show it is also substantially heritable, so \"influenced by genetics\" is still correct; it is not the false statement."}}, {"q": "Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an adaptation of:", "concept": "The answer is d: MISIC is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC). David Wechsler published the WISC in 1949 as a children's version of his adult scale. Like every Wechsler scale up to the WISC-III and WAIS-III, it has a verbal set of subtests (for example information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, vocabulary, digit span) and a performance set (for example picture completion, block design, object assembly, coding, mazes), and it yields verbal, performance and full-scale IQs (from the WISC-IV and WAIS-IV on, verbal and performance IQ gave way to four index scores). Arthur J. Malin adapted the WISC for Indian children in Nagpur, replacing culturally unsuitable items and building Indian norms; the result, Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, is still widely used in Indian clinics. A clinician assessing a 9-year-old with school difficulties in India would typically use MISIC to obtain verbal and performance IQs separately. Option d is right because MISIC keeps the Wechsler structure of verbal and performance subtests with deviation IQ scoring. Exam trap: Malin also produced the Indian adaptation of the Vineland Social Maturity Scale, so his name appears with two different tests. Keep them apart: MISIC measures intelligence (IQ), while Malin's Vineland measures social competence (social age and social quotient).", "traps": {"a": "The Vineland Social Maturity Scale (Edgar Doll, 1935) measures social competence as a social age and social quotient; Malin adapted it for India too, but that adaptation is not MISIC.", "b": "Raven's Coloured Progressive Matrices (John C. Raven) is a non-verbal, culture-reduced reasoning test for young children and older adults; MISIC is not based on it.", "c": "The Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) was adapted in India as the Kamat-Binet by V. V. Kamat, not by Malin; MISIC follows Wechsler's verbal-performance format instead."}}, {"q": "Which of the followings is not a correct branch of social process theory?", "concept": "The answer is d: social information processing theory is not a branch of social process theory. In criminology, social process theories argue that anyone can become delinquent, and that crime grows out of a person's interactions with family, peers, school and the justice system, rather than out of poverty or neighbourhood structure alone (the focus of social structure theories such as strain theory). They have three branches. Social learning theory says crime is learned: Edwin Sutherland's differential association theory held that people learn criminal attitudes and techniques in close groups, and Ronald Akers added reinforcement. Social control theory, best known through Travis Hirschi's social bond theory (1969), asks why most people do not offend and answers that bonds of attachment, commitment, involvement and belief hold them back. Labeling theory, linked with Edwin Lemert and Howard Becker, says that being publicly labelled deviant pushes a person toward a deviant identity (secondary deviance). Option d is right because social information processing is a different model: Nicki Crick and Kenneth Dodge's step-by-step account of how children encode and interpret social cues and choose responses, explaining aggression through biases such as hostile attribution. Exam trap: both names contain \"social\"; one is a criminological family of theories, the other a cognitive model of aggression.", "traps": {"a": "Social learning theory (Sutherland's differential association, Akers' differential reinforcement) is a core branch of social process theory: crime is learned through interaction.", "b": "Social control theory (Hirschi's social bond theory) is a core branch: strong bonds to family, school and conventional activities keep people from offending.", "c": "Labeling (social reaction) theory of Lemert and Becker is the third branch: official labels can push people into a deviant identity and further offending."}}, {"q": "A person has information from multiple observations at different times and situations and can perceive the relationship of an observed effect and its causes. This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is b: attribution. Attribution is the process by which we infer the causes of behaviour and events. Fritz Heider (1958) described people as \"naive scientists\" who look for causes, and Harold Kelley (1967) showed how we do this when we have many observations. His covariation model says we notice which possible cause is present when the effect occurs and absent when it does not, using three kinds of information: consensus (do other people react the same way?), consistency (does this person react this way at different times and in different situations?) and distinctiveness (does the person react this way to other things too?). For example, if your friend Meera has praised the same cafe's coffee every week for months, most of your other friends praise it too, and she rarely praises other cafes, you attribute her praise to the coffee, not to her personality. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly Kelley's situation: information from multiple observations across times and situations used to link an effect to its cause. Exam trap: when only a single observation is available, Kelley says we fall back on causal schemas and the discounting principle instead of covariation, but both are still attribution.", "traps": {"a": "Personalism is one factor in Edward Jones and Keith Davis's correspondent inference theory: we infer a disposition more readily when an act seems aimed at us personally. It is a specific factor, not the general process described.", "c": "Participative (democratic) leadership is a style in which the leader involves group members in decisions; it has nothing to do with inferring causes.", "d": "Intuitiveness is quick, gut-level judgement without systematic evidence, the opposite of weighing many observations across times and situations."}}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of Freud's Psychosexual Stages?", "concept": "The answer is c: oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages in which the libido (sexual or life energy) is focused on a different erogenous zone. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth: sucking, biting, feeding. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) it centres on retaining and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the key conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) it centres on the genitals, and the Oedipus complex is resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is quiet and redirected into schoolwork, skills and same-sex friendships. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexuality directed at others. Too much frustration or indulgence at a stage can cause fixation, for example an \"oral\" adult who smokes or overeats, or an \"anal retentive\" adult who is stingy and obsessively neat. Option c is right because it is the only option that lists the stages in this developmental order. Exam trap: do not confuse these five stages with Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages, and remember latency comes before, not after, the genital stage.", "traps": {"a": "Starts with latency and puts genital before phallic; latency is a middle-childhood period, not the first stage.", "b": "Puts genital second; the genital stage is the last one, beginning at puberty.", "d": "Starts with anal and places oral third; the oral stage always comes first, beginning at birth."}}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of collective unconscious?", "concept": "The answer is d: Carl Gustav Jung. Jung, a Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, divided the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds each individual's forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes. Below it lies the collective unconscious, a deeper layer shared by all humanity and inherited from our ancestral past. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in certain ways, which appear again and again in myths, dreams, religions and fairy tales across cultures. Important archetypes include the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, rejected side), the anima and animus (the feminine side of men and masculine side of women), the great mother, the wise old man, the hero and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala). Jung pointed out that the same images, such as the hero's journey or the great flood, occur in cultures that never met. Option d is right because the collective unconscious is Jung's signature concept. Exam trap: Freud also wrote about the unconscious, but his is personal, made of repressed wishes; \"collective\", \"archetypes\" and \"inherited\" always point to Jung.", "traps": {"a": "Freud described the unconscious as personal, made up of each individual's repressed wishes and memories, not an inherited collective layer.", "b": "Alfred Adler founded individual psychology, built around inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, social interest, style of life and birth order.", "c": "Hans Eysenck was a trait theorist who described personality through biologically based dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism, psychoticism), not layers of the unconscious."}}, {"q": "The Big Five Theory is commonly also known as ______.", "concept": "The answer is b: OCEAN. The Big Five (also called the Five-Factor Model) is the most widely accepted trait model of personality. It grew out of the lexical hypothesis, the idea that important personality differences become encoded as words in language; factor analyses of trait words and ratings, from Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal and Warren Norman onward, repeatedly produced five broad factors. Lewis Goldberg popularised the label \"Big Five\", and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae built the NEO Personality Inventory to measure them. The five factors are Openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), Extraversion (sociable and energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (warm and cooperative versus antagonistic) and Neuroticism (anxious and moody versus emotionally stable). Their initials spell OCEAN. A student who plans every exam week in a timetable and never misses a deadline is high on Conscientiousness. Option b is right because OCEAN is the standard mnemonic formed by the first letters of the five factors. Exam trap: the same five letters are also arranged as CANOE, a genuine alternative mnemonic; the other jumbled arrangements in this question (CEANO, EANCO, CANEO) are not used anywhere.", "traps": {"a": "CEANO contains the right letters but is not a recognised acronym for the Big Five.", "c": "EANCO is a random arrangement of the five initials and is not used in any textbook.", "d": "CANEO looks like the real alternative mnemonic CANOE but swaps the last letters; it is not a recognised acronym."}}, {"q": "Robert McCrae and ______ developed Big Five personality traits theory.", "concept": "The answer is c: Paul Costa. Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae, working at the US National Institute on Aging, are the researchers most closely associated with the Big Five or Five-Factor Model. Starting from a three-factor model (neuroticism, extraversion, openness), they added agreeableness and conscientiousness and published the NEO Personality Inventory in 1985, revised as the NEO-PI-R in 1992 with six facets under each of the five domains. Their longitudinal work showed that the five traits are fairly stable in adulthood, appear across many cultures and are partly heritable. The five factors themselves had earlier roots: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal and then Warren Norman found five factors in trait ratings, and Lewis Goldberg coined the name \"Big Five\". Option c is right because \"McCrae and Costa\" is the standard pairing for the Five-Factor Model and the NEO inventories. Exam trap: the distractor \"Normann Munn\" plays on two names. Warren Norman did help discover the five factors, but Norman L. Munn was the author of a well-known introductory psychology textbook and had nothing to do with the Big Five.", "traps": {"a": "Gordon Allport pioneered trait theory (cardinal, central and secondary traits) and, with Henry Odbert, listed trait words from the dictionary, but he did not develop the Big Five with McCrae.", "b": "Carl Jung proposed introversion and extraversion and the psychological types, an early typology, not the Five-Factor Model.", "d": "Norman L. Munn was an introductory psychology textbook author; the similar-sounding Warren Norman found five factors in 1963 but did not partner with McCrae."}}, {"q": "Which is the fourth stage of psycho-social development of an individual according to Erikson?", "concept": "The answer is a: industry versus inferiority. Erik Erikson, a psychoanalyst trained in Anna Freud's circle in Vienna, extended Freud's theory into eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and successful resolution gives a basic virtue. In order: (1) trust versus mistrust, infancy, hope; (2) autonomy versus shame and doubt, toddlerhood, will; (3) initiative versus guilt, preschool years, purpose; (4) industry versus inferiority, about 6 to 12 years, competence; (5) identity versus role confusion, adolescence, fidelity; (6) intimacy versus isolation, young adulthood, love; (7) generativity versus stagnation, middle adulthood, care; (8) integrity versus despair, old age, wisdom. In the fourth stage the school-age child learns the skills valued by the culture; a child praised for finishing a science project develops a sense of industry, while one who keeps failing or is unfavourably compared with others may feel inferior. Option a is right because industry versus inferiority is fourth in this sequence and corresponds to Freud's latency period. Exam trap: count carefully. Identity versus role confusion is the famous adolescent stage, but it is the fifth, not the fourth.", "traps": {"b": "Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage, young adulthood, when the task is forming close, committed relationships.", "c": "Identity versus role confusion is the fifth stage, adolescence; it is often wrongly picked because it is Erikson's best-known stage.", "d": "Generativity versus stagnation is the seventh stage, middle adulthood, concerned with guiding the next generation and productive work."}}, {"q": "According to Carl Rogers which role a teacher should play while giving knowledge that helps to create an atmosphere favorable to learning?", "concept": "The answer is b: facilitator. Carl Rogers, founder of client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, applied the same ideas to education, especially in his book Freedom to Learn (1969). He argued that significant learning cannot simply be poured into students; it happens when learners are self-directed and the material feels personally relevant. The teacher's job is therefore to facilitate learning: to create a climate of trust in which students set goals, explore and evaluate their own progress. Rogers said the facilitator needs the same core conditions that make therapy work: genuineness or realness (being a real person rather than playing the expert), unconditional positive regard (prizing and accepting the learner) and empathic understanding (seeing the classroom from the student's side). In a Rogerian class the teacher might help students choose a project on a topic that interests them and act as a resource, rather than lecture and test recall. Option b is right because \"facilitator\" is the exact term Rogers used for a teacher who creates an atmosphere favourable to learning. Exam trap: \"authoritative\" sounds positive because Diana Baumrind's authoritative parenting is the recommended style, but it is a parenting term and still teacher-centred; Rogers wanted learner-centred teaching.", "traps": {"a": "Authoritative is Baumrind's parenting style (warm but firm, with clear rules); in a classroom it still makes the teacher the authority, not the learner-centred facilitator Rogers described.", "c": "A commanding teacher directs and orders students, the opposite of Rogers's non-directive, trusting climate.", "d": "Authoritarian means rigid, controlling and punitive (Baumrind's harsh parenting style, Lewin's autocratic leadership), which Rogers saw as blocking real learning."}}, {"q": "Maslow theory states that individuals who are well adjusted attempt to follow the following process:", "concept": "The answer is b: well-adjusted individuals try to actualize themselves. Abraham Maslow, a founder of humanistic psychology, proposed a hierarchy of needs (1943, expanded in Motivation and Personality, 1954): physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem, and at the top self-actualization, the drive to become everything one is capable of becoming. He called the lower needs deficiency needs (D-needs) and the growth needs at the top being needs (B-needs). Maslow studied people he saw as psychologically healthy, such as Abraham Lincoln, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described self-actualizers as realistic, accepting of themselves and others, spontaneous, problem-centred, autonomous, creative and prone to peak experiences, with deep relationships and a feeling of kinship with humanity. A doctor who leaves a well-paid post to run a rural clinic because it fulfils her values is striving toward self-actualization. Option b is right because, for Maslow, self-actualization is the very process that marks healthy adjustment. Exam trap: Maslow noted that self-actualizers need privacy and are independent of their culture, which can tempt you toward \"remain aloof\"; but this is healthy detachment, not withdrawal from society, since they also have strong social interest.", "traps": {"a": "Trying to convince others one has no faults is defensive; Maslow's healthy people accept their own imperfections.", "c": "Winning others over to one's way of thinking reflects a need for esteem or dominance; self-actualizers are democratic and respect other views.", "d": "Self-actualizers value privacy and detachment, but they feel kinship with humanity (Gemeinschaftsgefühl) and are not aloof from society."}}, {"q": "What does attribution theory primarily seek to explain?", "concept": "The answer is a: attribution theory primarily seeks to explain how individuals perceive and interpret the behaviour of others. Fritz Heider, in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), argued that ordinary people act as \"naive psychologists\" who constantly ask why others behave as they do. His key distinction was between internal (dispositional) causes, such as ability, effort or personality, and external (situational) causes, such as luck, task difficulty or pressure from others. Later theorists built on this: Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) explained when we infer a person's traits from their acts (correspondent inference), Harold Kelley (1967) explained how we use consensus, consistency and distinctiveness, and Bernard Weiner added stability and controllability. Example: when a classmate snaps at you, you might decide she is rude (internal) or that she just got bad news (external), and that judgement shapes how you respond to her. Option a is right because the theory was built to explain how we make sense of other people's behaviour by assigning causes to it. Exam trap: attribution ideas were later applied to the self as well (Daryl Bem's self-perception theory), but that is an extension, not the primary aim.", "traps": {"b": "Self-perception is an extension (Bem's self-perception theory, the self-serving bias), but attribution theory began as an account of how we explain other people's behaviour.", "c": "How personality traits develop is a question for personality and developmental psychology, not attribution theory.", "d": "Friendship formation is studied under interpersonal attraction (proximity, similarity, reciprocity), not attribution theory."}}, {"q": "Which term refers to the tendency to attribute one's successes to internal factors and failures to external factors?", "concept": "The answer is b: self-serving bias. The self-serving bias is the tendency to take credit for our successes by attributing them to internal causes (ability, effort) while blaming our failures on external causes (bad luck, an unfair examiner, a noisy hall). A student who tops a test says \"I worked hard\", but after failing says \"the paper was out of syllabus\". The usual explanation is motivational: the bias protects and enhances self-esteem, and it also helps us present ourselves well to others. It is one of several attributional biases studied in social psychology, along with the fundamental attribution error and the actor-observer bias. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the success-internal, failure-external pattern that defines the self-serving bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (named by Lee Ross in 1977) is about how we explain other people's behaviour, overestimating their dispositions and underestimating the situation; the self-serving bias is about explaining our own outcomes and depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. If the stem mentions success versus failure, think self-serving bias; if it mentions judging others' behaviour as personality-driven, think fundamental attribution error.", "traps": {"a": "The fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is the tendency to over-explain other people's behaviour by their dispositions while ignoring situational forces; it does not hinge on success or failure.", "c": "The actor-observer bias (Jones and Nisbett) contrasts explaining our own behaviour situationally with explaining others' dispositionally; it is not about successes versus failures.", "d": "Attributional bias is the umbrella term for all systematic errors in attribution; the specific success-failure pattern has its own name, self-serving bias."}}, {"q": "Which term describes the tendency to attribute one's own actions to external factors while attributing others' actions to internal factors?", "concept": "The answer is c: actor-observer bias. Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) proposed that people explain the same behaviour differently depending on whether they are the actor doing it or an observer watching it. As actors we tend to explain our own behaviour by the situation; as observers we tend to explain other people's behaviour by their personality. If you trip on the stairs you blame the wet floor, but when a stranger trips you think he is clumsy. Two reasons are usually given: perceptual salience (the actor sees the situation, while the observer's eyes are on the actor) and information (we know our own behaviour varies across situations, but we know little of the stranger's history). Option c is right because the stem has both halves, external for one's own actions and internal for others' actions, which is the defining contrast of the actor-observer bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error describes only the observer half (over-attributing others' behaviour to dispositions); the actor-observer bias adds the contrast with oneself. And the self-serving bias depends on whether the outcome was a success or a failure, not on whose behaviour it is.", "traps": {"a": "Self-serving bias is about crediting our successes internally and blaming our failures externally; it hinges on the outcome, not on self versus others.", "b": "The fundamental attribution error covers only how we explain others (too dispositionally); it does not include the contrast with how we explain ourselves.", "d": "The ultimate attribution error (Thomas Pettigrew, 1979) works at the group level: negative acts by outgroup members are blamed on their disposition and positive ones explained away, with the reverse for the ingroup."}}, {"q": "Kelley's Attribution Theory focuses on:", "concept": "The answer is c: Kelley's theory deals with both internal and external attributions. Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) explains how, with repeated observations, we decide whether a behaviour is caused by the person (internal) or by the stimulus or situation (external). We use three kinds of information: consensus (do other people behave the same way toward this stimulus?), consistency (does this person behave this way toward it on other occasions?) and distinctiveness (does this person behave this way toward other stimuli?). Example: Arjun laughs at a comedian. If few others laugh (low consensus), Arjun laughs at this comedian every time (high consistency) and laughs at all comedians (low distinctiveness), we make an internal attribution: Arjun is easily amused. If everyone laughs (high consensus), Arjun always laughs at this comedian (high consistency) but rarely at others (high distinctiveness), we make an external attribution: the comedian is funny. Low consistency points to passing circumstances. Option c is right because the whole point of the model is to choose between internal and external causes. Exam trap: Jones and Davis's correspondent inference theory deals only with inferring internal dispositions, so \"internal attributions only\" describes them, not Kelley.", "traps": {"a": "\"Internal attributions only\" better describes Jones and Davis's correspondent inference theory, which asks when an act reveals a person's disposition.", "b": "Kelley's model does not ignore personal causes; low consensus, high consistency and low distinctiveness lead to an internal attribution.", "d": "Since option c correctly describes the model, \"none of the above\" is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which is not a sampling method?", "concept": "The answer is d: logistic is not a sampling method. Sampling is the process of choosing a subset (sample) from a population so that results can be generalised. Methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member has a known, non-zero chance of selection: simple random sampling (lottery or random number tables), systematic sampling (every kth person after a random start), stratified random sampling (divide the population into strata such as gender or class, then sample randomly within each) and cluster sampling (randomly select whole naturally occurring groups, such as schools or villages, and study everyone in them). In non-probability sampling selection is not random: convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. \"Logistic\" is not a sampling method at all; the word belongs to logistic regression, a statistical technique for predicting a two-category (dichotomous) outcome, such as relapse versus no relapse, from one or more predictors. Option d is right because it names a data-analysis technique, not a way of selecting participants. Exam trap: examiners often slip an analysis term into a list of sampling methods; ask of each option \"is this a way of picking people, or a way of analysing data?\"", "traps": {"a": "Cluster sampling is a probability method in which whole groups (such as schools or villages) are randomly selected.", "b": "Stratified sampling divides the population into subgroups and samples randomly within each; it is a standard probability method.", "c": "Random sampling, in which every member has an equal chance of selection, is the basic probability sampling method."}}, {"q": "In order to pursue the research, which of the following is priorly required?", "concept": "The answer is b: formulating a research question is required before anything else. Research is usually described as a sequence of steps: identify and formulate the research problem or question, review the literature, state the hypothesis, choose a research design and sample, collect data, analyse the data, interpret the results and write the report. The research question is the starting point because every later decision depends on it. You cannot write a hypothesis until you know what you are asking, you cannot choose between an experiment and a survey without knowing what you want to find out, and the statistical test depends on the kind of question and data. For example, the question \"Does yoga reduce exam anxiety in college students?\" leads to the hypothesis \"Students who practise yoga will report lower anxiety than those who do not\", which in turn suggests a two-group design and a t-test. A good research question is clear, specific, feasible, researchable and significant. Option b is right because the question must exist before a hypothesis, design or analysis plan can be built. Exam trap: the hypothesis is sometimes thought to come first, but a hypothesis is a tentative answer to a question, so the question always comes before it.", "traps": {"a": "The research design is the plan for answering the question; it can only be chosen once the question is clear.", "c": "The data-analysis procedure depends on the question, the design and the kind of data collected, so it comes later.", "d": "A hypothesis is a tentative, testable answer to the research question, so it is formulated after the question (and usually after reviewing the literature)."}}, {"q": "Questions in which only two alternatives are possible is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: dichotomous questions. A dichotomous question offers only two possible answers, such as yes or no, true or false, or agree or disagree. Example: \"Have you ever consulted a psychologist? Yes / No\". Such items are quick to answer, easy to code (often as 0 and 1) and easy to analyse, which makes them useful for screening and factual questions. Their drawbacks are that they force people into one of two boxes, lose information about degree or intensity, and invite guessing or acquiescence. Questionnaire items are often classified as closed (structured) or open. Closed questions give fixed answer choices and include dichotomous items (two choices), multiple-choice items (three or more choices) and rating scales; open-ended questions let respondents answer in their own words. Option b is right because \"only two alternatives\" is the defining feature of a dichotomous question; the word comes from the Greek for \"cut in two\". Exam trap: dichotomous questions are a kind of structured question, so \"structured\" is not false, but it is the broader category; when the stem specifies exactly two alternatives, the precise answer is dichotomous.", "traps": {"a": "Multiple choice questions offer three or more answer options, not just two.", "c": "Open-ended questions provide no fixed options at all; respondents answer in their own words.", "d": "Structured questions is the broad category of fixed-format questions (dichotomous, multiple choice, rating scales); it is not the specific name for two-alternative items."}}, {"q": "Which one is called non-probability sampling?", "concept": "The answer is a: quota sampling is a non-probability method. In non-probability sampling the chance of any member being selected is unknown, because selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience rather than on chance. In quota sampling the researcher first decides how many people of each type are needed, usually to mirror the population on characteristics such as gender, age or religion, and then fills each quota with whoever is available. For example, an interviewer told to survey 50 men and 50 women at a mall will stop passers-by until both quotas are full; the proportions look right, but who gets picked within each quota is not random. Other non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement) and snowball sampling. Option a is right because quota sampling fills subgroups by convenience, so it lacks random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum (probability), while quota sampling does not (non-probability).", "traps": {"b": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups such as schools or villages, so it is a probability method.", "c": "Systematic sampling picks every kth person from a list after a random start, which makes it a probability method.", "d": "Stratified random sampling selects randomly within each subgroup, so it is a probability method, unlike quota sampling."}}, {"q": "Random sampling is also called ______.", "concept": "The answer is c: random sampling is also called probability sampling. Probability sampling means that every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, so selection is governed by chance rather than by the researcher's choice. This is why many textbooks use \"random sampling\" and \"probability sampling\" as rough synonyms. Strictly speaking, simple random sampling (every member has an equal chance, as in drawing names from a hat or using random number tables) is the basic type, and systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling are other forms of probability sampling. Because selection is by chance, probability samples allow sampling error to be estimated and results to be generalised to the population with stated confidence. For example, to survey 200 of 2,000 college students, you could number all the students and let a computer pick 200 numbers at random. Option c is right because random selection is the defining feature of probability sampling. Exam trap: the opposite family, non-probability sampling, includes availability (convenience), purposive, quota and snowball sampling, where the chance of selection is unknown and results cannot be generalised with the same confidence.", "traps": {"a": "Availability (convenience) sampling uses whoever is easy to reach, a non-probability method and the opposite of random sampling.", "b": "\"Probation sampling\" is not a sampling term; it is a distractor built to sound like \"probability\".", "d": "\"Prospect sampling\" is not a recognised sampling method; do not confuse it with prospective designs, which follow people forward in time."}}, {"q": "SPSS is an acronym of the following:", "concept": "The answer is c: SPSS stands for Statistical Package for the Social Sciences. It is a computer program for entering, managing and statistically analysing data, developed in 1968 at Stanford University by Norman Nie, Dale Bent and C. Hadlai (Tex) Hull to help social scientists analyse survey data. IBM acquired SPSS Inc. in 2009, and the program is now sold as IBM SPSS Statistics. Data are entered in a spreadsheet-like Data View, with each row a participant and each column a variable, while the Variable View defines each variable's name, type, labels and level of measurement. Analyses run from menus or from syntax commands and cover descriptive statistics, t-tests, ANOVA, correlation, regression, factor analysis, chi-square and non-parametric tests. A psychology student who has collected anxiety scores from 100 participants would typically enter them in SPSS and run an independent-samples t-test to compare men and women. Option c is right because it gives the program's actual full name. Exam trap: option a sounds technical and plausible, but SPSS is a \"package\" of statistical procedures, not \"predictions\". Other common packages are R, SAS, Stata and JASP.", "traps": {"a": "Plausible-sounding but invented; SPSS is a package of statistical procedures, not \"statistical predictions\".", "b": "A joke expansion with no connection to statistics or software.", "d": "Another joke expansion; SAD (seasonal affective disorder) has nothing to do with the software."}}, {"q": "Which test is the part of the parametric test?", "concept": "The answer is d: the z-test is a parametric test. Parametric tests estimate or compare population parameters such as the mean, and they rest on assumptions: the data are on an interval or ratio scale, the population (or sampling distribution) is approximately normal, and, when groups are compared, variances are roughly equal. The main parametric tests are the z-test, t-tests, ANOVA and Pearson's correlation. A z-test compares a sample mean with a population mean, or two means, when the population standard deviation is known or the sample is large (commonly above 30). For example, testing whether the mean IQ of 100 children in a school differs from the population mean of 100 (SD 15) uses a z-test. Non-parametric (distribution-free) tests make fewer assumptions and usually work with ranks, signs or frequencies; they suit ordinal data, small samples or skewed distributions. Option d is right because it is the only test listed that assumes normality and works directly with means and standard deviations. Exam trap: learn the pairs: independent t-test with Mann-Whitney U, paired t-test with Wilcoxon signed-rank (or the sign test), one-way ANOVA with Kruskal-Wallis, and Pearson r with Spearman rho.", "traps": {"a": "The sign test is non-parametric: for paired data it uses only the direction (plus or minus) of each difference, not its size.", "b": "The runs test (Wald-Wolfowitz) is non-parametric: it checks whether a sequence of observations is random by counting runs.", "c": "The Kruskal-Wallis H test is the non-parametric, rank-based alternative to one-way ANOVA for three or more independent groups."}}, {"q": "The split-half method is used as a test of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the split-half method is a test of internal reliability (internal consistency). Reliability means consistency of measurement. In the split-half method a test is given once, then divided into two comparable halves, most often odd-numbered versus even-numbered items, and scores on the two halves are correlated. If the items measure the same thing, people who do well on one half should do well on the other. Because each half is only half as long as the full test, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the whole test; it is corrected with the Spearman-Brown formula: full-test reliability = 2r / (1 + r). If the halves correlate 0.60, the corrected reliability is 1.20 / 1.60 = 0.75. Other internal consistency methods are the Kuder-Richardson formulas (KR-20, KR-21) and Cronbach's alpha, which in effect average across all possible splits. Option b is right because the split-half method needs only one administration and checks whether the items hang together. Exam trap: stability over time is test-retest reliability, which needs two administrations, and equivalence is parallel-forms reliability; split-half is neither.", "traps": {"a": "Stability is assessed by test-retest reliability, giving the same test twice over a time interval; split-half uses a single administration.", "c": "Inter-observer (inter-rater) consistency is agreement between scorers, measured with correlations or Cohen's kappa, not by splitting a test.", "d": "External validity concerns whether a study's findings generalise to other people, settings and times; it is a validity concept, not reliability."}}, {"q": "The degree of freedom for paired t-test based on n pairs of observations is:", "concept": "The answer is d: n - 1. Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values free to vary once a statistic has been fixed; with n scores and a known mean, n - 1 of them can vary but the last one is determined. A paired (dependent or related samples) t-test is used when each score in one condition is matched with a score in the other, as in a before-and-after design or matched pairs. The test works on the difference score for each pair: it computes the mean and standard deviation of the n differences and tests whether the mean difference is zero. Because it is really a one-sample t-test on n difference scores, df = n - 1. Example: anxiety is measured in 15 patients before and after therapy; there are 15 pairs, so df = 14. Option d is right because n pairs give n difference scores, and one df is lost in estimating their mean. Exam trap: for an independent-samples t-test, df = n1 + n2 - 2, which equals 2(n - 1) when both groups have n people, so option c is the independent-groups answer; and n - 2 is the df for testing the significance of a Pearson correlation.", "traps": {"a": "2n - 1 does not correspond to any standard t-test; it would treat all 2n scores as one sample.", "b": "n - 2 is the df for testing the significance of Pearson's r (or a simple regression), not for a paired t-test.", "c": "2(n - 1) = 2n - 2 is the df for an independent-samples t-test with two groups of n each; a paired test uses only the n differences."}}, {"q": "The strange situation study is related to:", "concept": "The answer is c: Mary Ainsworth. The Strange Situation is a laboratory procedure developed by Mary Ainsworth (first described with Barbara Wittig in 1969 and fully reported in Patterns of Attachment, 1978, with Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall) to assess the quality of an infant's attachment to the caregiver. Infants of about 12 to 18 months go through eight short episodes, each about 3 minutes, in an unfamiliar playroom: mother and infant explore, a stranger enters, the mother leaves and later returns, the infant is left alone, and so on. Observers note how the infant uses the mother as a secure base for exploration, how upset it is at separation and, above all, how it behaves at reunion. Ainsworth identified three patterns: secure (Type B), anxious-avoidant (Type A) and anxious-resistant or ambivalent (Type C), and linked them to the mother's sensitivity, based on home observations in Baltimore and earlier fieldwork in Uganda. Option c is right because the procedure was created by Ainsworth. Exam trap: Mary Main and Judith Solomon later used the Strange Situation to add a fourth, disorganized pattern (Type D), so their names are linked with it, but they did not create it.", "traps": {"a": "Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964) studied 60 Glasgow infants at home and described the stages of attachment and multiple attachments; they did not devise the Strange Situation.", "b": "Mary Main and Judith Solomon added the disorganized/disoriented category using Ainsworth's procedure; they did not originate it.", "d": "Jean Piaget studied cognitive development (sensorimotor to formal operations), not attachment."}}, {"q": "A child throws regular tantrum in eating dinner and the parents are suggested to decrease the quantity of food at tea time and praising the child when the child eats dinner properly. This is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: antecedent operation and consequent operation. Behaviour analysts describe behaviour with the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens before), the Behaviour itself, and Consequences (what follows). Behaviour can be changed from either end. An antecedent operation alters conditions before the behaviour so that it becomes more or less likely; an important kind is the motivating (establishing) operation, a concept developed by Jack Michael, which changes how valuable a reinforcer is. Food deprivation, for example, makes food more reinforcing. A consequent operation alters what follows the behaviour, such as reinforcement, extinction or punishment. Here the parents cut down the tea-time snack, so the child arrives at dinner hungry and eating becomes more attractive: an antecedent (establishing) operation. They then praise the child for eating dinner properly: a consequent operation (positive reinforcement). Option b is right because the plan combines one change before the behaviour with one change after it, and only option b names both parts. Exam trap: option a is tempting because praise is positive reinforcement, but reducing the snack is not negative reinforcement. Negative reinforcement means removing an unpleasant stimulus after a behaviour; the snack reduction happens before dinner and does not depend on how the child behaves.", "traps": {"a": "Praise is positive reinforcement, but cutting the tea-time snack is not negative reinforcement, which means removing something unpleasant after a behaviour; it is an antecedent change made before dinner.", "c": "Systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe) pairs relaxation with a graded anxiety hierarchy to treat phobias and anxiety; it plays no part in a feeding-tantrum plan.", "d": "Differential reinforcement reinforces one behaviour while withholding reinforcement from another (DRO, DRA, DRI); it is a consequence-only procedure and does not cover the snack reduction."}}, {"q": "Which personality theory is also called the Tripartite Theory?", "concept": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud. Freud's structural model of the mind, set out in The Ego and the Id (1923), divides personality into three parts, which is why it is called the tripartite theory. The id is present from birth, entirely unconscious, and seeks immediate gratification of instinctual drives according to the pleasure principle, using primary process thinking. The ego develops to deal with reality; it follows the reality principle, uses secondary process (logical) thinking and mediates between the id, the superego and the outside world. The superego, which forms as the child resolves the Oedipus complex by internalising parental and social standards, contains the conscience (which punishes with guilt) and the ego ideal (which rewards with pride). Example: a hungry student in a lecture wants to grab a snack (id), feels it would be rude (superego) and decides to eat at the break (ego). Option c is right because \"tripartite\" refers to Freud's three-part structure of id, ego and superego. Exam trap: Freud's earlier topographic model (conscious, preconscious, unconscious) also has three parts, but it describes levels of awareness, not structures of personality; and Eysenck's three dimensions (P, E, N) are traits, not a tripartite structure.", "traps": {"a": "Jung's psyche has ego, personal unconscious and collective unconscious, but his theory is called analytical psychology, not the tripartite theory.", "b": "Eysenck proposed three trait dimensions (psychoticism, extraversion, neuroticism), a hierarchical trait model rather than a three-part structure of the psyche.", "d": "Carl Rogers's person-centred theory is built around the self-concept, conditions of worth and incongruence, not a three-part structure."}}, {"concept": "The answer is a: Guilford. The cube in the figure is J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect (SI) model, introduced in the 1950s and set out fully in The Nature of Human Intelligence (1967). Guilford rejected a single general factor and proposed that every intellectual ability is a combination of three dimensions. Operations (what the mind does): cognition, memory, divergent production, convergent production and evaluation. Contents (what it works on): figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural. Products (the form the result takes): units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications. Each small block of the cube is one ability, so the original model had 5 x 4 x 6 = 120 abilities. Guilford later split figural content into visual and auditory (150 abilities) and memory into memory recording and memory retention (180 abilities). Example: listing as many uses for a brick as possible is divergent production of semantic units. Option a is right because the three-faced cube of operations, contents and products is uniquely Guilford's. Exam trap: Guilford's divergent production is the origin of modern creativity tests, so link him with creativity too; and do not confuse his 120 independent abilities with Thurstone's seven primary mental abilities, which were also multiple but far fewer.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman proposed the two-factor theory: a general factor g plus specific factors s, the opposite of Guilford's many independent abilities.", "c": "Louis Thurstone proposed seven primary mental abilities (verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed, reasoning), not a three-dimensional cube.", "d": "Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory has three kinds of intelligence (analytical, creative, practical), usually drawn as a triangle, not a cube."}}, {"q": "Harry Harlow's experiment on maternal deprivation and social isolation in 1950s and 1960s explored:", "concept": "The answer is a: impact of early bonds on behaviour and functioning. Harry Harlow, working with infant rhesus monkeys at the University of Wisconsin, separated babies from their mothers and raised them with two surrogate mothers: one of bare wire that held a feeding bottle, and one covered in soft terry cloth that gave no food. The infants spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother and ran to her when frightened, visiting the wire mother only to feed. In his 1958 paper \"The Nature of Love\" Harlow concluded that contact comfort, not feeding, is the basis of the infant's bond, contradicting the drive-reduction or \"cupboard love\" view. His later isolation studies reared monkeys alone for months; they developed rocking, self-clasping, fearfulness, aggression and severe social difficulties, and isolated females later neglected or abused their own infants. Effects of shorter isolation could be partly reversed by contact with younger \"therapist\" monkeys. Option a is right because it is the broad conclusion that covers both programmes: early bonds shape later social, emotional and parenting behaviour. Exam trap: options b, c and d each describe only a slice of the work, or the wrong one; also keep Harlow (monkeys, contact comfort) apart from John Bowlby (the human maternal deprivation hypothesis).", "traps": {"b": "Social isolation was studied, but \"personality development\" is too narrow and not Harlow's framing; his conclusion concerned early bonds and their effect on overall social functioning.", "c": "Harlow did not measure developmental milestones such as sitting or walking; he studied attachment and social behaviour.", "d": "Harlow did not classify secure versus insecure relationships; that framework comes from Ainsworth's Strange Situation research with human infants."}}, {"concept": "The answer is d: Poggendorff illusion. In the figure a straight diagonal line passes behind a vertical bar, and on the far side two parallel candidate lines emerge. The segment that looks like the continuation is usually not the true one, because the line seems to shift out of alignment as it crosses the gap. The second panel makes the bar see-through and shows which segment is truly collinear with the first. The illusion is named after the German physicist Johann Christian Poggendorff and was first reported in 1860. It is a geometric illusion of direction and alignment, in the same family as the Zöllner, Hering and Müller-Lyer figures. One classic explanation is that the acute angles where the line meets the edges of the bar are perceptually enlarged, which bends the apparent path of each segment; other accounts appeal to depth, since the bar looks like an object lying in front of the line. Option d is right because an interrupted oblique line whose two halves look misaligned is the defining Poggendorff figure. Exam trap: the Zöllner illusion also uses oblique lines, but there long parallel lines look tilted because of short crossing hatch marks; in the Poggendorff, a single straight line looks broken across a gap.", "traps": {"a": "Phantaglyphs (a kind of phantogram) are flat stereoscopic pictures that seem to stand up off the page in 3D when viewed at an angle, usually through coloured anaglyph glasses; they have nothing to do with line alignment.", "b": "The Ternus illusion (Josef Ternus, 1926) is an apparent-motion display: a row of dots shifted by one position is seen either as one dot jumping (element motion) or as the whole row moving (group motion), depending on the timing.", "c": "The Orbison illusion (William Orbison, 1939) makes a square or circle look distorted when drawn over a background of concentric circles or radiating lines."}}, {"q": "According to Luria, the fundamental symptoms in schizophrenia essentially are:", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: Standard textbooks do not credit Alexander Luria, the Soviet neuropsychologist, with a theory of the \"fundamental symptoms\" of schizophrenia; that phrase belongs to Eugen Bleuler (1911), so the item has probably mixed up the names. The official key (a, cognitive) still fits Bleuler's view and the modern one, so answer a in the exam. The answer is a: cognitive in nature. Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist who coined the term schizophrenia (a splitting of psychic functions, not a split personality), divided its symptoms into fundamental symptoms, present in every case, and accessory symptoms, such as hallucinations, delusions and catatonia, which may or may not appear. The fundamental symptoms are the four As: loosening of Associations, disturbed Affect, Ambivalence and Autism (withdrawal into an inner world). Bleuler regarded the disturbance of associations, a disorder of thinking, as the primary one. Modern research agrees that cognitive impairment (attention, working memory, processing speed, executive function) is a core, early feature that predicts functional outcome. Option a is right because the fundamental disturbance is a cognitive one. Exam trap: positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions are Bleuler's accessory symptoms and Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms, not the fundamental ones.", "traps": {"b": "Affect is one of Bleuler's four As, but he saw the thinking (association) disturbance as primary, so \"affective in nature\" is not the core answer.", "c": "Positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions are what Bleuler called accessory symptoms; they are dramatic but not fundamental.", "d": "Negative symptoms (blunted affect, alogia, avolition) matter a great deal, especially in Tim Crow's Type II schizophrenia, but they are not the classic answer for the fundamental symptoms."}}, {"q": "Children from families where parents may serve as both a source of comfort and fear may develop:", "concept": "The answer is b: disorganized attachment. Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation originally sorted infants into three patterns: secure, avoidant and ambivalent (resistant). Some infants fitted none of them, and in 1986 and 1990 Mary Main and Judith Solomon described a fourth, disorganized/disoriented pattern (Type D). These infants show no coherent strategy at reunion: they may approach the parent with the head turned away, freeze, rock, look dazed, or start toward the parent and then back off. Main and Erik Hesse explained why: the parent is frightening (for example, abusive) or frightened (for example, haunted by unresolved trauma or loss). The child is caught in a paradox, because the person it should run to for safety is also the source of fear, so approach and avoidance are triggered at once, a state described as \"fright without solution\". Disorganized attachment is common among maltreated children and predicts later dissociation and behaviour problems. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this: a parent who is both a source of comfort and a source of fear. Exam trap: ambivalent attachment comes from inconsistent but not frightening care; the infant clings and resists at the same time, yet still has an organised strategy, while the disorganized infant has none.", "traps": {"a": "Avoidant attachment (Type A) arises from consistently rejecting or insensitive care; the infant shows little distress at separation and ignores the parent at reunion, an organised strategy.", "c": "Ambivalent (resistant) attachment (Type C) arises from inconsistent care; the infant is very distressed at separation and both clings and resists at reunion, but the parent is not a source of fear.", "d": "Secure attachment (Type B) comes from sensitive, responsive care; the infant uses the parent as a secure base and is easily comforted at reunion."}}, {"q": "People tend to see cause-and-effect relationships is a part of:", "concept": "The answer is d, Heider's attribution theory. Attribution is the process by which we explain why a behaviour or event happened. Fritz Heider, an Austrian-born psychologist, set out the idea in his 1958 book The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations. He argued that ordinary people act like \"naive scientists\": we need the social world to feel orderly and predictable, so we constantly look for cause-and-effect links behind what people do. Heider's key distinction was between internal (personal or dispositional) causes, such as ability, effort or personality, and external (situational) causes, such as luck, task difficulty or pressure from others. For example, if a classmate fails an exam, you may decide she is lazy (internal) or that the paper was unfair (external). Later theories built on this base: Edward Jones and Keith Davis's correspondent inference theory (1965), Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) and Bernard Weiner's model of success and failure. Option d is right because the stem describes the core assumption of attribution theory, the human tendency to perceive cause-and-effect relationships in behaviour. The exam trap is Heider's other famous idea, balance theory, which is about keeping our likes and dislikes of people and objects consistent, not about explaining causes.", "traps": {"a": "Elizabeth Hurlock wrote standard texts on child and life-span development (principles and stages of growth); she did not propose a theory of how people infer causes.", "b": "Erik Erikson's theory describes eight psychosocial crises across the life span, such as trust versus mistrust; it is about ego development, not causal explanation.", "c": "J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect model splits intelligence into many abilities formed by operations, contents and products, and introduced convergent versus divergent thinking; it says nothing about perceiving causes."}}, {"q": "When a person assigns the cause of behavior to some internal characteristic of a person rather than to outside forces, that is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c, dispositional attribution. When we explain a behaviour we can locate its cause inside the person or outside the person. Fritz Heider (1958) called these internal and external attributions; today they are usually called dispositional and situational attributions. A dispositional attribution says the behaviour reflects something stable about the person, such as a trait, attitude, ability or motive: \"Ravi shouted at the waiter because he is rude.\" A situational attribution says the behaviour was produced by circumstances: \"Ravi shouted because the waiter spilled hot tea on him.\" Which kind we make matters, because it shapes how we judge, blame, help or punish people. Harold Kelley's covariation model later described the information (consensus, distinctiveness and consistency) we use to choose between the two. Option c is right because the stem defines exactly this: assigning the cause to an internal characteristic of the person rather than to outside forces. The exam trap is option b, situational attribution, which is the direct opposite; remember that \"disposition\" means something inside the person, while \"situation\" means something around the person.", "traps": {"a": "Social desirability is the tendency to present oneself in a socially approved way (a response bias in tests); in Jones and Davis's correspondent inference theory, an act low in social desirability makes a dispositional inference more likely, but it is not the name of the attribution itself.", "b": "Situational attribution is the opposite: the cause is placed in external forces such as the task, other people or luck, not in the person.", "d": "\"Incidental behaviour\" is not a technical term in attribution theory; it is a distractor."}}, {"q": "The ability of the therapist to share the client's emotions is an important process known as:", "concept": "The answer is b, empathy. Empathy is the therapist's ability to sense and understand the client's feelings and personal meanings as if they were the therapist's own, while never losing that \"as if\" quality. Carl Rogers made it central to client-centred (person-centred) therapy. In his 1957 paper on the \"necessary and sufficient conditions\" of therapeutic change he named three therapist conditions: accurate empathic understanding, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). For example, when a client says \"Nobody at home even notices me\", an empathic therapist grasps the loneliness and hurt behind the words and conveys that understanding, instead of judging or giving advice. Meta-analyses of psychotherapy research find that therapist empathy is reliably related to better outcome. Option b is right because the stem asks for the therapist's ability to share and understand the client's emotions, which is the definition of empathy. The exam trap is to confuse empathy with sympathy or with reflection: sympathy is feeling sorry for the client from the outside, and reflection is a technique (restating feelings) through which empathy is communicated, while empathy is the underlying understanding itself.", "traps": {"a": "Reflection (of feeling or content) is a counselling technique of mirroring back what the client said or felt; it is one way of showing empathy, not the ability itself.", "c": "Catharsis is the release of pent-up emotion by expressing it, the basis of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's early cathartic method; it happens in the client and is not the therapist sharing feelings.", "d": "Transference is the client's unconscious redirection of feelings from past significant figures (such as a parent) onto the therapist; it is a psychoanalytic process in the client, not the therapist's understanding."}}, {"concept": "The answer is b, Carl Jung. The figure shows Jung's three-layer model of the psyche from his analytical psychology. Carl Gustav Jung, a Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud around 1913, described an outer layer of consciousness organised by the ego; beneath it the personal unconscious, holding forgotten or repressed material grouped into emotionally charged \"complexes\"; and at the deepest level the collective unconscious, an inherited store of experience shared by all humankind. The contents of the collective unconscious are archetypes, universal predispositions to perceive and react in certain ways. The figure names four of them: the persona (the social mask we show to others), the shadow (the dark, animal side we deny in ourselves), the anima (the feminine side of a man) and the animus (the masculine side of a woman). Jung's central archetype, the self, strives for wholeness through individuation. Option b is right because the collective unconscious and the archetypes are uniquely Jungian; no other theorist in the options uses them. The exam trap is option a: Freud also layered the mind (conscious, preconscious and unconscious, drawn as an iceberg, plus id, ego and superego), but Freud's unconscious is personal, built from one's own repressed wishes, with no collective layer or archetypes.", "traps": {"a": "Freud's topographic model (conscious, preconscious, unconscious) and structural model (id, ego, superego) are usually drawn as an iceberg; it has no collective unconscious, persona, shadow, anima or animus.", "c": "Hans Eysenck's theory is a hierarchical trait-type model built by factor analysis, with the superfactors extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism; it is not a model of conscious and unconscious layers.", "d": "Erik Erikson's theory is a sequence of eight psychosocial stages across the life span (trust versus mistrust to integrity versus despair), drawn as a stage chart, not as layers of the psyche."}}, {"q": "The predisposition of an individual to evaluate some symbol or object or aspect of his world in a favourable or unfavourable manner is defined as:", "concept": "The answer is d, attitudes. An attitude is a learned, relatively lasting evaluation of an \"attitude object\", which can be a person, group, idea, symbol or thing, as favourable or unfavourable. The wording in the stem is Daniel Katz's classic 1960 definition: an attitude is the predisposition of the individual to evaluate some symbol or object or aspect of his world in a favourable or unfavourable manner. Katz also proposed that attitudes serve four functions: utilitarian (adjustive), ego-defensive, value-expressive and knowledge. Attitudes are commonly described by the ABC model with three components: affective (how you feel about the object), behavioural (how you tend to act toward it) and cognitive (what you believe about it). For example, a student who enjoys yoga, believes it reduces stress and attends classes regularly holds a favourable attitude toward yoga. Option d is right because the key words \"predisposition\" and \"evaluate in a favourable or unfavourable manner\" are the defining features of an attitude. The exam trap is to choose a thinking-process term such as heuristics or bias: those describe how we think or where we go wrong, while an attitude is an evaluative stance toward a particular object.", "traps": {"a": "Judgmental heuristics are mental shortcuts for quick judgements, such as availability and representativeness, described by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman; they are ways of thinking, not evaluative predispositions toward objects.", "b": "A cognitive bias is a systematic error in thinking or judgement (such as confirmation bias); it may distort attitudes but is not the definition of an attitude.", "c": "\"Social prediction\" (forecasting how people will behave) is not the defined term for an evaluative predisposition; it is a distractor."}}, {"q": "Uncompleted or interrupted tasks are remembered better than completed ones. This is which type of memory bias?", "concept": "The answer is c, the Zeigarnik effect. This is the tendency to remember tasks that were interrupted or left unfinished better than tasks that were completed. It was demonstrated by Bluma Zeigarnik, a student of Kurt Lewin in Berlin, in work published in 1927. The story goes that Lewin noticed a waiter who remembered every detail of unpaid orders but forgot them once the bill was settled. Zeigarnik gave participants a series of simple tasks (puzzles, modelling, arithmetic) and interrupted some before they were finished; later, people recalled the interrupted tasks more often than the completed ones. Lewin's field theory explained this as a \"tension system\": starting a goal creates psychological tension that keeps the task active in memory until the goal is reached, and finishing releases it. A familiar example is an unanswered question that keeps returning to your mind. Option c is right because the stem defines exactly the Zeigarnik effect: better memory for uncompleted or interrupted tasks. The exam trap is the Von Restorff effect, which is also a memory-advantage effect but is about distinctiveness (an item that stands out from the rest is remembered better), whereas Zeigarnik is about incompleteness.", "traps": {"a": "The Von Restorff (isolation) effect, described by Hedwig von Restorff in 1933, is better memory for an item that stands out from the others in a list (for example one red word among black ones), not for unfinished tasks.", "b": "The primacy effect is better recall of items presented at the beginning of a list (part of the serial position curve), explained by extra rehearsal and transfer to long-term memory.", "d": "The modality effect is better recall of the last items of a list when it is heard rather than read, because auditory traces last longer; it concerns the mode of presentation, not task completion."}}, {"q": "When we explain the behavior of others, we look for enduring internal attributions, such as personality traits. This is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a, the fundamental attribution error. This is our tendency, when explaining other people's behaviour, to overestimate internal (dispositional) causes such as personality traits and to underestimate the influence of the situation. Lee Ross named it in 1977; it is also called the correspondence bias. The classic demonstration is Edward Jones and Victor Harris's 1967 study: students read essays for or against Fidel Castro and were told the writer had been assigned the position, yet they still judged that the writer really held the view expressed. In daily life, if a driver cuts in front of you, you think \"what a reckless person\" and do not consider that he may be rushing to a hospital. One explanation is that the person is the most noticeable thing we see, while situational pressures are invisible. The bias is weaker in collectivist cultures, which pay more attention to context. Option a is right because the stem describes looking for enduring internal causes such as traits when explaining others' behaviour. The exam trap is the actor-observer effect: the fundamental attribution error is about how we explain others, while the actor-observer effect is the contrast between explaining others by their dispositions and explaining our own behaviour by the situation.", "traps": {"b": "Selection bias is a research-design error in which the groups compared differ systematically because of how participants were chosen or assigned; it is not a way of explaining behaviour.", "c": "\"Cognitive errors generalization\" is not a standard term; overgeneralization is one of Aaron Beck's cognitive distortions (drawing a broad conclusion from one event), which is different from attributing behaviour to traits.", "d": "Sampling bias occurs when a sample does not represent its population (for example the 1936 Literary Digest poll); it is a methodological problem, not an attribution bias."}}, {"q": "Which one of the statements is not correct?", "concept": "The answer is d, because it is the only incorrect statement. Harold Kelley's covariation model (1967) says we decide whether a behaviour has an internal or an external cause by checking three kinds of information. Consensus asks whether other people behave the same way in the same situation. Distinctiveness asks whether this person behaves this way only in this situation or with this stimulus, or in many situations. Consistency asks whether the person behaves this way in this situation repeatedly over time. Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency lead to an internal attribution (\"it is something about Aditi\"); high consensus, high distinctiveness and high consistency lead to an external attribution (\"it is the situation\"). Applying this: if Aditi's friend also smokes, consensus is high (a); if only Aditi smokes, consensus is low (b); if she smokes only when out with friends, distinctiveness is high (c). Option d is right as the answer because its second half is wrong: smoking at any time or place does mean low distinctiveness, but it says nothing about whether other people smoke, so it cannot make consensus high. The exam trap is mixing the three up: consensus is always about other people, distinctiveness is about the same person across different situations, and consistency is about the same person in the same situation over time.", "traps": {"a": "This statement is correct, so it is not the answer: consensus concerns other people, and if her friend smokes in the same situation, consensus is high.", "b": "This statement is correct, so it is not the answer: if Aditi is the only one who smokes, others do not share the behaviour, so consensus is low.", "c": "This statement is correct, so it is not the answer: smoking only in one situation (out with friends) and not elsewhere makes the behaviour specific to that situation, which is high distinctiveness."}}, {"q": "Progressive series of changes that occur in an orderly predictable pattern as a result of maturation and experience in a human being was proposed in:", "concept": "The answer is b, Elizabeth Hurlock's life-span development theory. Elizabeth B. Hurlock was an American developmental psychologist whose textbooks, Child Development and Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach, are standard reading in Indian courses. She defined development as a progressive series of orderly, coherent changes resulting from maturation and experience. \"Progressive\" means the changes are directional, leading forward rather than backward; \"orderly\" and \"coherent\" mean each stage builds on the one before in a predictable pattern; \"maturation and experience\" means development is the joint product of biological unfolding and learning. Hurlock also listed principles of development: early foundations are critical, the pattern is predictable, all individuals are different, each period has characteristic behaviour and social expectations (developmental tasks), and each stage carries potential hazards. She traced development across the whole life span, from the prenatal period to old age. For example, babies sit before they stand and stand before they walk in every culture, though the exact ages differ between children. Option b is right because the stem quotes Hurlock's own definition of development. The exam trap is to pick a famous stage theorist: Freud, Kohlberg and Erikson each describe one domain of development, not a general definition of development itself.", "traps": {"a": "Freud's psychosexual theory describes how libido shifts across body zones (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital); it explains personality formation, not development in general.", "c": "Kohlberg's theory describes three levels and six stages of moral reasoning, based on answers to dilemmas such as Heinz's; it is limited to the moral domain.", "d": "Erikson's theory covers the life span, but it describes eight psychosocial crises of ego identity and relationships, not the general definition of development quoted in the stem."}}, {"q": "The author Gilligan in her book \"In a Different Voice: Psychological Theory and Women's Development\" emphasizes on:", "concept": "The answer is c: women are not deficient but different in their moral reasoning. Carol Gilligan, an American psychologist who worked with Lawrence Kohlberg at Harvard, published In a Different Voice in 1982. She criticised Kohlberg's stage theory because it was built on interviews with boys and men and treated an abstract \"ethic of justice\" (rights, rules, fairness) as the highest form of morality. When women were scored on Kohlberg's scale they often landed at Stage 3 (the \"good boy, good girl\" stage), which made them look morally less developed. Gilligan argued that many women reason from an \"ethic of care\", which stresses relationships, responsibility and avoiding harm. From interviews, including with women deciding about abortion, she proposed three levels: concern for individual survival, goodness as self-sacrifice, and a morality of nonviolence that balances care for self and others. Option c is right because the book's central claim is that women speak in a different moral voice, not a weaker one. The exam trap is option a: Gilligan did describe women suppressing their own needs, but only as the middle \"self-sacrifice\" level to be outgrown, not as her main thesis. Note also that later research finds only small gender differences in care versus justice reasoning.", "traps": {"a": "This misstates Gilligan: self-silencing appears only in her middle level (goodness as self-sacrifice), which mature care reasoning moves beyond; her thesis is that theory overlooked women's voice, not that women choose to suppress it.", "b": "Gilligan did not explain gender differences by how permissive a society is; she argued that theories built on male samples mislabelled women's care-based reasoning as inferior.", "d": "Gilligan did not argue from biology; she located the difference in how relationships and identity develop, so a biological-determinist claim is not her emphasis."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statement is false about Piaget's concept development theory?", "concept": "The answer is a, because it is the false statement. Jean Piaget, the Swiss developmental psychologist, described the child as an active builder of knowledge. The basic units of knowledge are schemas (schemata): organised patterns of action or thought that a child uses to understand and interpret experience, such as a sucking schema in infancy or a \"dog\" schema later. Schemas grow through adaptation, which has two complementary processes. Assimilation is taking in new information by fitting it into an existing schema (calling a new dog \"dog\"). Accommodation is changing an existing schema, or creating a new one, when information does not fit (learning that a cow is not a dog). Equilibration is the self-regulating drive to restore balance between assimilation and accommodation; when new experience creates a mismatch (disequilibrium), the child is pushed to a higher level of understanding. Option a is right as the answer because schemas are internal frameworks for organising and interpreting information for oneself, not models for spreading or \"disseminating\" information to others. The exam trap is to hunt for small wording flaws in b, c and d; they are standard textbook definitions, and the error to spot is the wrong purpose given to schemas in a.", "traps": {"b": "This is true: assimilation means interpreting new information in terms of what we already know, fitting it into an existing schema.", "c": "This is true: accommodation means modifying a schema (or forming a new one) when new information does not fit.", "d": "This is true: Piaget called the balancing of assimilation and accommodation equilibration and saw it as the motor of cognitive growth."}}, {"q": "Which one of the followings is an example of assimilation?", "concept": "The answer is a. In Jean Piaget's theory a schema is an organised mental framework for a category of experience. Assimilation means taking new experience into an existing schema without changing the schema; accommodation means changing the schema because the new experience does not fit. Imagine a child whose \"dog\" schema comes from the family's golden retriever: four legs, furry, barks, friendly. When the family adopts two more golden retrievers, they match the existing schema perfectly, so the child simply applies the \"dog\" schema to them. The schema now covers more animals, but its rules are unchanged; that is assimilation. If the same child then sees a sheep and calls it \"dog\", and the parent says \"no, that is a sheep\", the child must modify the schema so that it excludes sheep and foxes; that is accommodation. Option a is right because the new golden retrievers fit the existing schema and are absorbed into it unchanged, the textbook definition of assimilation. The exam trap is the word \"expanded\" in option a, which tempts students toward accommodation; what matters is whether the schema's rules change (accommodation) or the same schema is simply applied to more cases (assimilation).", "traps": {"b": "Adjusting the dog schema to exclude sheep and foxes changes the schema itself, which is accommodation, not assimilation.", "c": "Balancing two schemas to reach a stable understanding describes equilibration, Piaget's process that regulates assimilation and accommodation.", "d": "A dog schema that fails to differentiate from cow is an undifferentiated, overgeneralised schema, showing that accommodation has not yet happened; a child calling a cow \"doggie\" is assimilating, but the option describes the faulty state of the schema, so a is the clean example."}}, {"q": "In Piaget vs Vygotsky argument which of the followings is/are true?", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c (both A and B). Statement B oversimplifies Lev Vygotsky, who held that thought and speech have separate roots but merge at about age two into verbal thought, so they do not stay separate; the setter clearly accepts B's contrast as true, so answer c in the exam. Jean Piaget saw the child as building knowledge through his or her own actions; development is driven largely from within, by maturation and equilibration, and learning follows development. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist, stressed that higher mental functions are social in origin: they arise through interaction with more skilled people and through cultural tools such as language, as in his zone of proximal development (what a child can do with help but not yet alone). On language, Piaget held that thought comes first and speech reflects it, so egocentric speech simply mirrors egocentric thought and fades away. Vygotsky argued that early thought and early speech develop separately, then join, and that private speech turns inward as inner speech that guides thinking. Option c is right because the key accepts both A (internal versus external emphasis) and B (thought first versus separate origins). The exam trap is egocentric speech: for Piaget it shows immature thought, for Vygotsky it is a self-guidance tool that becomes inner speech.", "traps": {"a": "Statement A is a correct contrast, but option a leaves out B, which the official key accepts; a is only defensible if you read B as claiming that thought and speech stay separate for life.", "b": "Statement B alone is incomplete, because statement A (Piaget's emphasis on internal factors, Vygotsky's on social and cultural ones) is also a standard, accepted contrast.", "d": "Neither statement is rejected by the key: A is the textbook contrast between the two theorists, so \"neither\" cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Who amongst the followings for the first time distinguished psychology from biology and philosophy and is considered as father of modern psychology?", "concept": "The answer is b, Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt. Before Wundt, questions about the mind were handled by philosophers (through reasoning) and by physiologists (through studies of nerves and senses). Wundt, a German physician and physiologist, argued that the mind could be studied as a separate experimental science. His book Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) set out the programme, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig he founded the first laboratory devoted to psychological research, which is why 1879 is often given as the birth year of psychology as an independent science. He studied immediate conscious experience, such as sensations, feelings and reaction times, using controlled introspection, founded the first psychology journal and trained many students who spread psychology worldwide, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin. Option b is right because Wundt is the person credited with separating psychology from philosophy and physiology (the \"biology\" of the stem), and he is called the father of modern or experimental psychology. The exam trap is to choose the founder of a later school: John B. Watson founded behaviourism and Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, but both worked after psychology already existed as a science.", "traps": {"a": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism in 1913, redefining psychology as the science of observable behaviour; he did not separate psychology from philosophy in the first place.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov was a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for work on digestion and discovered classical conditioning; he saw himself as a physiologist, not as the founder of psychology.", "d": "Albert Bandura developed social learning (later social cognitive) theory and the Bobo doll studies in the 1960s, long after psychology became a science."}}, {"q": "Which of the following learning theories states that behavior is based on how one interacts with and is influenced by their environment?", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c, social learning theory. The stem's wording also fits behavioural learning theory (behaviourism), which holds that behaviour is shaped by the environment, so b is a reasonable reading; in the exam answer c, taking \"interacts with\" as the cue for Bandura's two-way interaction. Social learning theory was developed by Albert Bandura from the 1960s and later renamed social cognitive theory. It holds that people learn by observing others in their social environment (observational learning or modelling), and that such learning depends on attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Bandura's key idea is reciprocal determinism: behaviour, personal-cognitive factors (beliefs, expectations, self-efficacy) and the environment all influence one another. A child who sees an older sibling praised for tidying up may start tidying; the child's tidying then changes how the parents respond, which further shapes the child. Option c is right under the key because social learning theory describes behaviour as the product of continuous interaction with, and influence from, the social environment. The exam trap is behaviourism: in strict behaviourism (John B. Watson, B. F. Skinner) influence runs one way, from environmental consequences to behaviour, with no role for thought, whereas Bandura's theory makes it two-way and includes cognition.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive learning theory focuses on internal mental processes such as insight, memory and information processing (for example Wolfgang Köhler's insight learning or Edward Tolman's cognitive maps), not on person-environment interaction as such.", "b": "Behavioural learning theory (Watson, Skinner) does say behaviour is shaped by the environment, which makes it a strong distractor, but it treats influence as one-way conditioning and ignores thought and observation of others.", "d": "Constructivist learning theory (Jean Piaget, and in its social form Lev Vygotsky) holds that learners actively build their own knowledge through experience; it is about constructing knowledge, not behaviour shaped by the environment."}}, {"q": "Which of the followings proved that learning occurs through association?", "concept": "The answer is c, classical conditioning. Classical conditioning is learning in which a neutral stimulus comes to produce a response after being repeatedly paired with a stimulus that already produces that response. It was discovered by Ivan Pavlov, a Russian physiologist, around the turn of the 20th century while he was studying digestion in dogs. Food in the mouth (unconditioned stimulus, UCS) naturally produced salivation (unconditioned response, UCR). When a neutral sound such as a metronome or bell (conditioned stimulus, CS) was presented just before the food again and again, the sound alone came to produce salivation (conditioned response, CR). Pavlov thus showed experimentally that an organism learns an association between two stimuli, giving the philosophers' old laws of association (contiguity, repetition) a laboratory method. In daily life, feeling anxious at the smell of a hospital after a painful treatment is a conditioned association. Option c is right because Pavlov's classical conditioning is the classic demonstration that learning occurs through association, specifically stimulus-stimulus association. The exam trap is operant conditioning: it is also associative, but the association is between a response and its consequence (reinforcement or punishment), and its classic names are Edward Thorndike and B. F. Skinner, not Pavlov.", "traps": {"a": "\"Proximal conditioning\" is not a recognised type of conditioning; the word may remind you of contiguity (closeness in time), but it is a distractor.", "b": "Instrumental conditioning (Edward Thorndike's puzzle-box cats and the law of effect) is learning from consequences, a response-outcome association; it is not the classic answer for learning through association.", "d": "Operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner) is the same family as instrumental conditioning, behaviour strengthened or weakened by its consequences; since b and d are near-synonyms, neither can be the single best answer here."}}, {"q": "Which is not a correct statement about Albert Bandura's social learning theory?", "concept": "The answer is d, because it is the incorrect statement. Albert Bandura's social learning theory holds that people learn by watching others (models) and the consequences of their actions, without having to perform the behaviour or be reinforced themselves. A central point is the distinction between learning (acquiring the knowledge or ability to perform a behaviour) and performance (actually doing it). In Bandura's 1965 Bobo doll study, children watched an adult model hit an inflatable Bobo doll and then saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Children who saw the model punished imitated less aggression. But when all the children were later offered rewards for showing what the model had done, they reproduced the aggressive acts about equally well. The behaviour had been learned by everyone; whether it was performed depended on expected consequences. Bandura also described reciprocal determinism, the two-way interaction of social, cognitive and environmental influences. Option d is right as the answer because Bandura specifically showed that learning can occur without any change in observable behaviour. The exam trap is to think like a behaviourist: behaviourists defined learning as a change in behaviour, while Bandura showed learning can stay hidden until there is a reason to perform it.", "traps": {"a": "This is true: observation of models is the core mechanism of social learning theory.", "b": "This is treated as true: observational learning can happen at any time and in any setting, from live models, verbal instructions or symbolic models such as television.", "c": "This is true: it describes reciprocal determinism, the two-way interaction between personal-cognitive, behavioural and environmental factors."}}, {"q": "Which statement is false about the difference between temperament and personality?", "concept": "The answer is d, because it is the false statement. Temperament refers to biologically based, early-appearing individual differences in the energy, speed, intensity and emotional reactivity of behaviour. Gordon Allport treated temperament as part of the inherited \"raw material\" from which personality is built. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess's New York Longitudinal Study classified infants as easy, difficult or slow-to-warm-up, and the Polish psychologist Jan Strelau described temperament as the formal (energetic and temporal) side of behaviour, seen even in animals. Personality is the broader, distinctly human organisation of traits, motives, values, attitudes and identity, shaped by culture, learning and life experience. Because temperament is rooted in biology, it is relatively stable across the life span, while personality content such as values and attitudes is more open to change, for example after dramatic life events. Option d is right as the answer because it calls temperament \"relatively unstable\" over the life course, which reverses the standard finding; its second half about personality change is accurate. The exam trap is the long, technical wording of a, b and c, which looks suspicious but is correct; read each option for the single factual reversal, here \"unstable\" where it should say \"stable\".", "traps": {"a": "This is true: temperament concerns neurochemically based behavioural regulation seen in animals, infants and adults, while personality is a human concept shaped by socio-cultural factors.", "b": "This is true: temperament traits have a documented biological basis, and some of them also appear in structural models of personality, viewed there through a socio-cultural lens.", "c": "This is true: temperament is defined by formal-dynamical features of behaviour (energy, tempo) that are consistent across situations, while personality traits reflect content shaped by socio-cultural appraisal."}}, {"q": "Where was the first laboratory ever to be exclusively devoted to psychological studies?", "concept": "The answer is c, Leipzig, Germany. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt established a laboratory at the University of Leipzig devoted exclusively to psychological research, which is why 1879 is treated as the formal start of psychology as an independent experimental science. Wundt's laboratory measured reaction times, sensations, perception and attention under controlled conditions, using trained introspection, and it attracted students from across Europe and America who then set up laboratories of their own. Before Leipzig, Wundt had worked at Heidelberg (including as an assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz) and had spent about a year at Zurich. William James had a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard around 1875, but it was used for teaching rather than research, so Leipzig is credited as the first true psychological laboratory. Option c is right because Leipzig is where the first laboratory exclusively for psychological studies was founded. The exam trap is that the other cities are also linked to famous names: Heidelberg and Zurich were earlier stops in Wundt's own career, and Vienna is Freud's city, so fix the triple \"Wundt, Leipzig, 1879\" firmly in memory.", "traps": {"a": "Vienna, Austria is where Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis; it was not the site of the first psychology laboratory.", "b": "Zurich, Switzerland is linked with Carl Jung, Eugen Bleuler and the Burghölzli hospital; Wundt taught there briefly before Leipzig but did not found his laboratory there.", "d": "Heidelberg, Germany is where Wundt worked earlier, including as Hermann von Helmholtz's assistant; the laboratory came only after he moved to Leipzig in 1875."}}, {"q": "Mr. R, 26 years old, complains of extremely fearful thoughts about something bad that will happen to him, without any apparent reason, and these thoughts keep coming back every now and then. He also complains that he is not able to stop these thoughts and spends hours together trying to control them. He stopped going out of the house because of these fearful thoughts. Mr. R most likely has:", "concept": "The answer is b, obsessive disorder. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, images or urges that are experienced as intrusive and unwanted and that usually cause marked anxiety or distress. The person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them, often with compulsions, and recognises them as coming from his or her own mind (unlike thought insertion in psychosis). DSM-5-TR places obsessive-compulsive disorder in its own chapter and requires that the symptoms be time-consuming (for example more than an hour a day) or cause significant distress or impairment; ICD-10 even had a subtype for predominantly obsessional thoughts or ruminations without compulsions. Mr R shows each feature: fearful thoughts that something bad will happen, coming back again and again without reason, which he cannot stop and spends hours trying to control, to the point that he avoids leaving home. Option b is right because the vignette stresses intrusion, recurrence, failed resistance and hours spent fighting the thoughts, the hallmark of obsessions. The exam trap is generalized anxiety: GAD worry is excessive concern about real-life matters (health, money, family) that feels like ordinary, if exaggerated, worrying, whereas an obsession is felt as an alien, unwanted intrusion that the person actively struggles against.", "traps": {"a": "A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation (such as dogs, heights or crowds) with avoidance of it; Mr R fears an unspecified future event, not a specific stimulus.", "c": "\"Anxiety disorder\" is a broad umbrella (OCD itself sat with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV-TR, until DSM-5 gave it its own chapter in 2013), but the most specific diagnosis for recurrent intrusive thoughts he struggles to stop is obsessive disorder.", "d": "A panic reaction is a sudden, discrete surge of intense fear with bodily symptoms (palpitations, breathlessness) that peaks within minutes; Mr R describes persistent recurring thoughts, not attacks."}}, {"q": "Which statement is true about American Psychological Association (APA)?", "concept": "The answer is c. The American Psychological Association (APA), founded in 1892 with G. Stanley Hall as its first president, is the largest professional body of psychologists in the United States. It is organised into divisions, each representing a subfield or interest area. The division numbers run from 1 to 56, but two numbers (4 and 11) are vacant, so there are 54 active divisions; exam questions and many textbooks simply say \"56 divisions\". Division 12 is the Society of Clinical Psychology, the home of clinical psychologists, which promotes clinical research, training and practice and is known for its lists of empirically supported treatments. Clinical psychology itself began when Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term \"clinical psychology\". Other well-known divisions include Division 15 (Educational Psychology), Division 17 (Counseling Psychology) and Division 39 (Psychoanalysis). Option c is right because it is the only option that pairs the usual division count (56) with the correct clinical division (12). The exam trap is the 56 versus 54 detail: an option saying 54 divisions with Division 12 as clinical would also be correct; what never changes is that Division 12 is clinical psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Division 3 is the Society for Experimental Psychology and Cognitive Science, and APA has far more than 36 divisions.", "b": "Division 15 is Educational Psychology, and the count of 41 divisions is wrong.", "d": "Division 4 is one of the two vacant numbers (no active division), and 27 divisions is far too few."}}, {"concept": "The answer is c, the extinction and spontaneous recovery curve of bad behaviour. In operant terms, extinction means stopping the reinforcement that has been maintaining a behaviour, so the behaviour gradually weakens. The graph shows its typical course for an inappropriate behaviour. During baseline the behaviour is frequent. When treatment begins (withholding reinforcement, for example no longer giving attention for a child's tantrums), there is first an extinction burst: a short-lived increase in the frequency or intensity of the behaviour, as if the child is testing whether it still works. Then the behaviour falls steeply. Later it may reappear for a while, the \"rebound effects\" on the graph, which corresponds to spontaneous recovery, a phenomenon first described by Ivan Pavlov. If the caregiver stays consistent, the behaviour settles at a low, stable level (\"treatment takes\"). If the caregiver gives in during the burst or the rebound, the behaviour is intermittently reinforced, becomes more resistant to extinction and can end up worse than at baseline, which is the dotted line. Option c is right because the burst, fall and rebound labelled on the graph are the signature of extinction and spontaneous recovery. The exam trap is option d: \"regression\" sounds right, but the graph is not a learning curve; it plots a problem behaviour being extinguished.", "traps": {"a": "A curve of diminishing intervention effects would show improvement fading back toward baseline; the graph's main line instead shows a burst, a steep fall, a rebound and then stable improvement.", "b": "An S-shaped (sigmoid) learning curve shows a skill rising slowly, then quickly, then levelling off; this graph plots a problem behaviour that falls, not a proficiency that rises.", "d": "\"Steep learning and regression\" borrows labels from the graph, but the graph is not a learning curve; the burst and rebound are features specific to extinction and spontaneous recovery."}}, {"q": "Verbal declarative memory deficits is found more in:", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is b, PTSD with comorbid depression. Verbal memory is also one of the largest cognitive deficits in first-episode schizophrenia, so a is defensible if \"more\" means size of deficit; the item comes from PTSD memory research, so answer b in the exam. Declarative (explicit) memory is memory for facts and events that can be consciously recalled; verbal declarative memory is tested by word-list or story recall, for example the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test. Research on posttraumatic stress disorder repeatedly finds poorer learning and recall of emotionally neutral verbal material; a meta-analysis by Chris Brewin and colleagues (2007) found the deficit is larger for verbal than for visual memory. Some studies found the deficit is greater when PTSD is accompanied by major depression, which itself impairs memory. A common explanation involves the hippocampus, which is vital for declarative memory and sensitive to stress hormones; several PTSD studies report smaller hippocampal volume. Option b is right under the key because PTSD with comorbid depression is the group in which this specific deficit has been highlighted. The exam trap is option c: studies of Holocaust survivors by Rachel Yehuda's group linked verbal memory deficits to PTSD, not to generalized anxiety, so a trauma history alone does not make an option correct.", "traps": {"a": "First-episode schizophrenia does show marked verbal memory deficits (often among its largest cognitive impairments), which is why the item is debatable; the key, however, targets the PTSD with depression finding.", "c": "Holocaust-survivor studies tied verbal memory deficits to PTSD, not to generalized anxiety disorder, and GAD is not characterised by a specific declarative memory deficit.", "d": "Alcohol-induced psychosis (mainly hallucinations or delusions linked to heavy drinking) is not the condition associated with this finding; the classic alcohol-related memory disorder is Korsakoff's syndrome, which is a different condition."}}, {"q": "The ability to use one's knowledge of ocean tides to navigate unfamiliar seas is describing the concept of:", "concept": "The answer is a, crystallized intelligence. Raymond Cattell, later working with John Horn, divided general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on prior learning, such as completing abstract patterns in Raven's Progressive Matrices; it peaks in early adulthood and then declines. Crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills acquired through education and experience, together with the ability to apply it; it keeps growing or stays stable into late adulthood. Both are now part of the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model. In the stem, knowledge of ocean tides is learned, stored information, and navigating with it is applying that knowledge, which is the essence of crystallized intelligence; an experienced old sailor would be expected to do this better than a bright beginner. Option a is right because the key phrase is \"use one's knowledge\", the hallmark of Gc. The exam trap is the phrase \"unfamiliar seas\", which suggests novelty and pulls students toward fluid intelligence; ask whether the person is reasoning from scratch (fluid) or applying what they already know (crystallized), and here the stored knowledge does the work.", "traps": {"b": "Fluid intelligence is reasoning and problem solving in novel situations without relying on stored knowledge; \"unfamiliar seas\" hints at it, but the stem says the person uses existing knowledge of tides.", "c": "Episodic memory (Endel Tulving's term) is memory for personally experienced events tied to a time and place, such as recalling one's last voyage; the stem is about applying general knowledge, not recalling an episode.", "d": "Procedural memory is memory for how to perform skills, such as steering or rowing, shown in action rather than recalled; knowledge of tides is factual knowledge, not a motor skill."}}, {"q": "When we look out of the window of a train, nearby things pass very quickly, while far off objects appear stationary. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is b, motion parallax. Depth perception, the ability to see the world in three dimensions and judge distance, relies on binocular cues (needing two eyes, such as retinal disparity and convergence) and monocular cues (available to one eye). Motion parallax is a monocular, movement-produced cue: when you move, objects at different distances move across your visual field at different speeds and in different directions. Objects near you seem to rush past quickly in the direction opposite to your movement, while distant objects seem to move slowly, to stand almost still, or even to travel along with you. The brain uses these differences in relative speed to judge which objects are near and which are far. The train window is the textbook example: nearby poles and trees flash by, while distant hills and the moon seem to stay still. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this difference in apparent motion between near and far objects seen by a moving observer. The exam trap is option d: depth perception is the general ability, while motion parallax is one specific cue for it; when a question describes a particular mechanism, choose the specific cue, not the umbrella term.", "traps": {"a": "The phi phenomenon, described by Max Wertheimer in 1912, is apparent motion seen when stationary lights flash on and off in sequence (as in neon signs); it is an illusion of movement, not a depth cue from real movement.", "c": "\"Kinetic illusion\" is not the standard name for this effect; the similar-sounding kinetic depth effect is seeing three-dimensional shape from the moving shadow of a rotating object, a different phenomenon.", "d": "Depth perception is the overall ability to perceive distance and three dimensions; motion parallax is one of the cues that make it possible, so d is too general."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following problem-solving strategies, though time consuming, guarantees solution?", "concept": "The answer is b, use of an algorithm. An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that, if followed correctly, is guaranteed to produce the solution, because it systematically checks every possibility or applies a proven rule. Examples are a mathematical formula, or trying every combination of a three-digit lock (000 to 999) until it opens. The drawback is that algorithms can be very slow, sometimes impractical, when the number of possibilities is large. Heuristics, studied in problem solving by Allen Newell and Herbert Simon and in judgement by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, are rules of thumb or mental shortcuts (such as working backward, means-end analysis or breaking a problem into subgoals) that are fast and often successful but do not guarantee a solution. In daily life, searching for lost keys by checking every room in order is algorithmic; checking only the places you usually leave them is heuristic. Option b is right because the stem pairs \"time consuming\" with \"guarantees solution\", the defining trade-off of an algorithm. The exam trap is trial and error: it also feels slow and often succeeds in the end, but because it is unsystematic it may repeat attempts or miss options, so it does not guarantee a solution.", "traps": {"a": "Trial and error (as in Edward Thorndike's puzzle-box cats) is unsystematic trying until something works; it can be slow and still offers no guarantee.", "c": "Heuristics are shortcuts that are quick and often work, but by definition they can fail, so they do not guarantee a solution.", "d": "Studying the problem in parts (subgoal or means-end analysis) is itself a heuristic strategy; it simplifies the problem but does not guarantee success."}}, {"q": "Who is regarded as the \"Father of Psychology\"?", "concept": "The answer is c, Wilhelm Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) was a German physician, physiologist and philosopher who made psychology a separate, experimental science. In 1879 he opened the first laboratory for psychological research at the University of Leipzig; he also wrote Principles of Physiological Psychology, founded the first psychology journal and trained a generation of psychologists. He studied the elements of conscious experience (sensations, feelings) through controlled introspection and measured reaction times, and his student Edward Titchener carried these ideas to America as structuralism. For these reasons he is called the father of psychology, or more precisely the father of experimental or modern psychology. Other \"fathers\" appear in exam lore: Aristotle is sometimes called the father of psychology for his early writing on the mind, William James the father of American psychology, and Sigmund Freud the father of psychoanalysis. Option c is right because, when a question simply says \"Father of Psychology\", the standard answer is Wundt, the founder of psychology as a science. The exam trap is option b: Freud is the psychologist best known to the public, but he is the father of psychoanalysis, one school within psychology, not of psychology itself.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy and led humanistic psychology (the \"third force\"); he is not the founder of psychology.", "b": "Sigmund Freud is the father of psychoanalysis, a theory and therapy built on the unconscious; psychology as a science began with Wundt before Freud's work became influential.", "d": "Ivan Pavlov was a Russian physiologist known for classical conditioning; he influenced behaviourism but did not found psychology."}}, {"q": "Where was the first psychology laboratory established?", "concept": "The answer is a, Leipzig. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt set up the first formal laboratory for experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in Germany. It is treated as the birthplace of scientific psychology because, for the first time, mental processes such as sensation, perception, attention, feeling and reaction time were studied with controlled experiments in a setting devoted to that purpose. The laboratory trained many of the first professional psychologists, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin, who carried its methods to America and into psychiatry. Other early landmarks are often confused with it: Francis Galton opened an anthropometric laboratory in London in 1884 to measure people's physical and sensory abilities, an early step in mental testing; G. Stanley Hall founded the first American psychology laboratory at Johns Hopkins University in 1883; and Lewis Terman at Stanford University in California published the Stanford-Binet test in 1916. Option a is right because Leipzig is the city where Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory. The exam trap is option c: London did have an early laboratory, Galton's, but it came five years later and measured individual differences rather than founding experimental psychology.", "traps": {"b": "Romania has no connection with the founding of the first psychology laboratory; it is a pure distractor.", "c": "London is linked with Francis Galton's anthropometric laboratory (1884), which came after Leipzig and focused on measuring individual differences.", "d": "California is linked with later American work such as Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet test (1916), long after 1879."}}, {"q": "Which school of thought in psychology emphasized the analysis of the basic elements of consciousness?", "concept": "The answer is c, structuralism. Structuralism was the first school of psychology. It aimed to analyse conscious experience into its basic elements, much as chemistry breaks compounds into elements, and to find the laws by which those elements combine. The ideas grew out of Wilhelm Wundt's laboratory work, but the school was named and systematised by his student Edward Bradford Titchener at Cornell University in the United States. Titchener proposed three basic elements of consciousness: sensations (elements of perception), images (elements of ideas) and affections (elements of feelings). The method was introspection by highly trained observers, who reported the raw sensory qualities of an experience rather than the meaning of the object; describing an apple as \"red, round, shiny\" rather than \"an apple\" avoided what Titchener called the stimulus error. Structuralism faded because introspection was unreliable and could not be checked by others, and because it could not study children, animals or abnormal minds. Option c is right because \"analysis of the basic elements of consciousness\" is the defining aim of structuralism. The exam trap is functionalism: both schools studied consciousness, but functionalism asked what consciousness is for (its adaptive purpose), while structuralism asked what it is made of.", "traps": {"a": "Functionalism (William James, John Dewey, James Rowland Angell), influenced by Charles Darwin, studied the purpose and adaptive value of consciousness rather than its elements.", "b": "The psychodynamic school (Sigmund Freud) emphasised unconscious motives and conflicts, not the analysis of conscious elements.", "d": "Humanism (Abraham Maslow, Carl Rogers) emphasised free will, personal growth and self-actualisation, not the elementary structure of consciousness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following does not make psychology a science?", "concept": "The answer is d, absolutism. Psychology is called a science because it studies behaviour and mental processes with the scientific method, and that method has recognisable features. Objectivity means observations and conclusions should not depend on the personal beliefs or feelings of the observer, so independent observers should agree. Empirical observation means claims are tested against evidence gathered through observation and measurement, not accepted on authority or reasoning alone. Falsifiability, the criterion proposed by the philosopher Karl Popper, means a scientific hypothesis must be stated so that some possible result could prove it wrong; \"everything people do is controlled by fate\" cannot be tested and so is not scientific. Other features are replicability, systematic procedures and tentativeness: scientific conclusions are provisional and open to revision when new evidence arrives. Absolutism is the opposite of this last feature. It treats a claim as final, certain and beyond question regardless of evidence, which is how dogma works, not science. Option d is right because absolutism contradicts the open, self-correcting nature of science, while the other three are core scientific features. The exam trap is to confuse absolutism with objectivity: objectivity is about unbiased observation, whereas absolutism is about refusing to accept that conclusions might change.", "traps": {"a": "Objectivity, meaning unbiased observation and agreement between independent observers, is a defining feature that makes psychology scientific.", "b": "Empirical observation, testing claims against measurable evidence, is the foundation of scientific psychology.", "c": "Falsifiability, Karl Popper's test that a hypothesis must be capable of being shown false, is a key criterion separating science from non-science."}}, {"q": "Who are the proponents of Humanism?", "concept": "The answer is b, Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow. Humanistic psychology arose in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s as the \"third force\", a reaction against both psychoanalysis (seen as too focused on unconscious, pathological drives) and behaviourism (seen as treating people like machines shaped by reinforcement). It stresses free will, personal responsibility, subjective experience and each person's natural tendency toward growth. Abraham Maslow proposed the hierarchy of needs (1943), rising from physiological and safety needs through love and belonging and esteem to self-actualisation, the full realisation of one's potential, and he studied self-actualising people. Carl Rogers developed client-centred (person-centred) therapy, built on the self-concept, the actualising tendency and the therapist conditions of empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence; he explained how \"conditions of worth\" create incongruence between self and experience. A Rogerian therapist, for example, does not interpret or direct but trusts the client to find his or her own solutions. Option b is right because Rogers and Maslow are the two founding figures of humanistic psychology. The exam trap is the half-right pairs: a includes Maslow and d includes Rogers, but each is paired with a non-humanist, so only b has both names correct.", "traps": {"a": "Maslow is a humanist, but Donald Meichenbaum is a cognitive-behavioural therapist known for self-instructional training and stress inoculation training.", "c": "Erik Erikson was a psychoanalytic ego psychologist (psychosocial stages) and Wilhelm Wundt founded experimental psychology; neither is a humanist.", "d": "Rogers is a humanist, but Erik Erikson belongs to the psychoanalytic (ego psychology) tradition, so the pair is only half correct."}}, {"q": "Pick the correct order of psychosexual stages of development.", "concept": "The answer is d: oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905) and later works, proposed that personality develops through stages in which the libido (pleasure-seeking energy) is focused on different body zones. Oral stage (birth to about 1 to 1.5 years): pleasure comes from the mouth through sucking and biting. Anal stage (about 1.5 to 3 years): pleasure centres on holding and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the key conflict. Phallic stage (about 3 to 5 or 6 years): focus on the genitals, with the Oedipus complex in boys (and what Carl Jung named the Electra complex in girls), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. Latency (about 6 years to puberty): sexual impulses are repressed and energy goes into school, skills and same-sex friendships; Freud saw it as a quiet period rather than a true stage. Genital stage (puberty onward): mature sexual interest in others. A handy mnemonic is \"Old Age Pensioners Love Grapes\". Option d is right because it is the only option with the full sequence in order. The exam trap is option a, which swaps phallic and latency; remember that latency is the quiet period that follows the Oedipal conflict.", "traps": {"a": "This swaps phallic and latency; latency follows the phallic stage, after the Oedipus complex is resolved.", "b": "This starts with anal and ends with latency; the oral stage comes first, and the genital stage (from puberty) is always last.", "c": "This is a scrambled order beginning with latency; latency is the fourth period, not the first."}}, {"q": "Classical experiment by John B. Watson demonstrating classical conditioning was on:", "concept": "The answer is d, Little Albert. In 1920 John B. Watson, the founder of behaviourism, and his assistant Rosalie Rayner published \"Conditioned Emotional Reactions\", showing that fear could be learned through classical conditioning. Albert was an infant of about nine months at first testing, who showed no fear of a white rat. During conditioning, at about 11 months, each time Albert reached for the rat the experimenters struck a steel bar with a hammer behind his head, making a loud noise. The noise was the unconditioned stimulus, startle and crying the unconditioned response, the rat the conditioned stimulus and fear of the rat the conditioned response. After several pairings Albert cried at the sight of the rat alone, and his fear generalised to similar furry objects such as a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat and a Santa Claus mask (stimulus generalisation). Albert's fear was never removed, and the study is now cited as an ethical breach; in 1924 Mary Cover Jones showed how such fears could be unlearned, with Little Peter. Option d is right because Little Albert is Watson's classic classical conditioning experiment. The exam trap is Little Hans: also a child with a phobia, but it is Sigmund Freud's 1909 psychoanalytic case, explained through the Oedipus complex rather than conditioning.", "traps": {"a": "Little Hans is Sigmund Freud's 1909 case of a five-year-old boy's fear of horses, interpreted psychoanalytically as Oedipal anxiety, not a conditioning experiment.", "b": "Stuart Little is a fictional mouse from E. B. White's children's book; it has no connection with Watson's research.", "c": "The Rat Man is another Freud case (1909), a young lawyer with obsessional neurosis; the rats were part of his obsessive fears, not a conditioning experiment."}}, {"q": "Which type of memory is responsible for the conscious recall of factual information, such as historical dates or capital cities?", "concept": "The answer is b, semantic memory. Endel Tulving, a Canadian psychologist, proposed in 1972 that long-term declarative (explicit) memory has two parts. Semantic memory holds general facts, meanings and concepts that are no longer tied to when or where you learned them: knowing that New Delhi is the capital of India, that the Battle of Plassey was fought in 1757, or that a triangle has three sides. Episodic memory holds personal events stamped with a time and place, such as your own tenth birthday or last night's dinner. Both are declarative because you can consciously bring them to mind and put them into words. In Larry Squire's taxonomy they sit under declarative memory, while non-declarative (implicit) memory covers procedural skills, priming and classically conditioned responses. Option b is right because the stem asks for conscious recall of factual knowledge such as dates and capitals, which is general world knowledge. The exam trap is episodic memory: both are explicit, so ask whether the memory is a fact anyone could know (semantic) or an event you personally lived through (episodic). \"I know Paris is in France\" is semantic; \"I remember my trip to Paris in 2019\" is episodic.", "traps": {"a": "Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits (riding a bicycle, typing), shown in performance rather than conscious recall, so it does not store facts like dates.", "c": "Episodic memory is explicit memory for personally experienced events tagged with a time and place; historical dates and capitals are general knowledge, not personal episodes.", "d": "Sensory memory is the very brief store of raw sensory input (under a second for iconic, a few seconds for echoic) in the Atkinson-Shiffrin model; it holds no lasting factual knowledge."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes Edward Thorndike's Law of Effect?", "concept": "The answer is a. Edward L. Thorndike, an American psychologist, studied hungry cats in puzzle boxes in the late 1890s (Animal Intelligence, 1898). A cat had to pull a loop or press a lever to escape and reach food. On early trials it struggled randomly; across trials its escape time fell gradually, which he called trial-and-error learning. He explained this with the Law of Effect: responses followed by a satisfying state of affairs are \"stamped in\" and become more likely in that situation, while responses followed by an annoying state of affairs are \"stamped out\". This was the forerunner of B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning and the modern idea of reinforcement. Thorndike also proposed the Law of Exercise (use and disuse) and the Law of Readiness. Option a is right because it states the core claim: favourable consequences make a behaviour more likely to be repeated. The exam trap is option c, which sounds operant and is true, but it is the principle of immediacy (temporal contiguity) of reinforcement, a later refinement, not the Law of Effect itself. Remember: the Law of Effect is about what the consequence is (satisfying or annoying), not when it arrives.", "traps": {"b": "This is Ivan Pavlov's classical conditioning, where a neutral stimulus gains power by pairing with an unconditioned stimulus; it concerns stimulus associations, not the consequences of behaviour.", "c": "This is the principle of immediacy of reinforcement; it is true, but it describes the timing of reinforcement rather than Thorndike's law that consequences decide whether a response is repeated.", "d": "This is Albert Bandura's observational (social) learning with vicarious reinforcement; Thorndike's cats learned from their own consequences, not by watching others."}}, {"q": "According to classical conditioning, which term describes a previously neutral stimulus that, after being paired with an unconditioned stimulus, triggers a conditioned response?", "concept": "The answer is b, the conditioned stimulus. Ivan Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began to salivate before food reached their mouths. In his classic procedure, food (the unconditioned stimulus, UCS) naturally produces salivation (the unconditioned response, UCR) without any learning. A tone, such as a metronome or buzzer, is at first a neutral stimulus that produces no salivation. After repeated pairings in which the tone comes just before the food, the tone alone produces salivation. The tone is now the conditioned stimulus (CS), and the salivation it triggers is the conditioned response (CR). In everyday life, the whistle of a pressure cooker can make you hungry because it has so often preceded meals. Option b is right because the stem describes a stimulus that was once neutral and acquired its power through pairing, which is the definition of a CS. The exam trap is mixing up stimulus with response, and conditioned with unconditioned: a stimulus is what comes in, a response is what the organism does, unconditioned means unlearned and conditioned means learned. The same salivation is a UCR to food but a CR to the tone.", "traps": {"a": "The unconditioned stimulus (food) triggers a response naturally, with no learning; the stem describes a stimulus that gained its effect only through pairing.", "c": "The unconditioned response is the unlearned reaction (salivation to food); it is a response, not a stimulus.", "d": "The conditioned response is the learned reaction produced by the CS; the stem asks for the stimulus that triggers it, not the response itself."}}, {"q": "Which term refers to the process of decreasing the likelihood of a behavior by removing a desirable stimulus after the behavior occurs?", "concept": "The answer is d, negative punishment. In B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, consequences are sorted by two questions. First, does the consequence make the behaviour more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. That gives four cells: positive reinforcement (add something pleasant, such as praise for homework, so homework increases); negative reinforcement (remove something unpleasant, such as the seatbelt alarm stopping when you buckle up, so buckling increases); positive punishment (add something unpleasant, such as a scolding, so the behaviour decreases); and negative punishment (remove something pleasant, such as taking away a teenager's phone for coming home late, so coming home late decreases). Time-out and response cost (fines, loss of tokens) are the classic clinical forms of negative punishment. Option d is right because the stem has both clues: the behaviour decreases (punishment) and a desirable stimulus is taken away (negative). The exam trap is negative reinforcement, which students confuse with punishment because of the word \"negative\". Negative reinforcement always increases behaviour by removing something aversive; negative punishment decreases behaviour by removing something desirable.", "traps": {"a": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus and increases behaviour; the stem describes a decrease brought about by a removal.", "b": "Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus and increases behaviour (escape or avoidance); it never decreases behaviour.", "c": "Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus (a scolding, extra chores) to decrease behaviour; the stem says a desirable stimulus is removed, not that something unpleasant is added."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the concept of working memory?", "concept": "The answer is c. Working memory is the small, active workspace in which information is held for a short time and worked on during a task, such as keeping a phone number in mind while dialling, multiplying 47 by 6 in your head, or following a long sentence. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposed the model in 1974 to replace the single, passive short-term store of the Atkinson-Shiffrin model (1968). Their model has a central executive that directs attention and coordinates two slave systems: the phonological loop (an inner voice and inner ear for speech-based material) and the visuospatial sketchpad (for images and spatial layout). In 2000 Baddeley added the episodic buffer, which links working memory with long-term memory. Capacity is limited: George Miller's classic estimate for short-term span was seven plus or minus two items, and Nelson Cowan later argued for about four chunks. Option c is right because it names both defining features: temporary storage and active manipulation in the service of a cognitive task. The exam trap is plain short-term memory, which stresses passive holding for a brief period; working memory stresses manipulation, so \"storage plus processing\" is its signature.", "traps": {"a": "This describes long-term memory, the relatively permanent store of very large capacity; working memory is temporary and limited.", "b": "This describes encoding, the process of getting information into memory, or sensory memory as the entry point; it is a process, not the active workspace.", "d": "This describes implicit, non-conscious emotional processing (such as fast threat detection by the amygdala); working memory is a conscious, effortful system."}}, {"q": "Which term best describes the psychological drive that energizes and directs behavior toward a specific goal?", "concept": "The answer is c, motivation. Motivation is the general term for the processes that start (energise), direct and sustain behaviour toward a goal; the word comes from the Latin movere, \"to move\". Textbooks such as Morgan and King describe a motivational cycle: a need (a deficit, such as low blood sugar) gives rise to a drive (an aroused state of tension, such as hunger), which pushes behaviour toward an incentive or goal (food), and reaching the goal reduces the drive. Clark Hull's drive-reduction theory (1943) is built on this cycle. Motivation is the umbrella term covering needs, drives, incentives and arousal, and it shows itself in activation (getting started), persistence (keeping going) and intensity (how hard you try). A student who studies every evening for months to clear an M.Phil entrance shows all three. Option c is right because the stem asks for the force that both energises and directs behaviour toward a specific goal, and only motivation covers both energy and direction. The exam trap is option b, need: a need is the deficit that starts the cycle, not the force that steers behaviour, while arousal (option a) supplies energy without direction.", "traps": {"a": "Arousal is the general level of physiological and psychological activation (as in the Yerkes-Dodson law); it energises behaviour but does not by itself point it toward a specific goal.", "b": "A need is a deficit or lack (of food, water, achievement) that starts the motivational cycle; the goal-directed drive it produces is what we call motivation.", "d": "Homeostasis, Walter Cannon's term, is the body's tendency to keep its internal state (temperature, blood sugar) stable; it explains why drives arise but is not itself the goal-directed drive."}}, {"q": "According to the James-Lange theory of emotion, what is the sequence of events in the experience of emotion?", "concept": "The answer is a. The James-Lange theory was proposed independently by the American psychologist William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885). Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid, and then tremble and run. James reversed this: we see the bear, the body reacts (pounding heart, trembling, running), and our perception of those bodily changes is the emotion. In his famous phrase, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble. So the sequence is stimulus, then physiological arousal, then the felt emotion, and each emotion should have its own bodily pattern. Walter Cannon and Philip Bard attacked the theory in the 1920s, arguing that visceral changes are too slow and too similar across emotions; they proposed that the thalamus sends signals to the cortex (feeling) and the body (arousal) at the same time. Option a is right because it is the only choice that places physiological arousal before emotional experience, the defining claim of James-Lange. The exam trap is the rival theories: Cannon-Bard says arousal and feeling are simultaneous, Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory (1962) says arousal plus a cognitive label, and Richard Lazarus's appraisal theory puts cognitive appraisal first.", "traps": {"b": "Emotion first, then arousal, is the common-sense view that James and Lange rejected; it is the reverse of their theory.", "c": "Cognitive appraisal leading to emotion is Richard Lazarus's appraisal theory (and the cognitive step of Schachter and Singer's two-factor theory), not James-Lange.", "d": "Stimulus straight to emotion skips the bodily step that James and Lange said actually is the emotion, so it leaves out the theory's key ingredient."}}, {"q": "School of Individual Psychology was founded by:", "concept": "The answer is b, Alfred Adler. Adler (1870-1937), an Austrian physician, was an early member of Sigmund Freud's Vienna circle but broke with him in 1911, rejecting Freud's emphasis on sexuality, and named his own approach Individual Psychology. \"Individual\" here means indivisible: Adler saw the person as a unified whole moving toward goals, not a battleground of id, ego and superego. His key ideas are the feelings of inferiority every child has, compensation for them, striving for superiority (later, striving for perfection or completion), the style of life, fictional finalism (guiding goals that may be imagined), social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl) as the mark of mental health, and birth order effects, such as the \"dethroned\" first-born. The popular term \"inferiority complex\" comes from his school. Option b is right because Individual Psychology is the specific name of Adler's school. The exam trap is Carl Jung, the other famous dissenter from Freud, whose school is called Analytical Psychology. Memory hook: Adler, Individual; Jung, Analytical; Freud, Psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "Erik Erikson was a later ego psychologist known for the eight psychosocial stages and the identity crisis, not for founding Individual Psychology.", "c": "Anna Freud, Sigmund Freud's daughter, pioneered child psychoanalysis and ego psychology and systematised the defence mechanisms in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936).", "d": "Carl Jung founded Analytical Psychology, with the collective unconscious and archetypes; he is the classic confusion because he also broke with Freud (around 1913)."}}, {"q": "As per Erikson's psychosocial stages of development, at what age do children take the initiative on some activities, and may develop guilt when unsuccessful or when boundaries are overstepped?", "concept": "The answer is d, 3-6 years. Erik Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who worked in the United States, described eight psychosocial stages in Childhood and Society (1950). Each stage is a crisis between two poles, and resolving it well yields a basic virtue. The third stage, initiative versus guilt, covers the preschool or play years, roughly 3 to 6 (Freud's phallic stage). Children now plan and start activities on their own: inventing games, asking endless questions, trying to help cook. If adults support this, the child develops initiative and the virtue of purpose; if the child is constantly criticised, or oversteps limits and is shamed, guilt develops. The sequence to memorise is: trust versus mistrust (0-1), autonomy versus shame and doubt (1-3), initiative versus guilt (3-6), industry versus inferiority (6-12), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood) and integrity versus despair (old age). Option d is right because 3-6 years is the age band of initiative versus guilt. The exam trap is the neighbouring stages: autonomy versus shame (1-3) is about doing things by oneself, while industry versus inferiority (school age) is about competence compared with peers.", "traps": {"a": "7-11 years matches Piaget's concrete operational stage and falls inside Erikson's industry versus inferiority stage, not initiative versus guilt.", "b": "5-12 years roughly covers Erikson's school-age stage, industry versus inferiority, where the conflict is competence versus feeling inferior.", "c": "1-2 years falls in the toddler stage of autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1-3 years), where the issue is self-control and independence, not initiative."}}, {"q": "The definition of health provided by the World Health Organization (WHO) includes which of the following aspects of health?", "concept": "The answer is c. The Constitution of the World Health Organization, adopted in New York in 1946 and in force from 7 April 1948 (now marked as World Health Day), defines health as \"a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity\". In the option's wording, physical is biological, mental is psychological, and social stays social. The definition mattered because it moved health beyond the narrow medical model (no disease) toward positive well-being. It fits George Engel's biopsychosocial model (1977), which says illness must be understood through biological, psychological and social factors together, the foundation of modern health and clinical psychology. In 1998 the WHO Executive Board proposed adding \"spiritual\" well-being (and the word \"dynamic\"), but the World Health Assembly never adopted it, so the official definition still has three dimensions. Option c is right because biological, social and psychological map exactly onto physical, social and mental. The exam trap is option b, which adds spiritual: students remember the 1998 proposal and pick it, but it never became part of the definition. Likewise, environment is a determinant of health, not a dimension of the WHO definition.", "traps": {"a": "Biological and environmental leaves out the mental and social dimensions; environment is a determinant of health, not one of the WHO dimensions.", "b": "Spiritual well-being was proposed by the WHO Executive Board in 1998 but never adopted, and this option also drops the psychological (mental) dimension.", "d": "Adding environmental turns this into a list of health determinants or an ecological model, not the WHO definition, which has exactly three dimensions."}}, {"q": "In Herzberg's motivation theory, the factors that can cause higher level of dissatisfaction in a job are classified as:", "concept": "The answer is b, hygiene factors. Frederick Herzberg, an American psychologist, interviewed about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh and published The Motivation to Work (1959). He asked about times they felt very good or very bad about their jobs and found that the causes of unhappiness differed from the causes of happiness. This led to his two-factor (motivation-hygiene) theory. Hygiene factors belong to the job context: salary, company policy, supervision, working conditions, job security and relations with co-workers. When poor, they cause dissatisfaction; when good, they only remove dissatisfaction and bring a person to neutral, not to motivation. Motivators belong to the job content: achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth, and these create satisfaction and motivation. So satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one scale. A fair salary and a clean office stop complaints; challenging work and recognition make people want to work harder. Option b is right because the stem asks which factors cause dissatisfaction, and in Herzberg's theory that is the role of hygiene factors. The exam trap is option a, motivators: they produce satisfaction, and their absence leaves a worker unmotivated rather than actively dissatisfied.", "traps": {"a": "Motivators (achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth) produce satisfaction; their absence leaves a worker unmotivated, not actively dissatisfied.", "c": "Performing factors is not a category in Herzberg's theory; it is a made-up distractor.", "d": "Expectancy belongs to Victor Vroom's expectancy theory (1964), where motivation depends on expectancy, instrumentality and valence, not to Herzberg."}}, {"q": "Who suggested a three-factor theory of personality?", "concept": "The answer is c, Hans Eysenck. Hans J. Eysenck (1916-1997), a German-born British psychologist at the Institute of Psychiatry in London, used factor analysis to reduce many traits to a few broad, biologically based superfactors, which he called types. His first two were extraversion-introversion (E) and neuroticism-emotional stability (N); crossed together, they reproduce the four classical temperaments of Hippocrates and Galen. In the 1970s he added a third, psychoticism (P), describing a cold, aggressive, impulsive and tough-minded style. This is the three-factor PEN model, measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975, with Sybil Eysenck). He linked extraversion to the arousal level of the ascending reticular activating system (introverts are chronically more aroused, so they avoid extra stimulation) and neuroticism to the reactivity of the limbic system or \"visceral brain\". Option c is right because the three-factor theory of personality, PEN, is Eysenck's. The exam trap is counting factors: Raymond Cattell has 16 source traits (the 16PF), Robert McCrae and Paul Costa have five (the Big Five), and Gordon Allport has no fixed number but cardinal, central and secondary traits. Three means Eysenck.", "traps": {"a": "Gordon Allport classified traits as cardinal, central and secondary and stressed the idiographic study of the individual; he did not propose three factors.", "b": "Raymond Cattell used factor analysis to find 16 source traits, measured by the 16PF; his is a sixteen-factor, not a three-factor, theory.", "d": "Robert McCrae and Paul Costa developed the five-factor model (openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism), measured by the NEO-PI."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not one of the Big 5 Personality Traits?", "concept": "The answer is a, creativity. The Big Five, or five-factor model, describes personality with five broad dimensions that emerged repeatedly from factor analyses of trait words (the lexical hypothesis) and questionnaires. Early work came from Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) and Warren Norman (1963); Lewis Goldberg popularised the name \"Big Five\", and Robert McCrae and Paul Costa built the NEO Personality Inventory to measure them. The five, remembered as OCEAN, are Openness to experience (imaginative, curious, broad interests), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable, disciplined), Extraversion (sociable, assertive, energetic), Agreeableness (warm, trusting, cooperative) and Neuroticism (anxious, moody, emotionally unstable). Each is a continuum; a person low on conscientiousness, for instance, is careless and disorganised. Creativity is related to openness, and some older lists labelled that factor \"intellect\" or \"culture\", but creativity itself is not the name of any of the five. Option a is right because agreeableness, conscientiousness and neuroticism are all standard Big Five names, while creativity is not. The exam trap is that overlap: an option naming creativity, intelligence or imagination is trying to pull you toward openness, yet only Openness to experience is the official label.", "traps": {"b": "Agreeableness (warm, trusting, cooperative versus antagonistic) is one of the five factors, the A in OCEAN.", "c": "Conscientiousness (organised, dependable, self-disciplined) is one of the five factors, the C in OCEAN, and the most consistent Big Five predictor of work performance.", "d": "Neuroticism (anxious, moody, emotionally unstable) is one of the five factors, the N in OCEAN, and is shared with Eysenck's model."}}, {"q": "Who has referred to adolescence as a period of Identity Crisis?", "concept": "The answer is a, Erikson. Erik Erikson coined the term \"identity crisis\" in the 1940s while working with World War II veterans who had lost their sense of who they were, and he made it the centre of his fifth psychosocial stage, identity versus role confusion, which covers adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 or 20). The adolescent's task is to integrate childhood identifications, new sexual maturity, abilities and social roles into a coherent answer to \"Who am I, and where am I going?\" Erikson saw adolescence as a psychosocial moratorium, a socially permitted delay in which young people try out roles, beliefs and careers before committing. Success brings the virtue of fidelity; failure leads to role confusion or a negative identity. He developed the idea in Childhood and Society (1950) and Identity: Youth and Crisis (1968). James Marcia later turned it into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement) defined by crisis (exploration) and commitment. Option a is right because \"identity crisis\" is Erikson's own term for the adolescent stage. The exam trap is G. Stanley Hall, who called adolescence a period of \"storm and stress\" (1904): Hall is storm and stress, Erikson is identity crisis, Piaget is formal operations.", "traps": {"b": "Jean Piaget described adolescence cognitively, as the start of the formal operational stage (abstract, hypothetical reasoning), not as an identity crisis.", "c": "Sigmund Freud described adolescence as the genital stage, when sexual energy re-emerges after latency; he did not use the term identity crisis.", "d": "Anna Freud described adolescence as inner turmoil in which the ego uses defences such as intellectualisation and asceticism against strengthened drives; identity crisis is Erikson's term."}}, {"q": "According to Freud there is/are only:", "concept": "The answer is b, two instincts. Sigmund Freud's instinct (drive) theory changed over time, but his final version, set out in Beyond the Pleasure Principle (1920), grouped all instincts into two great classes. The life instincts, together called Eros, serve the survival of the individual and the species: hunger, thirst and above all sex; their energy is the libido. The death instinct, later labelled Thanatos by his followers, is an unconscious wish to return to an inorganic, tension-free state; turned outward it shows as aggression and destructiveness, turned inward as self-punishment. Freud was led to it partly by the repetition compulsion he saw in traumatised World War I soldiers who kept reliving painful experiences. He also said every instinct has four features: a source (a bodily need), an aim (removing the excitation), an object (what satisfies it) and an impetus (its strength). Option b is right because Freud's mature theory names only two instincts, life and death. The exam trap is the earlier version: his first theory also had two classes (sexual versus ego or self-preservative instincts), so the count is the same; and do not confuse the four characteristics of an instinct with four instincts.", "traps": {"a": "One instinct is closer to Carl Jung, who treated libido as a single, general psychic energy, rather than Freud's dual instinct theory.", "c": "Three is a confusion with Freud's three structures of personality (id, ego, superego); his instincts were always grouped into two classes.", "d": "Four comes from the four characteristics of an instinct (source, aim, object, impetus), not from four separate instincts."}}, {"q": "According to Hurlock's theory, human development has how many stages?", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is c (Eight), but Elizabeth Hurlock's Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach divides life into ten periods, and many Indian guides count eleven by splitting adolescence into early and late; eight is Erik Erikson's number of stages, not Hurlock's. Accept c for this paper, but in another exam choose eleven, or ten if eleven is not offered. Elizabeth B. Hurlock was an American developmental psychologist whose textbooks were long standard in Indian universities. Her ten periods are: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to about two weeks), babyhood (two weeks to two years), early childhood (2 to 6), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12), puberty (10 or 12 to 13 or 14), adolescence (13 or 14 to 18), early adulthood (18 to 40), middle age (40 to 60) and old age (60 onward). Dividing adolescence into early (13 to 16 or 17) and late (16 or 17 to 18) gives eleven. Option c is the official key, apparently because the setter borrowed Erikson's count of eight. The exam trap is exactly this mix-up of stage counts: Erikson has eight psychosocial stages, Freud five psychosexual stages, Piaget four cognitive stages, and Hurlock ten (or eleven) life-span periods.", "traps": {"a": "Six does not match any standard count of Hurlock's periods; it is a pure distractor.", "b": "Eleven is the count many Indian guides give for Hurlock (her ten periods with adolescence split into early and late); it is more defensible than the key, but this paper keyed c.", "d": "Ten is the number of periods in Hurlock's own life-span textbook, so it is textbook-accurate, but this paper keyed c."}}, {"q": "During stress the part of the brain which gets activated is:", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: The official key is d (autonomic nervous system), but the ANS is not part of the brain; it is a division of the peripheral nervous system. Standard texts name the hypothalamus (b) as the brain structure that switches on the stress response, so if a stem asks for a part of the brain, b is the safer textbook answer; on this paper, mark d. Here is the full pathway. When a threat is perceived, the amygdala, in the medial temporal lobe, flags it as dangerous and signals the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus then drives two systems. The fast one is the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system: the sympathetic branch of the ANS raises heart rate, blood pressure and breathing, and stimulates the adrenal medulla to release adrenaline and noradrenaline, which Walter Cannon called the fight-or-flight response. The slower one is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which then feeds back to shut the axis down. Option d is keyed because the setter treated the ANS, whose sympathetic branch produces the visible stress reaction, as the system activated in stress. The exam trap is mixing a brain structure (hypothalamus, amygdala) with a nervous system division (ANS, CNS).", "traps": {"a": "The central nervous system is the whole brain plus spinal cord; it is far too broad to be the specific part activated in stress.", "b": "The hypothalamus is the brain's stress command centre, starting both the sympathetic response and the HPA axis; it is the strongest textbook answer, but this paper keyed d.", "c": "The amygdala detects threat and alerts the hypothalamus, so it is active in fear and stress, but it is the alarm rather than the coordinator of the bodily stress response."}}, {"q": "A pattern of relatively permanent traits or dispositions or characteristics that give some consistency to a person's behaviour is called:", "concept": "The answer is b, personality. Gordon Allport (1937) gave the most quoted definition: personality is \"the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment\" (in 1961 he changed the ending to \"characteristic behavior and thought\"). Most textbooks paraphrase it as a relatively stable, enduring pattern of traits, dispositions and characteristics that gives consistency and uniqueness to a person's behaviour across situations and over time. The word comes from the Latin persona, the mask worn by actors in Roman theatre. Two features define it: consistency (you behave in recognisably similar ways in different settings) and distinctiveness (it marks you off from other people). A friend who is cheerful, punctual and talkative at college, at home and at weddings shows a consistent personality. Personality is the whole pattern; traits, temperament and genes are its parts or sources. Option b is right because the stem describes the overall pattern of relatively permanent traits that gives consistency to behaviour, which is the definition of personality itself. The exam trap is temperament: it is the biologically based, early-appearing core of personality (activity level, emotional reactivity, as studied by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess), so it is narrower than the full pattern.", "traps": {"a": "A general trait is a single disposition (such as honesty) found across people; the stem describes a whole pattern of traits, which is personality.", "c": "Temperament is the inborn, biologically based style of emotion and activity seen from infancy; it is one component of personality, not the whole pattern.", "d": "A genetic predisposition is an inherited tendency or vulnerability; it is one source of personality, not the organised pattern of traits itself."}}, {"q": "Under stressful condition which of the following hormone is produced?", "concept": "⚠ Key debate: Epinephrine (a) and adrenaline (c) are two names for the same hormone (epinephrine is the name used in American texts, adrenaline the British name), so a and c are equally correct in content; this paper keyed c. If both appear in an exam, nothing in the science separates them, so go with adrenaline as this setter did. Adrenaline is made by the adrenal medulla, the inner part of the adrenal glands that sit on top of the kidneys. Under stress the hypothalamus activates the sympathetic nervous system, whose nerves stimulate the adrenal medulla to release adrenaline and noradrenaline (norepinephrine) into the blood. These raise heart rate, blood pressure, blood sugar and breathing, and send blood to the muscles, preparing the body for what Walter Cannon called fight or flight. The slower, longer-lasting stress hormone is cortisol, released by the adrenal cortex through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis; Hans Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion) describes what happens when stress persists. Option c is right because adrenaline is the classic hormone of the immediate emergency response. The exam trap is the neurotransmitter options: dopamine and serotonin act mainly as brain neurotransmitters for reward and mood, not as the hormones the body releases in stress.", "traps": {"a": "Epinephrine is the same hormone as adrenaline under its American name, so it is not wrong in content; it is simply not the letter this paper keyed.", "b": "Dopamine is mainly a brain neurotransmitter for reward, movement and motivation (and a precursor of noradrenaline); it is not the classic adrenal stress hormone.", "d": "Serotonin is a neurotransmitter involved in mood, sleep and appetite, and the target of SSRIs; it is not the hormone released in the fight-or-flight response."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a feature of agoraphobia?", "concept": "The answer is c, fear of public speaking. In DSM-5-TR, agoraphobia is marked fear or anxiety about at least two of five situations: using public transportation, being in open spaces (markets, parking lots, bridges), being in enclosed spaces (shops, cinemas), standing in line or being in a crowd, and being outside the home alone. The person fears these places because escape might be difficult or help might not be available if panic-like or embarrassing symptoms occur (such as fainting or losing bladder control). The situations are avoided, endured with intense fear, or need a companion, and the pattern lasts six months or more. Since DSM-5 (2013), agoraphobia is a separate diagnosis that can be given with or without panic disorder. The word comes from the Greek agora, the marketplace. Option c is right because fear of public speaking is about being watched and judged, the hallmark of social anxiety disorder (social phobia), whose \"performance only\" specifier covers fear limited to speaking or performing in public. The exam trap is that crowds can appear in both: the agoraphobic person fears a crowd because escape is hard, the socially anxious person because of scrutiny. Ask: fear of being trapped, or fear of being judged?", "traps": {"a": "Using public transportation (buses, trains, planes) is one of the five DSM-5-TR agoraphobic situations, so it is a feature.", "b": "Being outside the home alone is one of the five agoraphobic situations; severe cases can become completely housebound.", "d": "Standing in line or being in a crowd is one of the five agoraphobic situations, feared because escape would be hard."}}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders includes an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, characterized by palpitations, sweating, trembling, chest pain, and fear of losing control or dying?", "concept": "The answer is c, panic disorder. DSM-5-TR defines a panic attack as an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches a peak within minutes, with at least four of thirteen symptoms, including palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, choking, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat sensations, numbness or tingling, derealisation or depersonalisation, fear of losing control or going crazy, and fear of dying. Panic disorder is diagnosed when attacks are recurrent and unexpected (out of the blue) and at least one is followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences, or a maladaptive change in behaviour such as avoiding exercise. For example, a woman has a sudden attack in a shopping mall, is sure she is having a heart attack, is cleared in casualty, and then lives in dread of the next one. Option c is right because the stem quotes the DSM wording for a panic attack, and panic disorder is the disorder built around these attacks. The exam trap is that panic attacks can occur in any anxiety disorder; in specific or social phobia they are expected, cued by the feared situation, whereas in panic disorder at least some are unexpected and the person comes to fear the attacks themselves.", "traps": {"a": "Specific phobia is marked fear of a particular object or situation (dogs, heights, injections); any panic is cued by that object, and the stem names no specific trigger.", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder is chronic, diffuse worry with tension and restlessness lasting at least six months, not sudden attacks that peak within minutes.", "d": "Social phobia (social anxiety disorder) is fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation; panic there is triggered by social settings, whereas the stem describes the attack syndrome itself."}}, {"q": "Blood-Injection-Injury is what type of disorder?", "concept": "The answer is specific phobia: blood-injection-injury (BII) is one of the five subtypes of specific phobia in DSM-5-TR, and ICD-11 also files fear of blood or injury under specific phobia. Specific phobia is marked, out-of-proportion fear of a particular object or situation that almost always provokes immediate fear, is actively avoided, and lasts six months or more. DSM-5-TR asks the clinician to specify the stimulus: animal (spiders, dogs), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury (seeing blood, injections, medical procedures), situational (planes, lifts, enclosed places) and other (such as choking or vomiting). BII phobia is unusual for its diphasic response: heart rate and blood pressure first rise, then drop sharply through a vasovagal reaction, so the person may faint, which is rare in other phobias. Lars-Göran Öst developed applied tension (tensing the muscles to keep blood pressure up) for it, used alongside graded exposure. Option d is right because BII is a named subtype of specific phobia, not a separate disorder. The exam trap is agoraphobia: agoraphobia spans several situations feared because escape would be hard or help unavailable, whereas a specific phobia is tied to one kind of cue, here blood, needles or injury.", "traps": {"a": "Social phobia is fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation by others; fear of blood, needles or injury has nothing to do with being judged.", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder is chronic, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, not fear of one specific stimulus such as blood or injections.", "c": "Agoraphobia is fear of two or more situations (public transport, open or enclosed spaces, crowds, being outside alone) because escape might be hard; BII fear is tied to one cue and is a specific phobia subtype."}}, {"q": "Which of the following disorders includes a persistent worry or uneasiness spread across multiple situations?", "concept": "The answer is generalized anxiety disorder (GAD): persistent worry spread across many situations is its defining feature in DSM-5-TR. GAD is excessive anxiety and worry about a number of events or activities (work, health, money, family, small daily matters), present more days than not for at least six months, which the person finds hard to control. Adults need at least three of six symptoms (children only one): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating or mind going blank, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance. ICD-11 similarly describes general apprehension or worry not restricted to any particular circumstance. Picture a mother who worries all day that her husband will crash the car, her son will fail, and the rent will be late, and cannot switch it off. Option c is right because the stem's two cues, persistent and spread across multiple situations, are exactly the free-floating worry of GAD. The exam trap is depression, which shares fatigue, poor concentration and sleep problems with GAD; depression centres on low mood and loss of interest, GAD on future-oriented worry. Acute stress disorder is the other trap: it needs a traumatic event and lasts only three days to one month.", "traps": {"a": "Agoraphobia is fear of particular situations (public transport, crowds, being outside alone) where escape may be hard, not diffuse worry across many areas of life.", "b": "Depression centres on persistent low mood or loss of interest; the free-floating, future-oriented worry in the stem points to GAD.", "d": "Acute stress disorder follows exposure to a traumatic event and lasts three days to one month, with intrusion, dissociation and arousal symptoms; it is not general worry across many situations."}}, {"q": "Which of the following psychotherapy is indicated for treatment of obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "concept": "The answer is b, exposure and response prevention (ERP). ERP is a behaviour therapy for obsessive-compulsive disorder. It grew from Victor Meyer's work in London in 1966 and was developed and tested by Isaac Marks and Stanley Rachman and later by Edna Foa. The logic: obsessions raise anxiety and compulsions bring it down, so the compulsion is negatively reinforced and the person never learns that the anxiety would fade on its own. In ERP the client is exposed, gradually and using a fear hierarchy, to the cues that trigger obsessions (touching a doorknob, leaving the house without checking the gas) and is helped to refrain from the compulsion (no washing, no checking). Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls (habituation), and the client learns that the feared outcome does not happen. ERP, usually delivered within cognitive-behavioural therapy, is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD in guidelines such as those of NICE, often combined with an SSRI. Option b is right because ERP is the specific, evidence-based psychotherapy for OCD. The exam trap is option d, REBT: it is a cognitive therapy that can be used for anxiety, but it is not the OCD-specific treatment; the words \"response prevention\" are what mark the OCD answer.", "traps": {"a": "Humanistic therapy (such as Carl Rogers's person-centred therapy) aims at growth and self-acceptance through empathy and unconditional positive regard; it has no technique for breaking the obsession-compulsion cycle.", "c": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, Irvin Yalom, Viktor Frankl) explores meaning, freedom and death anxiety; it is not an evidence-based treatment for OCD.", "d": "Rational emotive behaviour therapy, developed by Albert Ellis, disputes irrational beliefs using the ABC model; it is a general cognitive therapy, not the first-line OCD treatment."}}, {"q": "A 6-year-old girl was referred because she had not been attending school. If forced to attend school she would cry excessively and cling to her mother. She would only settle down in the classroom if her mother was in her sight at school. Which disorder is she likely suffering from?", "concept": "The answer is c, separation anxiety disorder. In DSM-5-TR, separation anxiety disorder is developmentally inappropriate and excessive fear or anxiety about separation from attachment figures, shown by at least three of eight features: distress when separation happens or is expected, worry about losing the attachment figure or harm coming to them, worry that an event such as getting lost or kidnapped will cause separation, reluctance or refusal to go out or to school, fear of being alone, reluctance to sleep away from the attachment figure, nightmares about separation, and physical complaints such as stomach aches when separation is expected. It must last at least four weeks in children and adolescents (typically six months in adults). DSM-5 moved it into the anxiety disorders chapter and allowed diagnosis in adults. Separation protest is normal in infants and toddlers, but this intensity at age six is excessive. Option c is right because the girl's school refusal, crying and clinging, and settling only when her mother is in sight all centre on separation from her mother. The exam trap is social anxiety disorder, which can also cause school refusal; there the child fears the judgement of classmates or teachers, and her mother's presence would not settle her.", "traps": {"a": "Social anxiety disorder is fear of scrutiny by peers or adults; such a child fears being judged, and her mother's presence would not settle her.", "b": "Agoraphobia is fear of situations such as crowds or public transport where escape might be hard, and it usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood; this child's fear is about being apart from her mother.", "d": "Generalized anxiety disorder is worry about many different topics (school performance, health, disasters) for six months; this child's anxiety has one focus, separation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of Body Dysmorphic Disorder?", "concept": "The answer is b, hair pulling. Body dysmorphic disorder (BDD), placed in DSM-5-TR under obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, is preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable or look slight to others. At some point the person performs repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing their appearance with others) in response to the concern, and it causes significant distress or impairment. Specifiers include muscle dysmorphia (believing one's body is too small or not muscular enough) and level of insight, which can range down to absent insight with delusional beliefs. The Italian psychiatrist Enrico Morselli called it \"dysmorphophobia\" in 1891. Option b is right because hair pulling is the defining behaviour of trichotillomania (hair-pulling disorder), a separate diagnosis in the same chapter, and it is not a BDD criterion. The exam trap is that a BDD patient may pluck hair to improve appearance, and a trichotillomania patient may be ashamed of hair loss; the tie-breaker is motive. In BDD the act aims to fix a perceived flaw, whereas in trichotillomania the pulling itself is the problem, with repeated failed attempts to stop.", "traps": {"a": "Reassurance seeking (asking others whether one's nose looks strange) is one of the repetitive behaviours listed in the DSM-5-TR criteria for BDD.", "c": "Excessive grooming (makeup, hair styling, shaving) to hide or correct a perceived flaw is a listed BDD behaviour.", "d": "Repeated mirror checking is the most typical BDD behaviour, though some patients do the opposite and avoid mirrors altogether."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following is not the feature of a projective test?", "concept": "The answer is b, structured questions. A projective test presents an ambiguous, relatively unstructured stimulus and an open task (say what you see, tell a story, finish the sentence), so the person must impose their own organisation on it. This rests on the projective hypothesis, named by Lawrence K. Frank in 1939: faced with an ambiguous stimulus, people project their own needs, conflicts, wishes and ways of seeing onto it, revealing parts of personality they may not be aware of or willing to report. Well-known examples are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935), sentence completion tests and the Draw-a-Person test. Because the person cannot easily tell what a \"good\" answer is, faking is harder; because responses are open-ended and scored with more examiner judgement, reliability is a known weakness. Option b is right because structured questions with fixed response choices are the hallmark of objective self-report inventories such as the MMPI or 16PF, the opposite of projective tests. The exam trap is to keep the pair straight: projective means ambiguous stimulus, free response and indirect, often unconscious, content; objective means structured items, fixed answers and standardised scoring.", "traps": {"a": "Ambiguous stimuli (inkblots, vague pictures) are the defining feature of projective tests, so this is a feature, not the exception.", "c": "Projection of unconscious needs and conflicts onto the stimulus is the theoretical basis of projective testing, the projective hypothesis.", "d": "Subjective, open-ended responses that need examiner interpretation are typical of projective tests, which is why their reliability is often questioned."}}, {"q": "Which of the following personality tests is not used in a clinical setting?", "concept": "The answer is a, the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator. The MBTI was developed in the 1940s by Katharine Cook Briggs and her daughter Isabel Briggs Myers, based on Carl Jung's Psychological Types (1921). It sorts people on four dichotomies: Extraversion or Introversion, Sensing or Intuition, Thinking or Feeling, and Judging or Perceiving, giving 16 types such as INTJ or ESFP. It describes normal preferences, has no scales for psychopathology, and is used mainly in counselling, career guidance, team building and organisational training. Psychologists also criticise it because many people get a different type on retest and because forcing continuous traits into either-or types loses information. The other three are used clinically: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943) is the most widely used clinical personality inventory, the Thematic Apperception Test is a projective test used in clinical assessment, and Raymond Cattell's 16PF, though built for the normal range, is used in clinical and counselling work. Option a is right because the MBTI is a type indicator for normal personality, not a clinical or diagnostic tool. The exam trap is the 16PF, which also measures normal personality but still appears in clinical batteries, whereas the MBTI never diagnoses.", "traps": {"b": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan) is a projective test in which the person tells stories about ambiguous pictures; clinicians use it to explore needs, conflicts and interpersonal themes.", "c": "The MMPI (now MMPI-2 and MMPI-3) was built by empirical criterion keying against psychiatric patient groups and is the most widely used clinical personality inventory.", "d": "Cattell's 16PF measures 16 normal-range source traits but is widely used in clinical and counselling assessment, and the Clinical Analysis Questionnaire extends it with clinical scales."}}, {"q": "Agoraphobia is often associated with:", "concept": "The answer is b, panic attacks. Agoraphobia and panic are closely linked, historically and clinically. In DSM-III-R and DSM-IV, agoraphobia was most often diagnosed as panic disorder with agoraphobia: a person has unexpected panic attacks and then starts avoiding places where an attack would be embarrassing or escape hard, such as buses, markets or crowds. DSM-5 (2013) made them independent diagnoses that can be given together, because some people develop agoraphobia without full panic disorder, but the link remains strong: many people with agoraphobia have panic attacks or panic disorder, and the catastrophe they fear is usually panic-like or incapacitating symptoms. Picture a man who had a panic attack on a metro train and now cannot travel unless his brother comes along. In learning terms this is avoidance learning: the place becomes linked with the attack, and avoiding it reduces anxiety, so avoidance is negatively reinforced. Option b is right because panic attacks are the condition most typically associated with agoraphobia. The exam trap is option a: enclosed spaces are one of the five agoraphobic situations, but a fear limited to closed spaces is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia, not what agoraphobia is classically associated with.", "traps": {"a": "Fear of closed spaces on its own is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia; enclosed spaces are only one of the five agoraphobic situations.", "c": "Depression is a common comorbidity of many anxiety disorders, but the classic, defining association of agoraphobia is with panic attacks.", "d": "Fear of eating alone in public, being watched while eating, is a social anxiety (social phobia) concern about scrutiny."}}, {"q": "Which disorder can be characterized by repeated checking of locks, gas stoves or light switches?", "concept": "The answer is a, obsessive-compulsive disorder. In DSM-5-TR, OCD is the presence of obsessions, compulsions or, usually, both. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause marked anxiety or distress, such as \"Did I leave the gas on? The house will burn down.\" Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules, aimed at reducing anxiety or preventing a dreaded event, but either not realistically connected to it or clearly excessive. Checking (locks, gas stoves, switches, doors) is one of the most common symptom dimensions, along with contamination and washing, symmetry and ordering, and forbidden or taboo thoughts. The symptoms must be time-consuming (more than one hour a day) or cause significant distress or impairment, and insight is specified as good or fair, poor, or absent with delusional beliefs. Option a is right because repeatedly checking locks, gas stoves and light switches is the textbook example of a checking compulsion. The exam trap is generalized anxiety disorder: GAD involves worry about real-life matters, but not rituals driven by an intrusive thought and repeated until things \"feel right\".", "traps": {"b": "Panic disorder involves recurrent unexpected panic attacks and worry about further attacks, not repetitive checking rituals.", "c": "Generalized anxiety disorder involves excessive worry about many everyday matters, but not rigid rituals such as checking a lock again and again.", "d": "Specific phobia is fear and avoidance of a particular object or situation; it involves avoidance, not compulsive checking."}}, {"q": "A 19-year-old male presents with excessive checking of his face in the mirror, believing that he is ugly. He spends 2-4 hours daily checking his face and is socially withdrawn. Which disorder is he suffering from?", "concept": "The answer is b, body dysmorphic disorder. BDD, in the DSM-5-TR chapter on obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, is preoccupation with one or more perceived defects in appearance that are not observable or look slight to others, together with repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's looks with others), causing significant distress or impairment. Typical concerns are the skin, hair and nose, but any body part can be involved. The most common age of onset is early adolescence, around 12 or 13, and patients often seek cosmetic surgery or dermatological treatment, which rarely helps. Social withdrawal, missed college or work and high rates of suicidal thinking are common. Treatment is cognitive-behavioural therapy with exposure and response prevention, plus SSRIs. Here a 19-year-old believes he is ugly, checks his face for 2 to 4 hours a day and has withdrawn socially. Option b is right because a belief in a defect in appearance plus repetitive mirror checking is the exact core of BDD. The exam trap is social anxiety disorder, because he avoids people; in BDD the avoidance comes from shame about a perceived physical flaw, whereas in social anxiety the fear is of acting in a way that will be judged.", "traps": {"a": "Social anxiety disorder is fear of negative evaluation of one's behaviour in social situations; his withdrawal stems from the belief that his face is ugly, which points to BDD.", "c": "Panic disorder involves recurrent unexpected panic attacks; nothing in this case describes sudden surges of fear.", "d": "OCD shares the obsession-and-ritual structure, but when the preoccupation is confined to appearance, DSM-5-TR diagnoses BDD instead."}}, {"q": "In Obsessive Compulsive Disorder, compulsions are:", "concept": "The answer is option c: compulsions are repetitive behaviours the person feels driven to perform to reduce anxiety, which is almost word for word the DSM-5-TR definition. In obsessive-compulsive disorder, obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (for example, \"my hands are covered in germs\"). Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (praying, counting, repeating words silently) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rules that must be applied rigidly. They aim to prevent or reduce anxiety or distress, or to prevent a dreaded event, but are not realistically connected to what they are meant to prevent, or are clearly excessive. The brief relief negatively reinforces the ritual, which is why exposure and response prevention works by blocking it. Option c is right because it names both parts of a compulsion: a repetitive act, and the drive to perform it to reduce anxiety. The exam trap is option d: repetitive thoughts about harming others are obsessions, not compulsions. Ask whether it intrudes on the person (obsession) or is something the person does to neutralise it (compulsion).", "traps": {"a": "Worrying is the anxious thinking itself, not a ritual done to neutralise an obsession (a mental ritual like silent counting can be a compulsion, worry is not); repetitive worry about bad events is closer to generalized anxiety disorder than to a compulsion.", "b": "Overwhelming fear of encounters with the opposite sex is a social or specific fear, not a feature of OCD at all.", "d": "Repetitive thoughts about harming others are aggressive obsessions, intrusive thoughts that cause anxiety; compulsions are the acts performed to neutralise such thoughts."}}, {"q": "People with social phobia avoid social situations because they fear:", "concept": "The answer is b, fear of negative evaluation. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) in DSM-5-TR is marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations in which the person may be scrutinised by others: conversations, meeting strangers, being watched while eating or drinking, or performing, such as giving a speech. The core fear is that they will act in a way, or show anxiety symptoms such as blushing, sweating or trembling, that will be negatively evaluated: humiliating, embarrassing, or leading to rejection. The situations are avoided or endured with intense fear, the fear is out of proportion, and it lasts six months or more. A \"performance only\" specifier applies when the fear is limited to speaking or performing in public. The cognitive model of David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995) explains how attention turns inward, people use safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences), and then misjudge how they came across. Option b is right because fear of negative evaluation or criticism by others is the defining fear in social phobia. The exam trap is option a: socially anxious people may have panic attacks, but these are cued by social scrutiny, whereas fear of the panic attacks themselves points to panic disorder or agoraphobia.", "traps": {"a": "Fear of the panic attacks themselves is the core of panic disorder and agoraphobia; in social phobia any panic is triggered by social scrutiny, which is the real fear.", "c": "Fear of contamination by other people is a typical OCD obsession, leading to washing or avoidance of touch.", "d": "Fear of enclosed spaces is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia, and one of the agoraphobic situations; it has nothing to do with being judged."}}]});
